var Q_OPHTHO_EP2 = [
{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-1-7", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-intro-exam",
  stem:"Action of the superior oblique muscle:",
  options:["elevation in abduction","Elevation in adduction","Depression in abduction","Depression in adduction"],
  answer:3,
  explanation:"The superior oblique is tested with the eye adducted, where its primary field of action is depression; it is supplied by the trochlear nerve (cranial nerve IV). The lecture's own muscle-action table (L1,2, slide 69) lists the superior oblique's primary anatomical action as intorsion, with depression and abduction as secondary and tertiary effects -- the \"depression in adduction\" phrasing used here describes the field of action tested clinically (the direction of gaze in which the muscle's vertical effect is greatest), which is the standard clinical convention rather than a direct restatement of the table's own columns (not taken from the course material).\n\nOption A, elevation in abduction, is the field of action of the superior rectus, which the same table lists with elevation as its primary action and adduction as its tertiary action -- abduction is not one of its listed columns, so this option combines the wrong muscle with the wrong position. Option B, elevation in adduction, belongs to the inferior oblique, whose primary action is extorsion with elevation as a secondary effect; adduction is not one of its listed columns either, so again the position given does not match this muscle's own table row. Option C, depression in abduction, belongs to the inferior rectus, whose primary action is depression with extorsion secondary and adduction tertiary; abduction is not printed as one of its own action columns, so it is the wrong pairing of muscle and position as well.\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> The superior oblique muscle primarily causes intorsion and depression when the eye is adducted. It’s supplied by the trochlear nerve (CN IV).\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"The superior oblique muscle's tested field of action is depression in adduction, and it is supplied by the trochlear nerve.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.1404-1405" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-1-25", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-red",
  stem:"Red eye is seen in all EXCEPT:",
  options:["Corneal ulcers","Conjunctivitis","Iridocyclitis","Primary open angle glaucoma"],
  answer:3,
  explanation:"Primary open-angle glaucoma is the classic painless, symptom-poor disease that damages the optic nerve over years without producing external inflammation, so the eye stays white; this is why it is picked out from the other three as the exception. The lecture material on red eye states this directly (L37, slide 12): \"the more common chronic open-angle glaucoma causes no redness of the eye,\" contrasting it with acute angle-closure glaucoma, which does present as a painful red eye (this is a different, uncommon form of glaucoma from the one named in the stem).\n\nCorneal ulcers, conjunctivitis and iridocyclitis are all inflammatory or infective surface/anterior-segment conditions and all produce visible redness -- ulcers and iridocyclitis typically as a circumcorneal (ciliary) flush and conjunctivitis as a more diffuse conjunctival injection; the source material's own differential table for the red eye lists acute iridocyclitis, keratitis, bacterial conjunctivitis, viral conjunctivitis and allergic conjunctivitis side by side as red-eye conditions, with open-angle glaucoma notably absent from that table (open-angle glaucoma not being a red-eye condition at all).\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Primary open-angle glaucoma causes painless, gradual vision loss without redness. In contrast, corneal ulcers, conjunctivitis, and iridocyclitis all present with red eyes.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Primary open-angle glaucoma is a painless, white-eye disease and is the exception among red-eye conditions such as corneal ulcers, conjunctivitis and iridocyclitis.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.1440-1441" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-1-27", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-lac",
  stem:"Acute dacryocystitis is treated by:",
  options:["Antibiotics and hot fomentation","Dacryocystorhinostomy","Probing","Dacryocystectomy"],
  answer:0,
  explanation:"The lacrimal system lecture gives acute dacryocystitis its own treatment heading (L9, slide 32), and it opens with exactly this: \"initial treatment involves the application of warm compresses and oral antibiotics,\" with the same slide going on to say that irrigation and probing should not be performed during the acute phase, and that incision and drainage is reserved for a pointing abscess.\n\nOption B, dacryocystorhinostomy, is the definitive surgical treatment named on the same slide, but it is explicitly positioned as something \"commonly required after the acute infection has been controlled\" -- i.e. a later, elective step, not the acute-phase treatment the stem asks for. Option C, probing, is the one intervention the lecture names as contraindicated at this stage (\"irrigation and probing should not be performed\"), because instrumenting an acutely infected, tense lacrimal sac risks rupturing it or spreading the infection. Option D, dacryocystectomy (excision of the lacrimal sac) is not mentioned anywhere in this slide's treatment section for either the acute or the chronic form; it is a salvage procedure used in specific circumstances such as a failed DCR or where the sac itself must be removed, and is not part of the standard first-line management the lecture describes (not taken from the course material).\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Acute dacryocystitis is an infection of the lacrimal sac and is treated initially with systemic antibiotics and warm compresses. Surgical procedures like DCR are reserved for chronic cases after the acute phase resolves.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Acute dacryocystitis is treated first with warm compresses and oral antibiotics; probing is avoided in the acute phase and DCR is deferred until the infection is controlled.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.1444-1445" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-1-32", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-neuro",
  stem:"Diurnal variation in ptosis is seen in:",
  options:["Congenital ptosis","Aponeurotic ptosis","Traumatic ptosis","Myasthenia gravis"],
  answer:3,
  explanation:"Myasthenia gravis is an autoimmune disease of the neuromuscular junction, and the muscle weakness it causes -- including ptosis of the levator palpebrae superioris -- is fatigable: it worsens with sustained use of the muscle over the course of the day and improves with rest, which is what produces the diurnal (day-to-day, worse-by-evening) pattern the stem describes (not taken from the course material, though the mechanism underlying this pattern is standard). The eyelid disease lecture's slide on myogenic (myasthenic) ptosis (L5, slide 13) illustrates exactly this variability with a before/after photograph pair of the same patient (one with ptosis, one after rest, now symmetrical), plus a separate pair showing the lid drooping again after sustained upgaze -- a direct visual demonstration of fatigability, alongside labelled photographs of the ice-pack test and the Tensilon test, both of which transiently reverse the ptosis and support the myasthenic diagnosis.\n\nCongenital ptosis is a fixed developmental defect of the levator muscle or its innervation and does not fluctuate through the day. Aponeurotic (involutional/senile) ptosis results from stretching or dehiscence of the levator aponeurosis with age and is likewise a fixed mechanical deficit, not a fatigable one. Traumatic ptosis follows direct injury to the lid, levator or its nerve supply and is also a fixed, structural problem rather than one that varies with the time of day (all three not taken from the course material, though the point that they are not fatigable is standard clinical teaching).\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> In myasthenia gravis, ptosis worsens as the day progresses due to fatigable muscle weakness. This diurnal variation is a key diagnostic clue.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Myasthenia gravis causes a fatigable ptosis that worsens through the day, unlike the fixed ptosis of congenital, aponeurotic or traumatic causes.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.1454-1455" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-1-34", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-uvea",
  stem:"Which of the following is a part of the uveal tract?",
  options:["The ciliary body","The limbus","The cornea","The retina"],
  answer:0,
  explanation:"The uvea & sclera lecture opens its uveal tract slide (L13,14, slide 3) with the tract broken into exactly three parts: iris, ciliary body (with its pars plicata and pars plana) and choroid, alongside a labelled cross-section diagram that picks the uveal tract out in red against the cornea, lens, vitreous, retina and sclera drawn around it. The ciliary body is one of those three named parts.\n\nOption B, the limbus, is the anatomical junction between the cornea and the sclera/conjunctiva -- it is a landmark on the ocular surface, not a layer of the vascular coat, and it is not listed among the uvea's parts on this slide. Option C, the cornea, and option D, the retina, are both drawn on the same cross-section diagram as separate, clearly distinct structures from the uveal tract picked out in red -- the cornea is the avascular anterior refractive window and the retina is the innermost neurosensory layer, and neither is one of the tract's three named components.\n\n**A live near-sibling entry, `ophep-uveal-tract-4`, asks the same underlying fact with a different distractor set (lens, sclera, cornea).**\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> The uveal tract includes the iris, ciliary body, and choroid, which are the vascular middle layers of the eye. The cornea and retina are not part of the uveal tract.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"The uveal tract is made up of the iris, ciliary body and choroid; the limbus, cornea and retina are separate structures.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.1458-1459" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-1-35", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-neuro-optic",
  stem:"Which of the following can cause painless loss of vision?",
  options:["Acute glaucoma","Anterior ischemic optic neuropathy","Corneal abrasion","Conjunctivitis"],
  answer:1,
  explanation:"Anterior ischemic optic neuropathy (AION) is caused by infarction of the optic nerve head and typically presents as sudden, painless visual loss; the optic nerve lecture's AION slide (L21, slide 9) shows this with a fundus photograph of a swollen, pale disc with a blurred margin and an obliterated cup, together with a visual field printout showing a dense altitudinal (superior-half) defect -- the classic AION picture -- though the word \"painless\" itself is not printed on that slide (not taken from the course material, though it is standard clinical teaching for AION).\n\nThe acute visual loss lecture separately sets up a painful/painless classification of sudden vision loss, but its own author never populates that classification with which named conditions fall on which side, and states that painful causes are represented by no case at all in the deck. Acute (angle-closure) glaucoma's pain is printed directly in its own lecture: the Acute Angle Closure Glaucoma symptoms slide lists \"Pain -> ++++\" as the first symptom (L12, slide 19), alongside coloured haloes, rapid diminution of vision, redness and nausea/vomiting -- a painful presentation, unlike the painless AION in the correct answer. The claim that corneal abrasion belongs on the \"painful\" side of this grouping rests on general clinical knowledge, not on anything printed in either source (not taken from the course material). Corneal abrasion is a surface epithelial defect that is intensely painful because the cornea is one of the most densely innervated tissues in the body, and it does not typically cause a marked drop in vision unless it is extensive or centrally located. Conjunctivitis is a surface inflammatory condition that classically spares vision altogether (aside from mild blurring from discharge) and is not a cause of true visual loss at all.\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Anterior ischemic optic neuropathy (AION) presents with sudden, painless vision loss due to infarction of the optic nerve head. Other options like acute glaucoma or corneal abrasion are typically painful.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Anterior ischemic optic neuropathy causes sudden painless visual loss with a swollen pale disc, unlike the painful presentations of acute glaucoma and corneal abrasion or the vision-sparing picture of conjunctivitis.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.1460-1461" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-1-46", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-uvea",
  stem:"Which of the following can cause miosis?",
  options:["Acute angle closure glaucoma","Acute iridocyclitis","Acute conjunctivitis","Allergic conjunctivitis"],
  answer:1,
  explanation:"The uvea & sclera lecture's slide on the signs of anterior uveitis (iridocyclitis) (L13,14, slide 17) lists the pupil sign directly: \"miosis with sluggish pupillary light reaction,\" together with an irregular pupil shape from synechiae -- the sphincter pupillae is irritated by the inflamed iris, producing a small, poorly reactive pupil, and the same slide's photograph shows a small, slightly irregular pupil in an inflamed eye.\n\nAcute angle-closure glaucoma produces the opposite pupillary sign: its own Signs slide (L12, slide 20) lists a \"mid-dilated vertically oval non-reactive pupil\" among the acute-angle-closure signs, the result of ischaemic paralysis of the iris sphincter from the sudden pressure rise (the ischaemic-paralysis mechanism itself is not taken from the course material, though it is standard clinical teaching and is the recognised contrast with iridocyclitis's miosis). Acute conjunctivitis is a surface inflammation of the conjunctiva that does not involve the iris or its sphincter muscle at all, so it does not alter pupil size. Allergic conjunctivitis is likewise a surface hypersensitivity reaction confined to the conjunctiva, without any effect on the pupil.\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Miosis occurs in acute iridocyclitis due to irritation of the sphincter pupillae muscle. Other signs include ciliary injection and pain.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Acute iridocyclitis causes miosis with a sluggish light reaction from sphincter irritation, unlike the mid-dilated pupil of acute angle-closure glaucoma or the uninvolved pupil in conjunctivitis.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.1482-1483" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-1-50", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-refract",
  stem:"Type of staphyloma in high myopia is:",
  options:["Anterior staphyloma","Ciliary staphyloma","Intercalary staphyloma","Posterior staphyloma"],
  answer:3,
  explanation:"The refractive errors lecture (L7,8, slide 46) lists posterior staphyloma as one of the named complications of high myopia, alongside chorioretinal degenerations, complicated cataract and a high association with primary open-angle glaucoma -- reflecting the progressive stretching and thinning of the posterior sclera around the optic disc and macula that occurs as the myopic eye elongates.\n\nAnterior, ciliary and intercalary staphyloma are all real staphyloma types classified by the site of the scleral outpouching (anterior, at the ciliary body region, and at the limbus/intercalary zone respectively), but they are associated with other causes of scleral thinning and ectasia -- such as previous inflammation, trauma or long-standing glaucoma -- rather than with the posterior elongation of high myopia; none of these three site-based categories is named on the myopia complications slide, so their distinction from posterior staphyloma is general ophthalmic knowledge rather than a fact printed in this lecture (not taken from the course material).\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> In high myopia, stretching of the posterior sclera causes a posterior staphyloma near the optic disc. It leads to progressive visual deterioration.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"High myopia produces a posterior staphyloma from progressive stretching of the sclera around the disc and macula, distinct from the anterior, ciliary and intercalary types seen with other causes of scleral ectasia.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.1490-1491" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-1-58", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-intro-exam",
  stem:"Color vision is tested by:",
  options:["Hess screen","Ishihara plates","Lancaster screen","Amsler's grid"],
  answer:1,
  image:"q-oph-ep-1506", imgAlt:"Circular plate made of many coloured dots of varying size",
  explanation:"The basic eye examination lecture lists colour vision testing as one of the complementary tests on its examination-summary slide (L1,2, slide 103), and Ishihara pseudoisochromatic plates -- coloured dot patterns that spell out numbers or shapes visible only to eyes with normal red-green colour discrimination -- are the standard clinical tool used for this purpose, though the lecture's summary slide names the test category without printing the word \"Ishihara\" itself (not taken from the course material for the specific plate name, though it is the universally used test and is exactly what the endpoint box below names).\n\nThe Hess screen and Lancaster screen are both used to chart and quantify ocular misalignment and diplopia from extraocular muscle imbalance (for example in cranial nerve palsies or thyroid eye disease) by plotting where each eye projects a target under red/green dissociation -- they assess motility, not colour perception, and neither is named in this lecture (not taken from the course material). The Amsler grid is a different complementary test on the same examination summary, used to assess the central 10 degrees of the visual field for metamorphopsia or scotomas, most often in macular disease -- it tests central field integrity, not colour discrimination.\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Ishihara plates are used to assess color vision, mainly detecting red-green color deficiencies. They consist of colored dot patterns forming numbers or shapes.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Ishihara plates test colour vision, distinct from the Hess and Lancaster screens (motility/diplopia charting) and the Amsler grid (central field testing).",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.1506-1507" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-1-73", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-conj",
  stem:"Recurrent pterygium may be treated by:",
  options:["Simple excision","Follow up","Excision with conjunctival autograft","Topical steroids followed by excision"],
  answer:2,
  explanation:"The conjunctiva lecture shows an operative photograph of pterygium excision with a sutured graft on its final pterygium slide (L6, slide 57), which is the only sourced anchor for surgical management in this deck -- the lecture never prints the words \"treatment,\" \"management,\" \"indication\" or a recurrence rate anywhere in its pterygium slides. Excision with conjunctival autograft covering the bare sclera is the standard surgical technique used clinically to reduce the risk of recurrence, since the graft restores a normal conjunctival surface over the excision bed and limits regrowth of fibrovascular tissue toward the cornea (not taken from the course material).\n\nSimple excision without a graft (the \"bare sclera\" technique) is the option this lecture's own photograph implicitly moves away from, and it is well recognised to carry a substantially higher recurrence rate than excision with grafting, which is precisely why a RECURRENT pterygium (one that has already failed a prior excision) is managed with the more definitive grafting approach rather than being re-excised alone (not taken from the course material). Follow-up alone is not treatment at all and does not address an already-recurrent lesion that is presumably growing and symptomatic. Topical steroids followed by excision is not a recognised staged protocol for pterygium in the source material or in general ophthalmic teaching; steroids may be used briefly to settle inflammation before elective surgery but are not a treatment sequence specific to recurrence, and grafting at the time of excision, not steroid pre-treatment, is what is understood to lower the chance of a further recurrence (not taken from the course material).\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Recurrent pterygium is best managed by excision followed by conjunctival autograft to reduce recurrence risk.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Recurrent pterygium is treated by excision with conjunctival autograft, which lowers recurrence risk compared with simple bare-sclera excision.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.1536-1537" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-3-43", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-peds",
  stem:"Even if retinopathy of prematurity is successfully-treated, those infants still have a risk to develop which of the following conditions?",
  options:["Hyperopia","Myopia","Amblyopia","All of the above"],
  answer:1,
  explanation:"Retinopathy of prematurity (ROP) is a proliferative retinopathy of preterm, low-birth-weight infants that ends in epiretinal fibrosis and tractional changes if untreated (L33,34, slide 19). Even after treatment succeeds in stopping the proliferative disease, treated ROP eyes carry a lasting higher rate of myopia than the general infant population -- refractive change is one of its long-term sequelae, distinct from the retinal-detachment risk that marks the untreated, late-stage disease (not taken from the course material, which does not discuss ROP's post-treatment refractive outcome).\n\nOption A, hyperopia, is the wrong direction of refractive shift -- ROP-associated refractive error runs myopic, not hyperopic, most likely from the altered anterior-segment and axial growth that accompanies the peripheral retinal disease. Option C, amblyopia, can certainly follow severe ROP, but usually as a consequence of a structural complication (macular dragging, retinal detachment, or the anisometropia produced by asymmetric disease) rather than as the direct, well-known residual risk after otherwise-successful treatment that this stem is asking about; it is not the association the question is built on. Option D, \"all of the above\", is therefore wrong because hyperopia is not part of the picture, so the combination cannot be correct.\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Prognosis of retinopathy of prematurity:\n> 🔴 Higher incidence of myopia among ROP infants, even if treated successfully.\n> 🔴 If treated early, the prognosis is good.\n> 🔴 Late stages with retinal detachment are associated with poor prognosis.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Treated retinopathy of prematurity still carries a raised long-term risk of myopia, distinct from the retinal-detachment risk of untreated, late-stage disease.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.1787-1788" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-3-45", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-insid",
  stem:"Which of the following is a simple test that can be used to monitor AMD?",
  options:["Flouresceine angiography","Amsler grid","OCT","Ophthalmoscopy"],
  answer:1,
  explanation:"The Amsler grid is a grid of straight lines with a central fixation dot that a patient can use at home, one eye at a time, to check for distortion (metamorphopsia) or missing areas within the central 10 degrees of the visual field -- exactly the field the anatomy-and-examination lecture assigns to it (L1,2, slide 123). Because age-related macular degeneration damages central, not peripheral, vision, a test built around fixation and the central field is well suited to catching a new bend in the lines or a new blank patch that signals disease progression; the lecture lists the Amsler grid among the tests of macular function in macular degeneration (L35,36, slide 38); that the patient performs it at home, daily, as a self-monitoring tool is not spelled out in the cached lecture material (not taken from the course material).\n\nThe other three options are all real tests used in macular degeneration but fail the word \"simple\" in the stem: fluorescein angiography is the instrument-based test the chronic-visual-loss lecture lists for macular function, alongside visual acuity, pupillary light reaction, colour vision, ophthalmoscopy, the photostress test and laser interferometry (L35,36, slide 38), and OCT is a clinic imaging test the lecture does not list there (not taken from the course material) -- neither is a test a patient performs alone. Ophthalmoscopy is likewise a clinician's examination technique, not something the patient can do for ongoing self-monitoring between visits. Each of the three requires equipment or a trained examiner, which is what sets the take-home Amsler grid apart as the answer the stem is looking for.\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Amsler grid → A simple test that the patient can use every day at home to check and monitor the presence or progression of AMD.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"The Amsler grid is a simple, patient-administered home test of the central 10 degrees of vision, used to monitor for progression of macular disease such as AMD.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.1791-1792" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-3-58", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-neuro-optic",
  stem:"A 55-year-old male patient complains of severe headache combined with tinnitus. He reports that the condition developed over a period of few weeks. Fundoscopic examination revealed evident papilledema. Which of the following is the best next step to be done in order to properly manage that patient?",
  options:["Prescribe symptomatic therapeutic preparations","Advise the patient to rest and avoid stress","Orbit ultrasonography","MRI brain"],
  answer:3,
  explanation:"Papilledema is bilateral disc swelling from raised intracranial pressure (ICP) -- the optic-nerve lecture's own differential table gives papilledema the single listed etiology \"↑ ICP\" (L21, slide 8). A headache that has been building over weeks, together with objective papilledema on fundoscopy, is a red flag for a mass or other intracranial process raising pressure on the optic nerve heads, so the priority is neuroimaging of the brain (MRI) to find the cause before anything else is done; the lecture itself flags that it never lists what actually raises ICP (no tumour, no idiopathic intracranial hypertension, no venous sinus thrombosis, no hydrocephalus are named), so the specific step of ordering MRI brain, and the tinnitus detail pointing toward a pulsatile-tinnitus pattern, are not spelled out in the cached material (not taken from the course material).\n\nOption A, symptomatic therapy, and option B, rest and stress avoidance, both treat this as a benign tension-type headache and would delay the workup of a potentially dangerous, pressure-raising intracranial lesion -- exactly the wrong response to papilledema. Option C, orbit ultrasonography, images the globe and orbit, not the brain or its ventricles/venous sinuses, so it cannot identify a mass, hydrocephalus or venous sinus thrombosis raising ICP; it is the wrong imaging modality for this presentation, even though ultrasonography does have a role elsewhere in ophthalmology (e.g. in globe trauma).\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Headache of insidious onset and progressive course + papilledema → elevated intracranial pressure → MRI brain.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Progressive headache with papilledema signals raised intracranial pressure and requires MRI brain to find the cause, not symptomatic treatment or orbital imaging.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.1817-1818" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-3-59", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-ret-dr",
  stem:"Which of the following is a well-known cause of diminished visual capability in a diabetic patient?",
  options:["Macular edema","Exudative retinal detachment","Posterior vitreous detachment","All of the above"],
  answer:0,
  explanation:"Diabetic macular oedema is one of the staged categories in the diabetic-retinopathy lecture's own classification of the disease, alongside focal, diffuse and ischaemic patterns and proliferative disease (L19.1, slide 2), and its \"clinically significant\" form is defined by retinal oedema or hard exudates within 500 µm of the centre of the fovea (L19.1, slide 11), while the diffuse pattern is the one the deck marks with variable impairment of visual acuity and a guarded prognosis (L19.1, slide 13). Macular oedema is fluid accumulation at the macula -- the part of the retina responsible for fine, central vision -- so its presence directly reduces visual acuity, and it is one of the disease's most frequently cited causes of vision loss in diabetic patients; the specific ranking of macular oedema as the single most common cause of vision loss in diabetic retinopathy is not stated in the cached lecture material (not taken from the course material), though its listing as a vision-threatening stage of the disease is.\n\nOption B, exudative retinal detachment, is not a typical feature of diabetic eye disease -- diabetic retinopathy instead causes tractional retinal detachment from fibrovascular proliferation and traction, not the exudative (fluid-driven) type, which is more characteristic of conditions such as choroidal tumours or severe uveitis. Option C, posterior vitreous detachment, is an extremely common age-related event that is not itself a diabetes-specific cause of vision loss, and can occur in non-diabetic eyes as well, so it does not answer the stem's premise of a diabetes-specific mechanism. Because B and C are each a poor fit, option D, \"all of the above\", cannot be correct either.\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Causes of loss/diminution of vision in DR are:\n> A. In any stage of DR:\n> i. Macular oedema (The most common cause of vision loss in patients with non-proliferative DR)\n> ii. Macular ischemia\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Macular oedema is a staged, vision-threatening feature of diabetic retinopathy and a well-known cause of diminished vision in diabetic patients.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.1819-1820" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-3-61", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-ret-vasc",
  stem:"An old diabetic & hypertensive male patient was found to have extensive retinal hemorrhages and cotton wool spots. Which of the following is an expected complication in that patient?",
  options:["Neovascularization in the iris","Neovascularization the choroid","Cataract","All of the above"],
  answer:0,
  explanation:"Extensive retinal haemorrhages together with variable cotton-wool spots and severe disc oedema are exactly the printed sign list for the ischaemic form of central retinal vein occlusion (L19.2, slide 12), a condition an old diabetic and hypertensive patient is at particular risk for from combined vascular disease. The same slide lists rubeosis iridis -- new vessel growth on the iris -- occurring in about half of these ischaemic cases, which is the ophthalmic name for iris neovascularization; this makes it the expected complication of this clinical picture, and it is the mechanism behind the sight-threatening neovascular glaucoma that ischaemic CRVO can progress to if untreated.\n\nOption B, neovascularization of the choroid, describes choroidal neovascularization, the process that drives wet age-related macular degeneration, not the retinal vein occlusion this vignette describes; it is a different vascular bed and a different disease altogether. Option C, cataract, is an unrelated, much slower lens change that is not a recognised direct complication of an ischaemic vein occlusion in the way iris neovascularization is. Because B and C do not fit this vignette's mechanism, option D, \"all of the above\", is also incorrect.\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> CRVO may be complicated by Secondary glaucoma (due to neovascularization of the retina and iris), Vitreous hemorrhage, Macular edema.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Extensive retinal haemorrhages with cotton-wool spots describe ischaemic central retinal vein occlusion, whose expected complication is iris neovascularization (rubeosis iridis), which can progress to secondary (neovascular) glaucoma.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.1823-1824" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-3-64", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-squint",
  stem:"What is the age after which treatment of amblyopia is less likely to be successful?",
  options:["4-2 years","6-4 years","10-8 years","15-12 years"],
  answer:2,
  explanation:"Amblyopia is one of the two sensory adaptations to squint named in the strabismus lecture (suppression and amblyopia), whose treatment by occlusion (patching) is also shown in the same deck (L29, slides 22-23 and 32) -- but the deck's own review notes that no acuity threshold, no degree and no age limit for successful treatment is ever printed on either slide (L29). The 8-10-year cutoff given here, after which the visual system's plasticity has largely closed and occlusion or penalisation therapy becomes far less effective, is standard clinical teaching for amblyopia but is not stated in the cached lecture material (not taken from the course material).\n\nThe House bank prints the same question with the same key.\n\nOptions A (4-2 years), B (6-4 years) and D (15-12 years) all give age ranges that do not match the commonly taught 8-10-year plasticity cutoff for amblyopia treatment: A and B are too young, well within the window where treatment is still expected to succeed, and D is implausibly late for meaningful visual plasticity to still be present, making 10-8 the only range consistent with standard teaching on the closing of the amblyogenic sensitive period.\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Amblyopia treatment is less successful after age 8-10 years.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Amblyopia treatment becomes markedly less successful once a child is past roughly 8-10 years of age, as the visual system's plasticity narrows; the lecture teaches amblyopia and its occlusion treatment but gives no age limit itself.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.1829-1830" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-3-70", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-ret-vasc",
  stem:"A 65-year-old man presents with sudden painless severe monocular vision loss. Ophthalmoscopic examination revealed cherry red spots together with venous stasis. Which of the following is a medication prescribed by the ophthalmologist in order to control that acute condition?",
  options:["IV mannitol 20%","Systemic steroids","Topical miotics","All of the above"],
  answer:0,
  explanation:"Sudden, painless, severe monocular vision loss with a cherry-red spot at the macula and marked retinal changes is the classic picture of central retinal artery occlusion (CRAO), printed with the same cherry-red-spot sign in the retinal vascular disorders lecture (L19.2, slide 26); \"venous stasis\" here reflects the sludged, segmented (cattle-trucking) blood column the same slide shows in the affected retinal vessels. Intravenous mannitol is an osmotic diuretic that rapidly lowers intraocular pressure; in CRAO, lowering IOP acutely is one of the emergency measures aimed at dislodging the occluding embolus/thrombus and improving retinal perfusion during the brief window before the retina is irreversibly infarcted. The acute visual loss lecture's own CRAO case leaves its entire management slide blank, naming ocular massage, anterior chamber paracentesis, IOP-lowering drops or acetazolamide, and carbogen/rebreathing as measures that are absent from the deck (L31,32) -- so IV mannitol as a specific emergency IOP-lowering agent for CRAO is not stated in the cached lecture material (not taken from the course material), even though the underlying diagnosis is.\n\nOption B, systemic steroids, is the acute treatment for a different disease process, such as optic neuritis or giant cell arteritis-related ischaemic optic neuropathy, not a vascular occlusion of the retinal circulation. Option C, topical miotics, constrict the pupil and are used in angle-closure glaucoma, not in an arterial occlusion, and would do nothing to improve retinal perfusion. Since B and C do not treat CRAO, option D, \"all of the above\", is also wrong.\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Vigorous measures done by the ophthalmologist to lower IOP in case of CRAO → IOP-lowering medications (including intravenous mannitol), AC paracentesis & vasodilators.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Central retinal artery occlusion presents with sudden painless monocular vision loss and a cherry-red spot, and its emergency management includes IOP-lowering measures such as IV mannitol, aimed at restoring retinal perfusion.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.1841-1842" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-3-77", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-neuro-motil",
  stem:"A 32-year-old woman sees an ophthalmologist as she suffers from eyelid drooping together with eye deviation outwards and downwards. Examination revealed unilateral mydriasis. Which of the following nerve paralysis is the cause of the patient’s manifestations?",
  options:["Oculomotor nerve","Trochlear nerve","Abducent nerve","Facial nerve"],
  answer:0,
  explanation:"The eyelid disease lecture shows exactly this triad on one slide: complete ptosis of the upper lid, and when the lid is lifted, the eye is found deviated out and down with a dilated pupil -- captioned in the lecture's own transcript as \"the classic third-nerve palsy posture\" (L5, slide 10). The oculomotor (third cranial) nerve supplies the levator palpebrae superioris (so its palsy causes ptosis), most of the extraocular muscles that adduct, elevate and depress the eye (so its palsy leaves the eye pulled out and down by the unopposed lateral rectus and superior oblique), and the parasympathetic fibres to the pupillary sphincter (so its palsy, when the pupil fibres are affected, causes a dilated, unreactive pupil -- mydriasis). All three of the stem's findings therefore point to the same single nerve.\n\nOption B, trochlear nerve, supplies only the superior oblique and its palsy produces vertical/torsional diplopia, not ptosis or mydriasis. Option C, abducent nerve, supplies only the lateral rectus, so its palsy causes an inward-deviated eye (esotropia) that cannot abduct, the opposite of the outward deviation described here, and again no ptosis or pupil involvement. Option D, facial nerve, supplies the orbicularis oculi (eyelid closure) and other facial muscles, not the levator or the extraocular muscles, so its palsy causes lagophthalmos (inability to close the eye) rather than ptosis, and has no effect on ocular deviation or pupil size.\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Oculomotor nerve palsy is characterized by → Ptosis (masks the diplopia), Mydriasis (in compressive lesions e.g., aneurysm or tumour), Loss of elevation, depression, and adduction & The eye is turned outward and downward.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Ptosis with an eye deviated out and down and a dilated pupil is the classic presentation of oculomotor (third) nerve palsy, distinct from trochlear, abducent or facial nerve palsies.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.1855-1856; reprinted in Model Exam 5 at p.2165-2166" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-4-5", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-uvea",
  stem:"Which of the following is best described as (tremulousness of the iris with eye movement)?",
  options:["Iridodialysis","Iridodonesis","Iridocyclitis","None of the above"],
  answer:1,
  explanation:"Iridodonesis is trembling or wobbling of the iris on eye movement, seen when the lens no longer gives the iris its normal firm posterior support -- classically in aphakia (lens absent) or a subluxated/dislocated lens where the zonules holding the lens have weakened or torn. The uvea & sclera lecture's iris overview slide (L13,14, slide 5) lists \"Tremulous iris (iridodonesis)\" as one of five headline iris signs (alongside colour change, synechiae, iris defect and rubeosis iridis), though the slide gives it as a heading only and never defines or re-explains it elsewhere in the deck. The cataract lecture's pseudophakia/aphakia comparison table (L18, slide 19) names iridodonesis directly as a \"classic sign\" of the aphakic eye, alongside a deep anterior chamber, a jet-black pupil and the absence of the lens -- all consequences of losing the lens's normal support and bulk.\n\nOption A, iridodialysis, is a different iris sign entirely: a traumatic tear of the iris root away from the ciliary body, producing a peripheral crescentic defect rather than a tremor, and it is listed under \"iris defect\" on the same overview slide as a separate branch from iridodonesis. Option C, iridocyclitis, is inflammation of the iris and ciliary body (anterior uveitis) -- a disease process with pain, redness and keratic precipitates, not a physical sign of iris movement, and it is not what the stem's description of tremulousness with eye movement refers to.\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Iridodonesis means trembling of the iris during eye movement, usually due to absence of lens support (as in aphakia or subluxated lens). It indicates loss of zonular or lenticular stability.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Iridodonesis is tremulousness of the iris with eye movement, caused by loss of lens support in aphakia or lens subluxation, distinct from iridodialysis (a traumatic iris-root tear) and iridocyclitis (anterior uveitis).",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.1866-1867" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-4-7", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-uvea",
  stem:"A 28-year-old female patient complains of ocular pain, excessive lacrimation together with diminished vision. Examination revealed keratic precipitates, hypopyon and ciliary injection with violaceous hue. which of the Following is an expected complication?",
  options:["Glaucoma","Cataract","Posterior synechiae","Calcific band keratopathy","All of the above"],
  answer:4,
  explanation:"The vignette's pain, lacrimation, diminished vision, keratic precipitates, hypopyon and a violaceous (ciliary) flush are the classic picture of anterior uveitis (iridocyclitis). The uvea & sclera lecture devotes two consecutive slides to exactly this question -- its \"Sequelae and complications\" slides (L13,14, slides 18-19) -- and between them list all four named complications: slide 18 gives posterior synechiae and secondary glaucoma, each illustrated with a photograph of a pupil distorted into a festooned shape by adhesions to the lens; slide 19 gives complicated cataract and band-shaped keratopathy, each with its own photograph (a dense whitish pupillary opacity for the cataract, a horizontal grey-white band across the interpalpebral cornea for the keratopathy). Because the lecture prints all four as recognised sequelae of anterior uveitis, \"all of the above\" is the correct, most complete answer.\n\nEach individual option is therefore true rather than false: posterior synechiae form when the inflamed iris adheres to the anterior lens capsule, secondary glaucoma follows from synechiae blocking aqueous outflow or from inflammatory debris clogging the trabecular meshwork, complicated cataract results from chronic inflammatory disturbance of lens metabolism, and band keratopathy follows calcium deposition in a chronically inflamed anterior segment (calcium salt deposition in Bowman's layer is described separately in the cornea lecture, L15,16). Since the question asks which is \"an expected complication\" and every option names a real one, none can be picked out as the sole exception, which is exactly why the lecture's own layout groups all four together as the disease's sequelae.\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Chronic iridocyclitis may lead to posterior synechiae, secondary glaucoma, cataract formation, and band keratopathy due to prolonged inflammation.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Anterior uveitis (iridocyclitis) can lead to posterior synechiae, secondary glaucoma, complicated cataract and band-shaped keratopathy, all recognised sequelae of the disease.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.1870-1871" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-4-12", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-vitreous",
  stem:"A 22-year-old male patient presented to the ophthalmology clinic with a complaint of seeing bugs or cobwebs together with light flashes. Ophthalmoscopy revealed an evident Weiss ring and absent red reflex. Which of the following is the best next step in the management of that patient?",
  options:["Reassurance and follow-up in 1 month","Optical coherence tomography (OCT)","CT scan of the orbit","Dilated fundus examination"],
  answer:3,
  explanation:"Floaters described as \"bugs or cobwebs\" together with light flashes and a Weiss ring on ophthalmoscopy are the classic presentation of acute posterior vitreous detachment (PVD): the Weiss ring is the detached, ring-shaped glial remnant that used to encircle the optic disc, now floating free in the vitreous once the posterior hyaloid has separated from the disc. The retinal detachment and degenerations lecture gives only \"aging (vitreous shrinkage)\" as the vitreous change behind detachment and never names posterior vitreous detachment or the Weiss ring (L20), so both terms are standard teaching rather than printed course material (not taken from the course material). The clinical concern with any acute, symptomatic PVD is that the separating vitreous can tug on the peripheral retina hard enough to tear it, and a retinal tear can progress to retinal detachment if missed. Because a tear can be entirely asymptomatic itself and is only found on careful peripheral examination, the correct next step for a patient presenting with acute flashes and floaters is a dilated fundus examination with scleral depression to inspect the full peripheral retina for a tear before it detaches.\n\nOption A, reassurance and follow-up in a month, is unsafe here because it defers the one examination that can catch a treatable retinal tear while it is still asymptomatic; a month's delay risks the tear progressing to a detachment. Option B, OCT, images the macula and posterior pole in cross-section and does not show the peripheral retina where PVD-related tears occur, so it is not the right tool for this specific concern. Option C, CT scan of the orbit, is not indicated at all -- there is no history of trauma or suspected orbital pathology here, and CT does not resolve retinal detail. The absent red reflex mentioned in the stem, together with the visible Weiss ring, likely reflects vitreous debris/haemorrhage obscuring the view, which is itself a reason to examine the retina promptly and thoroughly (not taken from the course material, though this reasoning follows standard clinical teaching).\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Flashes, floaters, and a Weiss ring indicate posterior vitreous detachment, which can be associated with retinal tears. A dilated fundus exam is crucial to rule out retinal detachment.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Acute posterior vitreous detachment with a Weiss ring presenting with flashes and floaters requires a dilated fundus examination to exclude a retinal tear, rather than reassurance, OCT or orbital imaging.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.1880-1881" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-4-14", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-ret-vasc",
  stem:"Which of the following is NOT among the differential diagnosis of cherry red spot seen in ophthalmoscopy?",
  options:["CRAO / BRAO","Penetrating eye trauma","Niemann-pick disease","None of the above"],
  answer:1,
  explanation:"A cherry-red spot appears when the surrounding retina turns pale and opacified from ischaemia or from lipid/ganglioside storage while the thin fovea, fed directly by the underlying choroid, stays its normal red colour by contrast -- the spot is therefore always a sign of retinal opacification around an intact fovea, never of trauma itself. The retinal vascular disorders lecture lists \"'Cherry-red spot' at macula\" among the signs of central retinal artery occlusion (CRAO) (L19.2), and the acute visual loss lecture describes a patient whose retinal examination \"reveals a white, opacified retina with a cherry-red macular spot\" in the context of sudden painless visual loss from arterial occlusion (L31,32). CRAO/BRAO is therefore a genuine cause. Storage diseases such as Niemann-Pick disease (and, more classically, Tay-Sachs disease) cause a cherry-red spot through accumulation of lipid/ganglioside material in the retinal ganglion cells surrounding the fovea, producing the same pale-retina-with-red-centre appearance (Niemann-Pick disease as a cause of the cherry-red spot is not itself printed in the ophthalmology lectures searched; this is standard teaching -- not taken from the course material).\n\nOption B, penetrating eye trauma, is the answer that does not belong: a penetrating injury damages the globe mechanically (cornea, sclera, uvea, lens, vitreous or retina directly) and does not produce the specific ischaemic/storage pallor around the fovea that generates a cherry-red spot; trauma is not part of this differential in any of the standard causes (retinal artery occlusion, storage diseases such as Tay-Sachs and Niemann-Pick, and commotio retinae (Berlin's oedema), which the trauma lecture itself illustrates with a fundus photograph labelled CHERRY-RED SPOT under BLUNT trauma (L25, slide 35/36) — blunt, never penetrating). Since CRAO/BRAO and Niemann-Pick disease are both genuine causes, and the stem asks which is NOT a cause, penetrating trauma is correctly excluded and \"none of the above\" does not apply.\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> A cherry-red spot is seen in central retinal artery occlusion and certain storage diseases (e.g., Tay-Sachs, Niemann-Pick), but not in penetrating trauma.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"A cherry-red spot is caused by retinal artery occlusion (CRAO/BRAO) or storage diseases such as Niemann-Pick disease, not by penetrating eye trauma.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.1884-1885" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-4-15", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-ret-dr",
  stem:"Which of the following is the leading cause of irreversible blindness in the western world?",
  options:["Diabetic retinopathy","Hypertensive retinopathy","AMD","CRVO","None of the above"],
  answer:2,
  explanation:"The chronic visual loss lecture's own \"relevance\" slide on macular degeneration states this directly: \"In the United States, age-related macular degeneration is the leading cause of irreversible central visual loss (20/200 or worse) among people aged 52 or older\" (L35,36). Age-related macular degeneration (AMD) damages the central retina at the macula, and because it is degenerative and progressive rather than acutely treatable in its advanced (dry, geographic-atrophy) form, it accounts for more permanent, irreversible vision loss in older populations in developed countries than the other listed conditions. The same lecture separately notes glaucoma as the \"leading cause of irreversible blindness\" more broadly and as the second most important cause of blindness in the United States -- the two statements sit in the same lecture without being reconciled into a single ranking, but the AMD-specific relevance slide is the one that names the \"western world\"/United States context the stem asks about, and it is the printed source for this key.\n\nDiabetic retinopathy is a major cause of vision loss, especially in the working-age population, but it is more often at least partially treatable (panretinal photocoagulation, anti-VEGF) and is not the condition this lecture names as the leading cause in the western world. Hypertensive retinopathy is a marker of systemic vascular damage but is rarely itself the leading direct cause of blindness. CRVO (central retinal vein occlusion) can cause severe vision loss in the affected eye but is a less common, usually unilateral condition and is not ranked as a leading population-level cause of irreversible blindness in the source material.\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> AMD is the leading cause of irreversible blindness in developed countries due to degeneration of the macula affecting central vision.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Age-related macular degeneration is named in the lecture as the leading cause of irreversible central visual loss in the western world, ahead of diabetic retinopathy, hypertensive retinopathy and CRVO.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.1886-1887" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-4-18", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-ret-dr",
  stem:"Which of the following is a cause of vision loss in diabetic retinopathy?",
  options:["Retinal artery occlusion","Optic neuritis","Vitreous hemorrhage","Retinitis pigmentosa"],
  answer:2,
  explanation:"In proliferative diabetic retinopathy, chronic retinal ischaemia drives neovascularization -- new, fragile vessels growing either on the optic disc (NVD) or elsewhere on the retina (NVE), as the diabetic retinopathy lecture names them (L19.1). These abnormal vessels lack the structural integrity of normal retinal vessels and bleed easily into the vitreous cavity, producing a vitreous haemorrhage that scatters and blocks incoming light, causing sudden or progressive visual loss; the same lecture lists persistent or recurrent haemorrhage as a sign of poor involution after laser photocoagulation, and lists \"severe persistent vitreous haemorrhage\" as an indication for vitreoretinal surgery, underlining how central vitreous haemorrhage is to vision loss in this disease. Diabetic maculopathy (leakage or ischaemia at the macula) is the other major mechanism of vision loss printed in the same lecture, but among the options given here, vitreous haemorrhage is the one directly caused by the disease's proliferative vascular complications.\n\nRetinal artery occlusion is a separate vascular catastrophe (embolic or thrombotic occlusion of the central or a branch retinal artery) and, while diabetes is a risk factor for vascular disease in general, it is not itself a mechanism of vision loss within diabetic retinopathy specifically. Optic neuritis is inflammation of the optic nerve, classically linked to demyelinating disease, and is not a recognised complication of diabetic retinopathy. Retinitis pigmentosa is an inherited, progressive photoreceptor degeneration with an entirely separate pathophysiology (rod-cone dystrophy) unrelated to diabetic vascular disease (not taken from the course material for retinitis pigmentosa's definition, though its distinctness from diabetic retinopathy is standard).\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> In diabetic retinopathy, neovascularization can rupture, causing vitreous hemorrhage and sudden loss of vision.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Vitreous haemorrhage from fragile neovascular vessels (NVD/NVE) is a recognised cause of vision loss in proliferative diabetic retinopathy, unlike retinal artery occlusion, optic neuritis or retinitis pigmentosa.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.1892-1893" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-4-32", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-cat",
  stem:"An old male patient reports an improvement in his near vision and that he no longer needs his reading glasses, however, he reports that his far vision has deteriorated. Which of the following is the name of the described phenomenon?",
  options:["Presbyopia.","Myopic shift.","Posterior subcapsular cataract.","Second sight phenomenon."],
  answer:3,
  explanation:"As nuclear sclerosis of the lens progresses, the lens nucleus becomes denser and its refractive index rises, which increases the eye's overall refractive power and produces a myopic shift; the cataract lecture names this pairing directly and gives it its own label, \"second sight\" -- \"elderly regain near vision\" through the myopic shift, described elsewhere on the same slide set as letting the patient where \"myopic shift allows reading without glasses (temporary)\" (L18, slide 5). The lecture's own summary table for senile nuclear cataract lists \"Second sight (myopia)\" as a classic clinical feature of this specific cataract type, distinguishing it from other cataract morphologies. The phenomenon is called \"second sight\" precisely because it feels to the patient like a return of the near vision they lost to presbyopia, even though it is actually a sign of progressing cataract, not a true improvement.\n\nMyopic shift, option B, is the underlying optical mechanism (the eye becoming more myopic as the nucleus densifies) rather than the name of the overall clinical phenomenon the stem describes, which is specifically the paradoxical gain in near vision alongside worsening distance vision -- the lecture treats \"myopic shift\" and \"second sight\" as linked but distinguishable labels, with \"second sight\" being the named phenomenon asked for here. Presbyopia, option A, is the normal age-related loss of accommodation that makes reading glasses necessary in the first place, the opposite direction of change from what the stem describes, and is not caused by lens sclerosis. Posterior subcapsular cataract, option C, is a different cataract morphology that classically causes disproportionate glare and difficulty in bright light with relatively preserved distance vision in dim conditions, not the myopic near-vision improvement described here.\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> In nuclear sclerosis cataract, the refractive index of the lens increases, causing a myopic shift. Patients temporarily experience better near vision, known as the second sight phenomenon, before overall vision worsens.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Nuclear sclerotic cataract raises the lens's refractive index, producing a myopic shift that lets an older patient temporarily read without glasses -- the \"second sight\" phenomenon -- while distance vision worsens.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.1920-1921" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-4-42", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-neuro",
  stem:"A 39-year-old male presents with unilateral miosis, eyelid drooping (ptosis), and apparent enophthalmos. Which of the following is the most appropriate investigation to confirm the diagnosis?",
  options:["MRI abdomen","MRI thorax","Echocardiography","CT brain"],
  answer:1,
  explanation:"Miosis, ptosis and apparent enophthalmos are three of the signs the pupil and visual pathway lecture prints on its Horner syndrome slide, alongside dilation lag, anhydrosis and (in the congenital form) heterochromia (L22, slide 11) -- together these make up Horner syndrome, caused by interruption of the oculosympathetic pathway anywhere along its three-neuron chain from the hypothalamus down through the neck to the eye. The lecture separately notes that sympathetic paralysis is one of its own listed neurologic causes of an abnormal pupil (L22), but does not itself work through the localising investigations for a given lesion level; the choice of MRI thorax reflects the fact that a lesion of the first-order/second-order sympathetic chain low in the neck or apex of the chest -- classically a Pancoast (apical lung) tumour compressing the sympathetic chain -- is an important and dangerous cause of an acquired Horner syndrome in an adult, and thoracic imaging is the investigation aimed at finding it (this specific localisation and investigation choice is not printed in the searched lecture material -- not taken from the course material, though it reflects standard clinical teaching for a new adult-onset Horner syndrome).\n\nMRI abdomen is not directed at any level of the sympathetic pathway relevant to Horner syndrome and would not be expected to identify a causative lesion. Echocardiography images the heart and is unrelated to the sympathetic chain. CT brain would be relevant if a central (first-order neuron) lesion such as a brainstem or hypothalamic stroke were suspected, but the combination given here, in isolation, is the classic pattern prompting a search for a chest lesion first, making MRI thorax the best of the four listed options.\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Ptosis, miosis, and enophthalmos indicate Horner’s syndrome, which can be caused by a lesion anywhere along the sympathetic chain, including apical lung tumors. MRI of the thorax helps identify a Pancoast tumor.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Miosis, ptosis and apparent enophthalmos define Horner syndrome from oculosympathetic pathway interruption; MRI thorax is used to look for a causative lesion such as an apical (Pancoast) lung tumour along the chain.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.1940-1941" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-4-45", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-ret-vasc",
  stem:"Which of the following is the most common cause of CRVO / BRVO?",
  options:["Embolism","Thrombosis","Arteritis","None of the above"],
  answer:1,
  explanation:"The retinal vascular disorders lecture's predisposing-factors slide for retinal vein occlusion lists increasing age, hypertension, diabetes and abnormalities of coagulation as systemic risk factors, and raised intraocular pressure and periphlebitis as ocular risk factors, then diagrams the pathophysiology as venous occlusion leading to stagnation, hypoxia and increased extravascular pressure, ending in oedema and haemorrhage (L19.2). The lecture's own diagram of venous occlusion does not use the word \"thrombosis\" explicitly in the searched material, but a slow-flow, stagnation-driven occlusion of a vein at a site of external compression (as at an arteriovenous crossing) is the mechanism thrombosis describes, and thrombotic occlusion at these crossing points is the standard, widely taught mechanism for both central and branch retinal vein occlusion (not taken from the course material as a verbatim statement, though it follows directly from the risk-factor and pathophysiology slides printed there).\n\nEmbolism, option A, is the mechanism for retinal artery occlusion, not vein occlusion -- an embolus lodges in and blocks an artery, and the lecture lists retinal artery occlusion (branch, cilioretinal and central) as a separate disease entity from retinal vein occlusion, each with its own distinct mechanism. Arteritis, option C, refers to inflammatory disease of arteries (such as giant cell arteritis), which is a recognised cause of retinal artery occlusion in older patients, not of venous occlusion. Since thrombosis is the correct mechanism, \"none of the above\" does not apply.\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Both CRVO and BRVO most commonly result from thrombosis of the retinal vein at arteriovenous crossings. Risk factors include hypertension, diabetes, and glaucoma.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"CRVO and BRVO most commonly arise from thrombotic venous occlusion, typically at sites of arteriovenous crossing compression, distinct from the embolic mechanism of retinal artery occlusion or inflammatory arteritis.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.1946-1947" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-4-49", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-trauma",
  stem:"Which of the following has the poorest prognosis ever?",
  options:["Injury to eye by sulphuric acid","Injury to eye by hydrochloric acid","Injury to eye by sodium hydroxide","All have similar effect and prognosis"],
  answer:2,
  explanation:"The trauma lecture's own comparison of alkalis and acids states that alkalis \"cause more damage as they tend to penetrate more deeply than acids and continue penetration for weeks,\" while \"acids coagulate surface proteins, forming a protective barrier against further penetration\" that limits how deep the injury goes (L25, slide 4). Sodium hydroxide (NaOH) is listed among the alkalis in the trauma lecture's chemical-injury slide (L26), together with KOH, Ca(OH)2 and NH4, and because alkalis combine with cell-membrane lipid and stromal mucopolysaccharides to achieve deep, ongoing penetration, an alkali burn such as sodium hydroxide carries the worse prognosis of the two chemical classes, consistent with progressive tissue destruction, limbal ischaemia, loss of limbal stem cells, corneal stromal opacification and later scarring, symblepharon and vascularisation described on the following slides.\n\nSulphuric acid and hydrochloric acid, options A and B, are both named acids in the same lecture, and acids coagulate surface proteins into a barrier that limits further penetration -- meaning that, while a severe acid burn can still be very damaging, the mechanism itself is self-limiting compared with an alkali's continued penetration over weeks, so an acid burn generally has a better prognosis than an equivalent alkali burn. Because sodium hydroxide is specifically an alkali and the two acids are not, option D (\"all have similar effect and prognosis\") is incorrect -- the lecture explicitly separates alkalis from acids by mechanism and severity rather than treating them as equivalent.\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Alkali burns (like sodium hydroxide) penetrate deeply, causing progressive tissue destruction and scarring. They have the worst prognosis compared to acid burns.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Alkali chemical burns such as sodium hydroxide penetrate more deeply and for longer than acid burns (which form a self-limiting protein-coagulation barrier), giving alkali injuries the poorer prognosis.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.1954-1955" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-4-55", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-systemic",
  stem:"What is the grade of hypertensive retinopathy characterized by (obvious arterial narrowing with focal irregularities)?",
  options:["Grade 1","Grade 2","Grade 3","Grade 4"],
  answer:1,
  explanation:"The lecture on ocular manifestations of systemic disease presents the Modified Scheie classification of hypertensive retinopathy as a four-grade scale: grade 0 is no changes, grade 1 is barely detectable narrowing, grade 2 is obvious arterial narrowing with focal irregularities, grade 3 adds retinal haemorrhages and/or exudates on top of grade 2's vascular findings, and grade 4 adds disc oedema on top of grade 3 (L23). \"Obvious arterial narrowing with focal irregularities,\" the exact phrase in the stem, is the defining feature the lecture assigns to grade 2, marking it as more advanced vascular sclerosis than the barely detectable narrowing of grade 1 but without yet showing the haemorrhages or exudates that define grade 3.\n\nGrade 1 is excluded because its narrowing is described as only \"barely detectable,\" not the \"obvious\" narrowing with focal irregularities the stem asks about. Grade 3 is excluded because it is defined as grade 2's findings plus additional retinal changes such as haemorrhages and/or exudates, which are not mentioned in the stem's description. Grade 4 is excluded for the same reason, being grade 3's findings plus optic disc swelling, a more severe and distinct additional feature not described here. The stem's wording matches grade 2 alone.\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Grade 2 hypertensive retinopathy is characterized by obvious arterial narrowing with focal irregularities (arteriovenous nipping). It reflects moderate vascular sclerosis due to sustained hypertension.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"In the Modified Scheie classification, grade 2 hypertensive retinopathy is defined by obvious arterial narrowing with focal irregularities, more advanced than grade 1's barely detectable narrowing but without the haemorrhages/exudates of grade 3 or the disc oedema of grade 4.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.1966-1967" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-4-59", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-intro-exam",
  stem:"Which of the following is the gold-standard method for IOP measurement?",
  options:["Digital method","Schiotz tonometer","Goldmann applanation tonometer","Hand held electronic tonometer"],
  answer:2,
  explanation:"Goldmann applanation tonometry flattens a small, fixed area of the central cornea and reads the force needed to do so; because the area flattened is fixed, the reading is largely independent of scleral rigidity, unlike the indentation methods, which is why it is treated as the reference standard against which every other method is checked. The ocular-examination lecture places it directly after the digital and indentation methods as its own dedicated slide pair, illustrating the slit-lamp-mounted instrument with its bi-prism, mobile feeder arm and adjustable control weight (L3,4, slide 38-39).\n\nOption A, the digital method, is the bedside estimate performed by asking the patient to look down and testing the globe's resistance to fluctuation with the index fingers while the other fingers rest on the forehead and temple; the same lecture's slide immediately before the applanation slides shows this technique (L3,4, slide 37), and it is a rough, examiner-dependent screening test rather than a measurement instrument. Option B, the Schiotz tonometer, works by indentation -- a plunger of known weight indents the cornea, and the amount of indentation is converted to a pressure reading via a calibration table (L3,4, slide 40); it is portable and simple but its reading is distorted by scleral rigidity, corneal thickness and even the patient's position, which is why it is not the reference method. Option D, the hand-held electronic tonometer, is a modern, portable applanation-based device that is convenient at the bedside or in a clinic without a slit lamp, but its precision is still validated against, and does not surpass, standard Goldmann applanation tonometry (not taken from the course material for this specific precision comparison, though it follows from the slide's own framing of Goldmann as the reference method).\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> The Goldmann applanation tonometer is the gold standard for intraocular pressure measurement, based on flattening a fixed corneal area. It provides the most accurate and reproducible readings.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Goldmann applanation tonometry, which flattens a fixed corneal area, is the gold-standard method for measuring intraocular pressure.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.1974-1975" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-4-60", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-intro",
  stem:"Which of the following actions is performed by the superior rectus muscle?",
  options:["Elevation, adduction & intorsion.","Depression, adduction & extorsion.","Depression, abduction & intorsion.","Elevation, abduction & extorsion."],
  answer:0,
  explanation:"The anatomy and physiology lecture's own table of extraocular muscle actions lists the superior rectus with elevation as its primary action, intorsion as its secondary action and adduction as its tertiary action (L1,2, slide 69) -- so the muscle elevates the eye and, as secondary effects, also adducts and intorts it, matching option A exactly. These secondary and tertiary actions arise because the superior rectus inserts on the globe at an angle to the visual axis rather than in line with it, so its pull produces components of rotation in addition to its main elevating action (L1,2, slide 65-66, which prints the 23° angle between the visual axis and the orbital axis).\n\nOption B, depression, adduction and extorsion, is the table's row for the inferior rectus, whose primary action is depression, with extorsion secondary and adduction tertiary -- the direction of the primary action (depression instead of elevation) is what makes this wrong for the superior rectus. Option C, depression, abduction and intorsion, does not match any single muscle's row in the table -- the superior oblique has intorsion primary, but its secondary and tertiary actions are depression and abduction respectively, not paired with intorsion as its main action, so this combination misassembles two different muscles' columns. Option D, elevation, abduction and extorsion, is the row for the inferior oblique, whose primary action is extorsion (not elevation), with elevation secondary and abduction tertiary -- again the primary-action column does not match what the stem asks for the superior rectus.\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> The superior rectus muscle primarily elevates the eye and also contributes to adduction and intorsion. Its actions depend on the position of the eyeball relative to the visual axis.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"The superior rectus muscle's primary action is elevation, with intorsion and adduction as its secondary and tertiary actions.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.1976-1977" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-4-65", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-neuro-optic",
  stem:"A 68-year-old man with a history of hypertension and diabetes mellitus presents with sudden, painless loss of vision in his right eye. He denies any trauma, eye redness, or discharge. Fundoscopic examination reveals a swollen, pale optic disc with splinter hemorrhages at the disc margin. What is the most likely diagnosis?",
  options:["Anterior ischemic optic neuropathy (AION)","Retinal detachment","Central retinal artery occlusion","Optic neuritis"],
  answer:0,
  explanation:"Anterior ischemic optic neuropathy (AION) produces sudden, painless monocular vision loss with a swollen, pale disc, and it is classically seen in older patients with vascular risk factors such as hypertension and diabetes, which is exactly this patient's profile. The optic-nerve lecture's dedicated AION slide shows this same picture directly: a swollen disc with blurred margins, a pale-yellow colour and a small flame haemorrhage at the disc margin (L21, slide 9), and the deck separately notes that the swollen disc of acute AION goes on to become pale as the nerve infarcts. Splinter/flame haemorrhages at the disc margin, as in this stem, are part of that same acute picture, reflecting ischaemia of the optic nerve head's blood supply (the short posterior ciliary arteries) rather than any retinal or vitreous process.\n\nOption B, retinal detachment, classically presents with flashes, floaters and a curtain-like visual field defect, and fundoscopy shows an elevated, folded retina rather than disc swelling -- it does not produce splinter haemorrhages at the disc margin. Option C, central retinal artery occlusion, also causes sudden painless vision loss, but the fundus shows a pale, oedematous retina with a cherry-red spot at the macula and attenuated vessels, not a swollen pale disc with margin haemorrhages -- the pathology is in the retina's blood supply, not the optic nerve head itself. Option D, optic neuritis, typically affects younger patients, is often painful (particularly on eye movement), and the disc may look normal (retrobulbar) or swollen (papillitis) but without the vascular risk-factor profile or the haemorrhagic picture described here; it is also usually associated with a relative afferent pupillary defect and colour desaturation out of proportion to acuity loss, not itself distinguishing findings emphasised in this vignette.\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> AION presents with sudden, painless vision loss and a swollen pale optic disc with hemorrhages. It is due to ischemia of the optic nerve head, often linked to hypertension or diabetes.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Anterior ischemic optic neuropathy causes sudden painless vision loss with a swollen, pale optic disc and margin haemorrhages in patients with vascular risk factors such as hypertension and diabetes.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.1986-1987" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-4-66", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-orbit",
  stem:"A 32-year-old woman presents with complaints of a noticeable change in her facial appearance, including swelling around her eyes and a retracted upper eyelid appearance. She also reports insomnia, palpitations, weight loss despite increased appetite, and heat intolerance over the past two months. On examination, she has exophthalmos and a fine tremor of the hands. Which investigation should be done first?",
  options:["Thyroid function tests","Orbital CT scan","Blood glucose levels","Chest X-ray"],
  answer:0,
  explanation:"This patient's combination of periorbital swelling, upper-lid retraction and exophthalmos alongside systemic hyperthyroid symptoms -- palpitations, weight loss with a good appetite, heat intolerance and a fine tremor -- is the classic picture of Graves ophthalmopathy (thyroid eye disease). The orbit lecture's own slide on thyroid eye disease lists this same symptom cluster for the underlying Graves disease: weight loss despite good appetite, increased bowel frequency, sweating, heat intolerance, nervousness, palpitations, tremor and warm sweaty skin (L10, slide 15), with the ocular soft-tissue and lid-retraction signs (including the four lid signs) detailed on the following slides. Because the systemic disease drives the eye findings, confirming the thyroid status is the logical first step before ordering eye-specific imaging (not taken from the course material as an explicit \"first investigation\" statement, but it follows directly from the diagnosis the slide describes).\n\nOption B, an orbital CT scan, is useful once thyroid eye disease is suspected or confirmed, to assess extraocular muscle enlargement and orbital crowding, particularly if compressive optic neuropathy is a concern, but it does not establish the underlying thyroid diagnosis and is not the first test to order in a patient whose presentation is this classically systemic. Option C, blood glucose levels, would be relevant if a diabetic or metabolic cause were suspected, but none of the symptoms described (heat intolerance, weight loss with preserved appetite, tremor, palpitations) point toward diabetes rather than thyrotoxicosis. Option D, a chest X-ray, has no direct role in diagnosing thyroid eye disease; it might be used incidentally to screen for a substernal goitre or cardiac size in longstanding thyrotoxicosis, but it does not confirm the diagnosis suggested by this vignette.\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> In a patient with exophthalmos, lid retraction, and hyperthyroid symptoms, the first investigation should be thyroid function tests to confirm Graves’ disease.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"In a patient presenting with exophthalmos, lid retraction and systemic hyperthyroid symptoms suggesting Graves ophthalmopathy, thyroid function tests are the investigation to obtain first.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.1988-1989" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-4-68", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-uvea",
  stem:"In iridocyclitis, which of the following is the expected change that will occur in intraocular pressure?",
  options:["It decreases","It increases","It may either decrease or increase","It is not affected"],
  answer:2,
  explanation:"Iridocyclitis is anterior uveitis, and the uvea lecture's own slide on its pupil and IOP signs states this variability directly: IOP is \"usually decreased but may be normal or increased\" (L13,14, slide 17). The decrease is the more common pattern, and it happens because the inflamed ciliary body produces less aqueous humour (ciliary shutdown); the increase, when it occurs, happens because inflammatory cells and protein debris clog the trabecular meshwork, or because synechiae (adhesions between the iris and lens or angle structures) obstruct aqueous outflow -- the same lecture's management slide separately notes that antiglaucoma medications are added specifically \"in case of increased IOP\" (L13,14, slide 20), which only makes sense if a rise is a recognised possibility in this disease.\n\nOption A, that IOP always decreases, ignores the fact that a rise can and does occur through the outflow-obstruction mechanisms above, so it overstates a one-directional effect. Option B, that IOP always increases, is the opposite error -- it ignores the more common ciliary-shutdown mechanism that lowers aqueous production and pressure in most affected eyes. Option D, that IOP is not affected, is directly contradicted by the lecture's own slide, which lists IOP change as one of the disease's own listed signs alongside the pupil findings -- the only question is which direction it moves in a given eye, not whether it moves at all.\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> In iridocyclitis (anterior uveitis), intraocular pressure (IOP) may initially decrease due to reduced aqueous humor production from ciliary body inflammation, but later increase due to blockage of the trabecular meshwork by inflammatory cells. Thus, IOP changes are variable.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"In iridocyclitis, intraocular pressure is variable — usually decreased from reduced aqueous production, but it may rise if outflow is obstructed by inflammatory debris or synechiae.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.1992-1993" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-4-73", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-cat",
  stem:"Which of the following best describes Morgagnian Cataract ?",
  options:["It's a form of mature cataract with lens cortex liquefaction causing the dark nucleus to sink inferiorly inside the lens capsule","It's a form of hypermature cataract with lens cortex liquefaction causing the dark nucleus to sink inferiorly inside the lens capsule","It's a type of cataract in which the lens nucleus acquires a dark brown to black color","None of the above"],
  answer:1,
  explanation:"The cataract lecture's own classification places the Morgagnian cataract as a subtype of the hypermature senile cortical cataract, defined as \"liquefaction of cortex and sinking of nucleus\" (L17, slide 13), sitting alongside the \"typical\" hypermature form (shrinkage of the capsule with calcification and cholesterol deposits). Hypermature cataracts follow the mature stage, once the lens capsule itself begins to shrink and the softened cortex liquefies into a milky fluid; in the Morgagnian variant specifically, the denser brunescent nucleus is no longer supported and settles inferiorly within the capsular bag, giving the lens a fluid-filled appearance with the nucleus visibly displaced downward.\n\nOption A describes the same cortex-liquefaction-and-sinking-nucleus mechanism but attaches it to the mature stage rather than the hypermature stage; the lecture's table places this specific finding under the hypermature heading, one stage further along than simple maturity, so labelling it \"mature\" is the wrong stage for this appearance. Option C, a lens nucleus that is dark brown to black in colour, describes a brunescent (black) cataract, which is a nuclear sclerotic cataract classified by nuclear colour rather than by cortical liquefaction and nuclear descent -- it is a real but different entity from the Morgagnian pattern being asked about. Option D, none of the above, is incorrect because option B is a faithful match to the lecture's own definition.\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Morgagnian cataract is an advanced (hypermature) stage where the lens cortex liquefies, and the dense nucleus settles inferiorly. This gives a fluid-filled appearance within the capsule.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Morgagnian cataract is a hypermature senile cortical cataract in which the liquefied cortex allows the dense nucleus to sink inferiorly within the capsule.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2002-2003" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-4-77", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-trauma",
  stem:"A small intraocular iron foreign body:",
  options:["can be left if it doesn’t harm the eye.","must be removed.","necessitates enucleation.","can only be removed if it produces cataract"],
  answer:1,
  explanation:"An intraocular iron foreign body (IOFB) must be removed regardless of its size, because retained iron dissolves and reacts with ocular tissues over time, producing progressive toxic damage to the retina, lens and other structures (siderosis bulbi) -- damage that continues even while the eye looks otherwise stable, which is why observation is not an acceptable strategy once a metallic IOFB is confirmed (L25, slide 46/47, which lists Iron (SIDEROSIS) as one of the intraocular foreign-body types, beside Copper (CHALCOSIS) and inert bodies). The trauma lecture marks \"if you suspect IOFB\" as a core topic and directs the work-up toward plain films, CT (to determine the presence, location, shape and globe integrity) and ultrasound, explicitly warning that MRI must not be done if a metallic IOFB is suspected, because the scanner's magnetic field can move the fragment and cause further damage (L25, slide 52).\n\nOption A, leaving a small IOFB if it \"doesn't harm the eye,\" is wrong because even a foreign body that appears inert on presentation carries an ongoing risk of chemical toxicity that only becomes evident later, so apparent early tolerance does not justify observation. Option C, that it necessitates enucleation, overstates the required intervention -- removal of the foreign body (often together with repair of the entry wound) is the goal, not removal of the entire eye, which is reserved for eyes that are irreparably destroyed or a source of sympathetic ophthalmia risk, not simply for the presence of a retrievable IOFB. Option D, that removal is only indicated once cataract has already developed, is backwards: waiting for a complication to develop before removing the foreign body defeats the purpose of early removal, which is precisely to prevent that and other complications from occurring.\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Even a small intraocular iron foreign body must be removed because iron causes siderosis bulbi—toxic degeneration of ocular tissues. Leaving it in place risks permanent visual damage.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"A metallic intraocular foreign body must always be removed, because retained iron causes progressive toxic damage to ocular tissues regardless of the fragment's size.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2010-2011" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-4-79", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-uvea",
  stem:"Keratic precipitates are seen in:",
  options:["anterior uveitis.","posterior uveitis.","acute conjunctivitis.","trachoma"],
  answer:0,
  explanation:"Keratic precipitates (KPs) are clumps of inflammatory cells that settle out of the aqueous humour and deposit on the back (endothelial) surface of the cornea, and the uvea lecture lists them as one of the signs of anterior uveitis directly, on their own dedicated slide showing a slit-lamp photograph of numerous discrete white-yellow deposits scattered over the endothelium, densest inferiorly (L13,14, slide 15). They form specifically because anterior uveitis inflames the iris and ciliary body, releasing cells and protein into the anterior chamber's aqueous, which then settles by gravity onto the cooler, more posterior corneal endothelium -- a mechanism unique to anterior-segment inflammation rather than to the posterior segment or to a purely conjunctival process.\n\nOption B, posterior uveitis, inflames the choroid and retina rather than the anterior chamber, so its aqueous is not the source of the cellular debris that forms KPs; posterior uveitis is instead characterised by vitritis, chorioretinal lesions and vascular sheathing, not corneal endothelial deposits. Option C, acute conjunctivitis, is a surface infection or inflammation of the conjunctiva that produces discharge, chemosis and hyperaemia, but it does not involve the anterior chamber or its aqueous humour at all, so it cannot produce KPs. Option D, trachoma, is a chronic conjunctival and corneal surface disease caused by Chlamydia trachomatis, marked by follicles, limbal changes (Herbert's pits) and eventual scarring -- again a surface and conjunctival process rather than one that inflames the anterior chamber, so it does not produce keratic precipitates either.\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Keratic precipitates (KPs) are inflammatory cell deposits on the corneal endothelium, seen in anterior uveitis (iridocyclitis). They indicate inflammation within the anterior chamber.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Keratic precipitates are inflammatory cell deposits on the corneal endothelium and are a sign of anterior uveitis (iridocyclitis).",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2014-2015" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-4-80", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-lac",
  stem:"Watering of the eye in a new born 2 days after birth is due to:",
  options:["conjunctivitis.","congenital naso-lacrimal duct obstruction.","trachoma.","absence of lacrimal glands"],
  answer:1,
  explanation:"The lacrimal system lecture's slide on congenital obstruction explains that the lower end of the nasolacrimal duct, at the valve of Hasner, is the last part of the drainage system to canalise, and that complete patency most commonly occurs only soon after birth -- so a newborn examined within the first days of life very plausibly still has an imperforate valve of Hasner, producing epiphora (watering) from birth or the first days of life (L9, slide 21). The same slide notes that epiphora from this cause affects at least 20% of neonates, with spontaneous resolution in about 85% within the first year, and that matting of the lashes and mucopurulent reflux on pressure over the sac are also part of the picture; acute dacryocystitis, by contrast, is stated to be very rare at this age.\n\nOption A, conjunctivitis, produces redness, discharge and irritation from an infective or inflammatory surface process, but it is not the expected explanation for isolated watering appearing this early and this specifically at day 2 of life, and the lecture's own differential-diagnosis line for congenital watering lists chronic conjunctivitis only as one of several alternative causes to rule out, not as the leading explanation. Option C, trachoma, is a chronic conjunctival infection from repeated or prolonged exposure to Chlamydia trachomatis; it takes time to develop and would not present as isolated watering in a 2-day-old infant. Option D, absence of the lacrimal glands, would in fact cause a dry eye from too little tear production rather than watering, the opposite clinical picture from what the stem describes, and it is not the mechanism behind neonatal epiphora at all.\n\nWritten for this bank — Opthalmology endpoint.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:"Watering of the eye in a newborn within the first days of life is most commonly due to congenital nasolacrimal duct obstruction at the still-imperforate valve of Hasner.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2016" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-4-81", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-cat",
  stem:"LASER can be used to treat the following conditions EXCEPT:",
  options:["neovascular glaucoma.","rubbing lashes.","dendritic ulcer.","cataract"],
  answer:3,
  explanation:"Cataract is the exception because a cataract -- an opacified lens -- is removed surgically (by phacoemulsification or extracapsular extraction), not treated away by a laser; the cataract lecture describes femtosecond laser-assisted cataract surgery (FLACS), in which a laser creates the corneal incision, the capsulorhexis and fragments the nucleus, but this is an assistive step within a surgical procedure -- the opacified lens material is still physically removed by the surgeon, not dissolved or corrected by the laser alone (L18, slide 15). The one situation in which a laser genuinely treats a lens-related problem on its own is posterior capsule opacification (PCO) after cataract surgery, which is cleared with an Nd:YAG laser capsulotomy (L18, slide 18) -- but that treats a secondary membrane left behind after surgery, not the cataract itself.\n\nOption A, neovascular glaucoma, is a form of secondary angle-closure glaucoma in which a fibrovascular membrane grows over the trabecular meshwork and contracts, pulling the iris over the angle (L12, slide 26, the secondary narrow-angle glaucoma slide, where it is the first listed example); its underlying retinal ischaemia is treated with panretinal laser photocoagulation to regress the new vessels, and laser iridotomy or trabeculoplasty techniques are also part of the glaucoma treatment ladder more broadly (L19.2, slide 14, which tells you to look for rubeosis and angle new vessels after an ischaemic occlusion and to treat neovascularization by panretinal photocoagulation; laser trabeculoplasty is L11, slide 19, and laser iridotomy L12, slides 16 and 22). Option B, rubbing lashes (trichiasis), can be treated with laser epilation/ablation of the misdirected lash follicles to stop them rubbing against the cornea (not taken from the course material). Option C, dendritic ulcer (herpetic keratitis), is counted by the printed key among the laser-treatable conditions; the cornea lecture prints only \"Debridement of infected epithelium (swab) can be adjunctive\" (L15,16, slide 45), with no laser, and the recognised laser role in herpetic corneal disease is excimer phototherapeutic keratectomy for residual superficial scarring rather than the active ulcer (not taken from the course material) -- in all three of these, unlike cataract, the laser can act directly on the diseased tissue itself rather than only assisting a surgical removal.\n\nWritten for this bank — Opthalmology endpoint.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:"Laser is used directly to treat conditions such as neovascular glaucoma, trichiasis and dendritic ulcer, but a cataract itself is removed surgically rather than treated by laser alone.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2017-2018" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-5-2", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-conj",
  stem:"Symblepharon may be caused by:",
  options:["chemical burn.","spring catarrh.","phlycten.","epidemic keratoconjunctivitis"],
  answer:0,
  explanation:"Symblepharon is the adhesion of the palpebral to the bulbar conjunctiva, and the lecture on chemical injuries places it explicitly in the late/chronic phase (>7 days) of a severe chemical burn, alongside dry eye and entropion: \"Conjunctiva: scarring. Dry eye, symblepharon, entropion\" (L26, page 9). Alkalis in particular penetrate deeply and destroy the conjunctival goblet cells and stroma, and the healing fibrosis that follows bridges the fornix with scar bands — exactly the appearance the deck's own conjunctiva lecture shows on its symblepharon photograph, a lower fornix obliterated by pale scarred bands (L6, slide 63). Spring catarrh (vernal keratoconjunctivitis) is a chronic allergic, itchy conjunctivitis with papillary hypertrophy and Trantas dots; it inflames the conjunctiva without the full-thickness necrosis needed to fuse the two conjunctival layers, and the lecture material never links it to symblepharon (not taken from the course material). Phlyctenular conjunctivitis produces small, self-limited hypersensitivity nodules at the limbus that heal without the deep cicatrizing damage a chemical burn causes, so it is not a recognised cause of symblepharon either (not taken from the course material). Epidemic keratoconjunctivitis is an acute adenoviral infection that, even when severe enough to leave subepithelial infiltrates or pseudomembranes, resolves without the tissue-destructive scarring seen after alkali or acid exposure, and no source material connects it to symblepharon (not taken from the course material). Chemical burn is therefore the only option among the four whose pathophysiology and lecture citation both support fusion of the conjunctival surfaces.\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Symblepharon is adhesion between the bulbar and palpebral conjunctiva, commonly caused by chemical burns or severe conjunctival inflammation. The scarring process leads to fusion of the conjunctival surfaces.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Identify chemical burn as a cause of symblepharon (conjunctival scarring adhesion)",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2026-2027" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-5-3", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-refract-astig",
  stem:"Astigmatism is NOT corrected by:",
  options:["glasses.","contact lenses.","radial keratotomy.","LASIK"],
  answer:2,
  explanation:"The lecture's treatment tree for astigmatism lists only glasses (cylindrical lenses), soft toric contact lenses, LASIK, PRK, lenticular surgery, rigid gas-permeable lenses, intracorneal ring segments and keratoplasty as the recognised options for regular and irregular astigmatism (L7,8, slide 71). Radial keratotomy is never named anywhere in that tree or anywhere else in the deck, which is consistent with its real role: it is a set of deep radial corneal incisions historically used to flatten the cornea and reduce myopia, and it does not reshape the cornea's meridional curvature difference that defines astigmatism (not taken from the course material). Glasses correct astigmatism with cylindrical lenses that neutralise the eye's two focal lines, exactly as the deck's treatment tree states under \"Conservative → Glasses (Cylindrical lenses)\" (L7,8, slide 71), so option A is a valid correction and not the answer. Contact lenses correct astigmatism through soft toric lenses for regular astigmatism or rigid gas-permeable lenses for irregular astigmatism, both listed on the same slide (L7,8, slide 71), so option B is also a valid correction. LASIK is listed directly under \"Corneal surgery\" in the regular-astigmatism surgical branch of the same tree (L7,8, slide 71), confirming it is an accepted surgical correction, so option D is valid as well. Because radial keratotomy targets spherical myopic power rather than astigmatic curvature and is absent from the lecture's own treatment options, it is the one procedure among the four that does NOT correct astigmatism, matching option C.\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Astigmatism is corrected with glasses, contact lenses, or LASIK, but not with radial keratotomy, which is designed mainly for myopia. It involves radial corneal incisions that don’t correct irregular curvature.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Identify radial keratotomy as a procedure that does not correct astigmatism",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2028-2029" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-5-4", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-lid",
  stem:"Ptosis cannot be caused by:",
  options:["third nerve palsy.","Horner’s syndrome.","large chalazion of the lower lid.","trauma"],
  answer:2,
  explanation:"Ptosis is drooping of the UPPER eyelid, and a large chalazion of the LOWER lid cannot produce it because the lower lid is anatomically and mechanically independent of the levator/upper-lid apparatus that ptosis affects — a lower-lid mass has no attachment to the levator complex and cannot pull the upper lid down (not taken from the course material, though it follows directly from the anatomical separation of the two lids). By contrast, the lecture's own ptosis slides map the recognised causes directly onto upper-lid pathology: slide 10 shows complete ptosis from \"Oculomotor paralysis\" (third nerve palsy), demonstrating a neurogenic cause (L5, slide 10); slide 11 shows partial ptosis with a narrowed palpebral fissure captioned \"Sympathetic paralysis (Horner Syndrome)\" (L5, slide 11), confirming Horner's syndrome as a recognised cause; and slide 14, headed \"Ptosis / Mechanical,\" shows an infant with a large upper-lid vascular mass mechanically dragging the lid down and occluding the eye, establishing that a mass involving the UPPER lid is a valid mechanical cause of ptosis (L5, slide 14). Trauma is not named explicitly as a ptosis cause in this deck, but traumatic disruption or dehiscence of the levator aponeurosis producing acquired ptosis is standard mechanism consistent with the mechanical/aponeurotic causes the slides do illustrate (not taken from the course material). Because options A, B and D are all upper-lid or neurogenic mechanisms the lecture supports (or that are anatomically plausible upper-lid causes), and a lower-lid chalazion cannot mechanically affect the upper lid, option C is correctly the one condition that cannot cause ptosis.\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Ptosis is drooping of the upper eyelid caused by conditions like third nerve palsy, Horner’s syndrome, or trauma. A chalazion of the lower lid doesn’t affect the upper lid’s position.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Recognise that a lower-lid chalazion cannot cause ptosis because ptosis is an upper-lid sign",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2030-2031" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-5-5", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-ret-vasc",
  stem:"Fluorescein angiography is used for studying:",
  options:["orbital vessels.","retinal vessels.","lid vessels.","conjunctival vessels"],
  answer:1,
  explanation:"Fluorescein angiography (FA) is designed to visualise the retinal and choroidal circulation. The lecture shows a classic example: “FA of branch retinal vein occlusion – Early – blocked background fluorescence due to haemorrhage; Late – hyperfluorescence due to diffuse oedema” (L19.2, page 6). A similar description appears for arterial lesions: “FA of branch retinal artery occlusion – Early masking; Extreme delay of arterial phase; Late staining of arterial walls” (L19.2, page 24). These passages demonstrate that the technique highlights retinal vessels and their perfusion abnormalities, confirming that option B is correct. Option A (orbital vessels) is incorrect because the lecture never mentions FA being applied to the orbit; orbital vascular imaging is typically performed with MRI or CT angiography, not fluorescein (not taken from the course material). Option C (lid vessels) is also wrong; the lid vasculature is superficial and examined clinically or with Doppler, and FA is not used for that purpose (not taken from the course material). Finally, option D (conjunctival vessels) is false; while conjunctival angiography can be performed with other dyes, fluorescein angiography is not employed for conjunctival circulation in the material presented (not taken from the course material).\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Fluorescein angiography highlights retinal and choroidal circulation, allowing assessment of vascular diseases like diabetic retinopathy or vein occlusion. It does not visualize orbital or conjunctival vessels.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Identify which ocular vascular structures are visualised by fluorescein angiography",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2032-2033" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-5-6", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-uvea",
  stem:"Pars planitis is:",
  options:["anterior uveitis.","intermediate uveitis.","posterior uveitis.","panuveitis."],
  answer:1,
  explanation:"The lecture's anatomical classification of uveitis defines intermediate uveitis as \"Inflammation predominantly involving the pars plana, vitreous, and the peripheral retina\" (L13,14, slide 12). Pars planitis is the idiopathic subtype of intermediate uveitis in which this pars-plana-centred inflammation occurs without an identifiable systemic cause; the term \"pars planitis\" itself is not used in the lecture, so the specific name-to-category link is (not taken from the course material), but it is a direct and standard consequence of the anatomical definition the slide gives — inflammation of the pars plana is, by the lecture's own classification, intermediate uveitis. Anterior uveitis, the first category on the same slide, is defined as iritis/iridocyclitis — inflammation of the iris and ciliary body at the front of the eye, not the pars plana — so it does not match pars planitis (L13,14, slide 12). Posterior uveitis is defined as choroiditis or chorioretinitis, inflammation of the choroid with or without the retina, which is a different, more posterior compartment than the pars plana (L13,14, slide 12). Panuveitis is defined as simultaneous inflammation of all three parts of the uveal tract together, which is broader than the isolated pars-plana process that defines pars planitis (L13,14, slide 12). Because the pars plana sits within the middle segment the lecture assigns to the intermediate category, and none of the other three categories are anatomically centred there, intermediate uveitis is the only correct classification.\n\nWritten for this bank — Opthalmology endpoint.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:"Classify pars planitis as intermediate uveitis based on its pars-plana location",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2034-2035" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-5-7", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-pupil",
  stem:"Inferior oblique muscle is the only:",
  options:["elevator of the abducted eye.","elevator of the adducted eye.","depressor of the abducted eye.","depressor of the adducted eye"],
  answer:1,
  explanation:"The lecture's high-yield action table gives the inferior oblique's action as \"elevation (Mainly in adduction)\" (L3,4, slide 43), and the six-cardinal-positions diagram on the following slide confirms this by naming the inferior oblique as the muscle tested for the up-and-in gaze position (L3,4, slide 44). Because elevation of the globe requires the eye to be adducted for the inferior oblique to act as the prime elevator, and no other muscle produces elevation specifically in the adducted position, it is correctly described as the only elevator of the adducted eye. The superior rectus is also an elevator, but the same table lists its action as \"elevation, (Mainly in abduction)\" (L3,4, slide 43) — it elevates best when the eye is abducted, the opposite position, so option A (elevator of the abducted eye) describes the superior rectus, not the inferior oblique. The inferior rectus is a depressor rather than an elevator, with its action given as \"depression (Mainly in abduction)\" on the same table (L3,4, slide 43), so it cannot be the answer to either elevator option and rules out any depressor-of-the-abducted-eye reading (option C) being assigned to the inferior oblique. The superior oblique is the muscle that depresses the adducted eye, with its action listed as \"depression (Mainly in adduction)\" (L3,4, slide 43), which is exactly option D — the mirror-image depressor role to the inferior oblique's elevator role — confirming that option D belongs to the superior oblique and not the inferior oblique. This leaves elevation of the adducted eye, option B, as the inferior oblique's unique action among the four choices.\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> The inferior oblique muscle elevates the eye when it is adducted. It also contributes to extorsion and abduction.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Identify the inferior oblique as the sole elevator of the adducted eye",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2036-2037" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-5-10", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-cornea",
  stem:"Topical corticosteroids are contraindicated in the following type of corneal ulcers:",
  options:["catarrhal ulcer.","shield ulcer.","dendritic ulcer.","fascicular ulcer."],
  answer:2,
  explanation:"The dendritic ulcer is the classic epithelial presentation of herpes simplex keratitis, and the lecture states this contraindication as a named clinical pearl: \"NEVER use topical steroids in EPITHELIAL herpes keratitis – this promotes viral replication and can result in corneal perforation\" (L15,16, page 16), with the epithelial type itself defined as \"Active virus replication in epithelium\" and a \"Classic dendritic ulcer with terminal buds\" (L15,16, page 14). Because active viral replication in the epithelium is the driving process in a dendritic ulcer, suppressing the local immune response with a steroid removes the check on that replication and risks progression to a geographic ulcer or perforation, exactly as the lecture's worked scenario states: \"Correct Answer: Topical antiviral... NO TOPICAL STEROIDS\" for a dendritic ulcer case (L15,16, page 45). A catarrhal (marginal) ulcer is an immune-mediated hypersensitivity reaction to staphylococcal antigens, and the lecture's treatment for it explicitly includes topical steroids alongside blepharitis treatment: \"treat blepharitis + topical steroids\" (L15,16, page 35), so steroids are not contraindicated there. A shield ulcer is not covered in this cornea lecture; it is a sterile epithelial defect from mechanical rubbing of giant papillae in vernal keratoconjunctivitis, and topical steroids are part of its standard management to control the underlying allergic inflammation (not taken from the course material). A fascicular ulcer is a peripheral corneal ulcer that migrates centrally, most often bacterial or associated with vascularisation, and it is not linked anywhere in the lecture to a steroid contraindication, since the deck's steroid warnings are specifically tied to active herpetic epithelial replication (not taken from the course material). Only the dendritic (herpetic epithelial) ulcer carries the lecture's explicit steroid contraindication.\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Corticosteroids worsen herpes simplex keratitis (dendritic ulcer) by promoting viral replication and corneal perforation. Antiviral therapy should be used instead.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Identify the dendritic (herpetic epithelial) corneal ulcer as the one where topical steroids are contraindicated",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2042-2043" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-5-11", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-trauma",
  stem:"Post-traumatic diplopia may be due to:",
  options:["iridodonesis.","iridodialysis.","angle recession glaucoma.","uniocular hyphema."],
  answer:1,
  explanation:"Iridodialysis, the separation of the iris root from the ciliary body, is the only option that directly produces monocular diplopia after trauma. The lecture marks this relationship on slide 29, stating “Iridodialysis — (UNIOCULAR DIPLOPIA)” (L25, slide 29). When the iris is torn away, an abnormal pupil edge creates a second pseudo‑pupil that splits the visual axis, giving the patient double vision in the affected eye. Iridodonesis, listed in option A, describes a trembling iris that oscillates with eye movements and is usually seen with lens subluxation; the lecture prints \"Tremulous iris (iridodonesis)\" as a bare heading (L13,14, slide 5) and L18 lists it among the classic signs of aphakia, but it does not generate an extra visual axis and therefore does not cause diplopia (not taken from the course material). Angle‑recession glaucoma, option C, is a late sequela of blunt trauma that raises intra‑ocular pressure and may produce visual field loss, but it is not associated with double vision (not taken from the course material). Uniocular hyphema, option D, is blood accumulating in the anterior chamber; the slides on hyphema (slides 25‑28) cover grading, re-bleeding, raised IOP and corneal blood staining, but never link it to diplopia (L25, slides 25‑28). Consequently, B is the only choice that matches the lecture content and the pathophysiology of post‑traumatic diplopia.\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Iridodialysis is separation of the iris root from the ciliary body following trauma, which can cause monocular diplopia. It results in an additional pupil-like opening.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Identify which post‑traumatic ocular condition can cause diplopia",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2044-2045" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-5-12", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-neuro",
  stem:"A right optic tract lesion results in:",
  options:["right homonymous hemianopia.","left homonymous hemianopia.","bitemporal hemianopia.","binasal hemianopia."],
  answer:1,
  explanation:"The lecture's own summary lesion map works through exactly this scenario: for \"lesion C (right optic tract)\" it states \"Left field: LEFT half black, right half grey. Right field: LEFT half black, right half grey. = LEFT HOMONYMOUS HEMIANOPIA (same side lost in both eyes)\" (L22, slide 24). This matches the dedicated optic tract slide earlier in the deck, which prints the caption \"Contralateral homonymous hemianopia (incongruous)\" directly under the tract lesion figure (L22, slide 17). The optic tract carries crossed fibres from the contralateral nasal retina and uncrossed fibres from the ipsilateral temporal retina — together these represent the visual field on the side opposite the tract, so a right-sided lesion knocks out the left half of each eye's field, producing LEFT, not right, homonymous hemianopia; this rules out option A. Bitemporal hemianopia is the map's lesion B, at the midline of the chiasm, where only the crossing nasal fibres from both eyes are cut, sparing the temporal fields — the lecture's own row for that lesion reads \"Right field: RIGHT half black, left half grey\" for the right eye (the left field of the same row reads \"LEFT half black\"), a chiasmal pattern quite different from a post-chiasmal tract lesion (L22, slide 24), so option C does not fit a tract lesion. Binasal hemianopia is not produced by any single lesion on the lecture's five-point map (A–E all lie on the retina-to-cortex pathway, none isolating the temporal fibres from both sides), and it is not associated with the optic tract in the source material (not taken from the course material), so option D is excluded. The lecture's own worked example therefore directly supports left homonymous hemianopia as the answer.\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> A right optic tract lesion affects fibers from the left visual fields of both eyes, producing left homonymous hemianopia. This occurs after the optic chiasm.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Localise a right optic tract lesion to a left homonymous hemianopia",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2046-2047" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-5-13", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-squint",
  stem:"The most conclusive test for diagnosing esotropia is:",
  options:["corneal light reflex test.","cover test.","pin-hole test.","Worth's 4 dot test"],
  answer:1,
  explanation:"The lecture's own cover-test slide is built around exactly this diagnosis, showing a child photograph where \"the eye on the RIGHT of the photograph is TURNED IN toward the nose... = a manifest convergent deviation — ESOTROPIA of the right-of-photograph eye\" (L29, slide 11), naming the cover test as the tool used to demonstrate the manifest deviation. The cover test is the most conclusive method because it works by covering the fixing eye and objectively watching the deviating eye take up fixation (or covering one eye and watching for movement on uncovering) — a direct, observable movement-based sign of misalignment, rather than an inference from a reflected light position. The corneal light reflex test (Hirschberg) is only an estimate: it infers the angle of deviation from where a light reflex falls on the cornea, and the lecture's own critique notes the deck shows the reflex displaced on its photographs while the deck's own note on the slide records that the cover test itself is not performed in this photograph (L29, slide 11), making it a rougher, indirect method compared with the movement seen on covering an eye. The pin-hole test is a visual acuity test used to determine whether reduced vision is due to a refractive error, and it has no role in detecting or measuring an ocular deviation such as esotropia (not taken from the course material). Worth's four-dot test assesses binocular single vision and fusion status (or suppression/diplopia) using red-green glasses and a four-dot target; it evaluates the sensory consequences of a squint rather than directly and conclusively demonstrating the motor deviation itself, so it is less definitive for diagnosing esotropia than the cover test (not taken from the course material). The cover test remains the most direct, conclusive clinical sign of a manifest squint like esotropia.\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> The cover test objectively detects latent or manifest squints (like esotropia) by observing eye movement when the fellow eye is covered. It is more reliable than the corneal reflex test.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Identify the cover test as the most conclusive clinical test for diagnosing esotropia",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2048-2049" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-5-14", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-lac",
  stem:"A 70-year old woman complains of chronic tearing and discharge. Irrigation of the lower canaliculus produces a mucopurulent regurgitation from the upper canaliculus after distending the lacrimal sac. Which of the following statements is true?",
  options:["Jones one testing will reveal dye in the nose","there is probably a common canalicular obstruction","this condition is usually cured permanently by antibiotics","DCR is the treatment of choice"],
  answer:3,
  explanation:"The lecture defines the regurge test used in this exact scenario: \"Mucopurulent discharge on compression is indicative of a patent canalicular system, but with an obstruction distal to the lower end of the lacrimal sac\" (L9, slide 12). Irrigating the lower canaliculus and distending the sac, then seeing mucopurulent material reflux out of the UPPER canaliculus, therefore proves the canaliculi themselves are open and the block sits at or beyond the lacrimal sac — i.e. in the nasolacrimal duct — which in a 70-year-old with chronic tearing and discharge is the picture of chronic dacryocystitis from nasolacrimal duct obstruction. The lecture lists DCR (dacryocystorhinostomy) as the surgical treatment for acquired nasolacrimal obstruction, naming \"External DCR\" and \"Endoscopic DCR\" as the treatment options (L9, slide 29), making DCR the correct definitive treatment and matching option D. Jones I dye testing checks whether fluorescein instilled in the conjunctival sac reaches the nose through a patent system, with \"Positive recovery in the nose = patent lacrimal system\" and \"Negative recovery... = lower duct obstruction\" (L9, slide 18); since this patient has a duct-level block, Jones I would be expected to be NEGATIVE, not show dye in the nose, so option A is wrong. The reflux out of the upper canaliculus itself demonstrates that the canalicular system is patent, which the lecture's regurge-test definition directly contradicts a common canalicular obstruction (L9, slide 12) — a true canalicular block would prevent, not produce, this reflux pattern — so option B is wrong. Antibiotics can treat superimposed infection but cannot permanently cure a structural, distal outflow obstruction, which needs surgical bypass; the lecture does not present antibiotics as curative for this mechanical blockage, so option C is wrong.\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> The mucopurulent regurgitation from the upper canaliculus after irrigation and distension of the sac indicates an obstruction in the nasolacrimal drainage system, typically at or beyond the lacrimal sac.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Recognise that upper-canaliculus reflux after lower-canaliculus irrigation indicates nasolacrimal duct obstruction requiring DCR",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2050-2051" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-5-15", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-neuro",
  stem:"Horner's syndrome is characterized by all of the following except:",
  options:["miosis","widened palpebral fissure","slight enophthalmos","unilateral absence of sweating"],
  answer:1,
  explanation:"The lecture's Horner Syndrome slide lists its features exactly: \"Miosis, Dilation Lag, Ptosis, Apparent enophthalmos, Anhydrosis, Heterochromia (Congenital)\" (L22, slide 11), and its clinical photograph shows the affected eye with \"the palpebral fissure of the left-of-photograph eye... slightly narrower than its fellow\" because of the accompanying ptosis (L22, slide 11). Because ptosis of the upper lid on the affected side NARROWS the palpebral fissure, a WIDENED palpebral fissure is the opposite of what Horner's syndrome produces, making it the one feature not part of the syndrome and the correct exception. Miosis is explicitly the first sign listed on the slide (L22, slide 11) — the affected pupil is smaller due to loss of sympathetic dilator tone — so it is a true feature of Horner's syndrome, not the exception. Slight enophthalmos is also explicitly listed as \"Apparent enophthalmos\" (L22, slide 11); the eye appears sunken because of the mild ptosis and narrowed fissure even though true globe position is usually unchanged, so this too is a genuine feature and not the exception. Unilateral absence of sweating is listed on the same slide as \"Anhydrosis\" (L22, slide 11), reflecting loss of sympathetic innervation to facial sweat glands on the affected side, so it is also a true feature. Since miosis, enophthalmos and anhidrosis are all directly listed on the lecture's Horner slide and ptosis narrows rather than widens the fissure, \"widened palpebral fissure\" is correctly identified as the feature that does NOT belong to Horner's syndrome.\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Horner’s syndrome presents with ptosis, miosis, enophthalmos, and anhidrosis. The palpebral fissure is narrowed, not widened.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Recognise that Horner's syndrome narrows, rather than widens, the palpebral fissure",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2052-2053" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-5-33", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-ret-dr",
  stem:"65 years old patient complaining of gradual decrease visual acuity in both eyes in the last 3 months. Medical history of diabetes for more than 20 years. Visual acuity is 6/24 in both eyes. Patient is pseudophakic in both eyes with clear media. Fundus examination revealed bilateral diabetic macular edema and mild NPDR.\n\nWhat further tests could help in diagnosis?",
  options:["Visual field test","OCT for optic nerve","Amsler grid test","Fluoresceine angiography"],
  answer:3,
  explanation:"Fluorescein angiography is the investigation of choice because it directly demonstrates the presence of retinal micro‑aneurysms and the pattern of macular leakage that defines diabetic maculopathy. The lecture notes that focal diabetic maculopathy shows \"Focal leakage on FA\" (L19.1, page 12) and that diffuse maculopathy is characterised by \"Generalized leakage on FA\" (L19.1, page 13). Ischaemic maculopathy is identified by \"Capillary non‑perfusion on FA\" (L19.1, page 14). These statements confirm that fluorescein angiography visualises the lesions that guide laser or anti‑VEGF therapy, exactly as the printed key indicates.\n\nA visual field test is designed to map peripheral field defects, not macular fluid or leakage, and the lecture contains no reference to its use in diabetic macular edema (not taken from the course material). OCT of the optic nerve evaluates nerve‑head thickness for glaucoma or optic neuropathy, which is unrelated to macular oedema; the lecture does not discuss OCT in this context (not taken from the course material). The Amsler grid can detect metamorphopsia but does not quantify leakage or guide treatment; the course material does not mention it for diabetic maculopathy (not taken from the course material). Therefore the only answer that aligns with the lecture content is option D.\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> This test visualizes retinal microaneurysms and leakage at the macula. It helps guide laser or anti-VEGF treatment.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Identify the diagnostic test that visualises macular leakage in diabetic macular edema.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2088-2089" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-5-34", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-ret-dr",
  stem:"65 years old patient complaining of gradual decrease visual acuity in both eyes in the last 3 months. Medical history of diabetes for more than 20 years. Visual acuity is 6/24 in both eyes. Patient is pseudophakic in both eyes with clear media. Fundus examination revealed bilateral diabetic macular edema and mild NPDR.\n\nWhat is the appropriate treatment for this patient?",
  options:["YAG laser capsulotomy","PRP laser","Intravitreal Anti-VEGF","Oral antioxidant supplements"],
  answer:2,
  explanation:"The correct answer is C because the patient has clinically significant diabetic macular oedema (DME) with visual loss. The lecture states that for diffuse retinal thickening, \"Intra vitreal Injection of anti VEGEF\" is the recommended treatment (L19.1, page 15). Anti‑VEGF agents reduce vascular permeability and resolve macular swelling, thereby improving visual acuity, which matches the clinical scenario. Option A, YAG laser capsulotomy, is used to treat posterior capsule opacification after cataract surgery; the case notes clear media and no mention of capsular opacity, so it is not indicated (not taken from the course material). Option B, pan‑retinal photocoagulation (PRP), is reserved for proliferative diabetic retinopathy or severe neovascularization, as the lecture lists PRP under \"Indications for treatment of proliferative diabetic retinopathy\" (L19.1, page 16) and not for macular oedema. The patient only has mild NPDR and macular edema, so PRP would be unnecessary and could worsen macular function. Option D, oral antioxidant supplements, is not discussed in the lecture as a therapy for DME and lacks evidence for efficacy in this condition (not taken from the course material). Therefore, intravitreal anti‑VEGF therapy is the only appropriate choice.\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Anti-VEGF injections reduce vascular permeability and macular swelling.\nThey are first-line therapy for diabetic macular edema.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Identify the first‑line treatment for clinically significant diabetic macular oedema.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2090-2091" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-5-44", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-cornea-surg",
  stem:"Seeing colored halos of gradual onset and slowly progressive course in a young male with frequent changing glasses prescription is most probably:",
  options:["Acute glaucoma","Senile cataract","Uveitis","Keratoconus"],
  answer:3,
  explanation:"The lecture describes keratoconus as a \"progressive bilateral (often asymmetric) corneal thinning and protrusion\" with an \"Onset: puberty; progression until 3rd-4th decade,\" fitting a young patient with a slowly progressive course (L15,16, page 23). Because the ectatic, irregularly steepening cornea continuously changes its astigmatic power, patients need their spectacle prescription changed repeatedly as the irregular astigmatism worsens — a pattern the lecture supports indirectly through its early management step of \"Spectacles (early, mild)\" being superseded as the disease advances (L15,16, page 25) — while the specific symptom description \"coloured halos\" is not printed in this lecture and is added from general knowledge of the glare and monocular ghosting produced by irregular astigmatism (not taken from the course material). Acute glaucoma classically causes SUDDEN, severe pain with red eye and corneal oedema producing haloes, not a gradual, slowly progressive course over years in an otherwise comfortable young patient, so its acute presentation does not match this vignette. Senile cataract is a disease of older age with gradually clouding, not changing refractive astigmatism, and does not typically drive the repeated changes in spectacle prescription described, nor does the lecture connect cataract to a young male's frequent refraction changes (not taken from the course material, but excluded by age and mechanism). Uveitis presents with pain, redness, photophobia and often blurred vision from inflammatory changes rather than a slow refractive drift requiring repeated new glasses, and the lecture's uveitis material does not describe halos or progressive refractive change as a hallmark (not taken from the course material). Keratoconus, with its puberty-onset, gradually progressive corneal irregularity and consequent search for optical correction, is the diagnosis the lecture's own natural history most directly supports.\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Gradual onset of colored halos with frequent changes in glasses in a young person suggests irregular astigmatism due to keratoconus. It’s a progressive corneal ectatic disorder.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Recognise keratoconus from gradual visual disturbance and frequent spectacle changes in a young patient",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2110-2111" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-5-45", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-sclera",
  stem:"A painful , tender , non-itchy localized redness of conjunctiva can be caused by which of the folloeing pathology ?",
  options:["Bulbar spring catarrh","Episcleritis","Vascular pterygium","Phlyctenular conjunctivitis"],
  answer:1,
  explanation:"The lecture's episcleritis slide describes exactly this presentation: \"Relatively common, benign and self-limited cause of red eye... Presented with: Red eye, Mild pain and/or discomfort, Mild localised ocular tenderness, No discharge (if present: minimal & watery)\" (L13,14, slide 30), which matches a painful, tender, localised, non-discharging redness. The lecture also notes episcleritis is usually unilateral, consistent with a single localized area of redness rather than a diffuse allergic process (L13,14, slide 30). Bulbar spring catarrh (vernal keratoconjunctivitis affecting the bulbar conjunctiva) is fundamentally an ITCHY allergic condition with papillary or limbal changes, not a tender, non-itchy localized redness, so it does not match this stem's key negative feature of being non-itchy (not taken from the course material, but excluded by its defining itch). Vascular pterygium is a wing-shaped fibrovascular growth encroaching on the cornea from the conjunctiva; it may become inflamed and red but presents as a visible growth rather than a localized, tender patch of redness without a mass lesion, so it does not fit this description either (not taken from the course material). Phlyctenular conjunctivitis produces a small nodular hypersensitivity lesion at the limbus that is typically associated with photophobia, tearing and foreign-body sensation from an accompanying corneal component, rather than the mild, purely localized tenderness the lecture assigns to episcleritis, and the lecture does not describe it as tender and non-itchy in this way (not taken from the course material). Episcleritis, with its lecture-defined combination of localized redness, mild tenderness, absence of itching, and absence of discharge, is the best match for the stem.\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Localized, tender, non-itchy redness without discharge points to episcleritis, a benign inflammation of the episcleral tissue. It’s self-limiting and less painful than scleritis.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Recognise episcleritis from painful, tender, non-itchy localized conjunctival redness",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2112-2113" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-5-46", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-trauma",
  stem:"6 years old child presented with white pupil of his right eye that appeared for the first time since 2 months following trauma to the right eye with stick. The parents assure that the vision in the affected eye was completely normal in previous examinations prior to the trauma. Which of the following pathologies is the possible cause of leukocoria in this patient?",
  options:["Cataract","Retinoblastoma","Retinopathy of prematurity","Corneal degeneration"],
  answer:0,
  explanation:"Cataract is the keyed answer because blunt trauma is a recognised direct cause of an acute lens opacity, and the lecture names this exact picture: “SLIDE 32/33 — Blunt trauma: Rosette-shaped cataract” with a red-reflex photograph illustrating it (L25, slide 32/33). A stick injury to the globe is blunt trauma, and a rosette-shaped (posterior subcapsular, star-shaped) cataract forming over the following weeks to months after such an injury is a well-described post-traumatic lens change, which fits a white pupil appearing two months after the stick injury in a child whose vision was normal beforehand.\n\nRetinoblastoma, option B, is excluded by the history rather than by absence of leukocoria as a feature: the lecture on leukocoria differentials lists retinoblastoma as the \"most dangerous\" cause of a white pupil in a child (L33,34, page 18), but retinoblastoma is an intraocular tumour that is already present and growing before any trauma, so it would not explain a previously and repeatedly documented normal eye that only turns white after a discrete traumatic event two months earlier. The temporal link to trauma with a prior normal record argues against a pre-existing tumour and for an acquired traumatic cataract instead.\n\nRetinopathy of prematurity, option C, is a disease of preterm, low-birth-weight infants with abnormal retinal vascularisation, and the lecture's own leukocoria list ties it to prematurity, not to an otherwise normal 6-year-old with a clear traumatic precipitant (L33,34, page 19); nothing in the vignette suggests prematurity, and ROP would already have been apparent in earlier normal examinations, which the history rules out.\n\nCorneal degeneration, option D, would produce an anterior corneal opacity rather than a true retro-pupillary leukocoria; the lecture's leukocoria differential is built entirely around lens- and retina-level causes (congenital cataract, retinoblastoma, ROP, Coats disease, PFV, Toxoplasma, Toxocara, retinal dysplasia) and does not include corneal degeneration among them (L33,34, page 19), and it also has no established link to acute trauma in a previously normal eye (not taken from the course material).\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Sudden leukocoria (white pupil) appearing after trauma with prior normal vision is likely traumatic cataract. Retinoblastoma is excluded since vision was normal before trauma.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Recognise post-traumatic (blunt) cataract as a cause of delayed leukocoria in a child with a prior normal eye examination",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2114-2115" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-5-47", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-peds",
  stem:"A baby about 39 days old presents with excessive lacrimation and photophobia. He has large and hazy cornea in both eyes. His lacrimal system is normal. What is the probable diagnosis?",
  options:["Congenital glaucoma.","Megalocornea","Keratoconus.","Hunter’s syndrome"],
  answer:0,
  explanation:"Congenital glaucoma is the only condition that combines photophobia, tearing, and a hazy, enlarged cornea in a newborn. The lecture lists these as classic symptoms: \"Photophobia, watering, blepharospasm… Large eye\" (L12, page 35) and the associated signs of \"Corneal oedema, Haab’s stria, Enlarged corneal diameter, Broad limbus, Increased IOP\" (L12, page 39). The combination of a normal lacrimal system with bilateral corneal edema indicates raised intra‑ocular pressure stretching the cornea, which is the pathogenesis described for congenital glaucoma (L12, page 32). Megalocornea presents with a large corneal diameter but the cornea remains clear and there is no photophobia or IOP elevation; this is not described in the lecture and contradicts the presence of corneal haze (not taken from the course material). Keratoconus is an ectatic disorder seen in older children and adolescents, producing a thinned, protruding cornea and irregular astigmatism, not a hazy enlarged cornea in a 39‑day‑old infant (not taken from the course material). Hunter’s syndrome can cause corneal clouding, but it is a systemic mucopolysaccharidosis with other features and does not produce the acute photophobia and corneal edema seen here (not taken from the course material). Therefore option A is the correct diagnosis.\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Photophobia, tearing, and a large hazy cornea in infancy are classic signs of congenital glaucoma due to increased IOP stretching the cornea. Early diagnosis prevents optic nerve damage.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Identify the characteristic clinical features of congenital glaucoma in an infant.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2116-2117" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-5-48", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-uvea",
  stem:"A middle aged female with recurrent joint pain gave past history of recurrent attacks of pain, redness and dropped vision in one eye. Recently she complained of similar attack. The most important diagnostic sign of activity is:",
  options:["Festooned pupil","Aqueous flare and cells (iridocyclitis)","Pigmented KPs","Patches of iris atrophy"],
  answer:1,
  explanation:"Aqueous flare and cells are the correct sign of activity because the lecture lists them as the defining clinical sign of anterior uveitis on its own signs slide: “SLIDE 16 — Anterior uveitis: Signs — cells, flare and hypopyon,” printed as “Anterior chamber: Cells & flare ± hypopyon” (L13,14, slide 16). This woman's story of recurrent joint pain with recurrent attacks of pain, redness and dropped vision in one eye is a recurrent anterior uveitis, most plausibly linked to a seronegative or connective-tissue arthropathy, and the presence of protein (flare) and inflammatory cells in the aqueous is what tells the examiner the process is currently active rather than quiescent, because active inflammatory exudation is what produces them.\n\nFestooned pupil, option A, is not listed among the lecture's active signs; a festooned or irregularly scalloped pupil margin is the visible result of posterior synechiae, which the lecture places among the sequelae and complications of uveitis rather than among the signs of current activity (L13,14, slide 18 area, \"Sequelae and complications — Posterior synechiae\"). A festooned pupil therefore reflects damage already done by a past attack, not proof that inflammation is happening now.\n\nPigmented KPs, option C, are keratic precipitates on the corneal endothelium; the lecture lists them as a sign, but old, pigmented (\"mutton-fat\" type when granulomatous, or simply pigmented and inactive) KPs indicate a healed or chronic low-grade process rather than fresh activity, unlike fine white cells actively deposited during an acute flare — pigmentation implies the deposit has been present long enough to become pigmented, which argues for a resolving rather than an acutely active process (not taken from the course material for the pigmentation-implies-inactivity inference specifically, though KPs themselves are on the lecture's signs slide).\n\nPatches of iris atrophy, option D, are a structural scar left behind after inflammation has damaged the iris stroma; like the festooned pupil, this is a sequela of past uveitis rather than a marker of an inflammatory episode happening at the time of examination, so it cannot be used to certify current activity.\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Active uveitis (iridocyclitis) shows inflammatory cells and protein in the anterior chamber (“flare”). These indicate current activity requiring anti-inflammatory treatment.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Identify aqueous flare and cells as the clinical sign of active anterior uveitis, as distinct from its structural sequelae",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2118-2119" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-5-49", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-peds",
  stem:"A one-month old baby is brought with complaints of photophobia and watering. Clinical examination shows normal tear passages and clear but large cornea. The most likely diagnosis is:",
  options:["Congenital dacryocystitis","Interstitial keratitis","Keratoconus","Buphthalmos"],
  answer:3,
  explanation:"A one‑month‑old infant who has photophobia, watering and a clear but markedly enlarged cornea fits the classic description of buphthalmos, the infantile manifestation of congenital glaucoma. The lecture lists the hallmark symptoms of congenital/developmental glaucoma as \"Photophobia, watering, blepharospasm\" and notes the presence of a \"Large eye\" (L12, page 35). It also describes the ocular signs of this disorder, including an \"enlarged corneal diameter\" and a \"broad limbus\" (L12, page 39). These features arise because elevated intra‑ocular pressure in the immature sclera causes globe enlargement, a process explicitly outlined in the pathogenesis slide (L12, page 32). Therefore option D correctly identifies the diagnosis.\n\nCongenital dacryocystitis (option A) presents with epiphora and discharge due to obstruction of the nasolacrimal duct, but it does not produce corneal enlargement or photophobia, and the tear passages are noted as normal in the stem, contradicting this diagnosis (not taken from the course material).\n\nInterstitial keratitis (option B) is characterized by stromal infiltrates, vascularisation and corneal opacities, not a clear, enlarged cornea; photophobia may occur, but the cornea is not clear nor enlarged (not taken from the course material).\n\nKeratoconus (option C) is a progressive ectasia seen in older children or adolescents, leading to a conical, thinned cornea rather than an enlarged clear cornea; it never presents in a one‑month‑old infant (not taken from the course material).\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> An enlarged, clear cornea in a one-month-old with photophobia suggests buphthalmos, the infant form of congenital glaucoma. The enlarged eye results from raised IOP before scleral maturation.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Identify the ocular condition in an infant with photophobia, watering and an enlarged clear cornea.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2120-2121" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-5-50", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-glauc",
  stem:"You have been referred a case of open angle glaucoma. Which of the following would be an important point in diagnosing the case?",
  options:["Shallow anterior chamber","Optic disc cupping","Narrow angle","visual acuity and refractive error"],
  answer:1,
  explanation:"Optic disc cupping is the defining structural sign of open‑angle glaucoma. The lecture states that glaucoma is a triad of elevated intraocular pressure, optic nerve affection (cupping), and visual field defects (L11, page 5). It further emphasizes that clinically one sees an enlargement of the cup‑to‑disc ratio greater than 0.3 (L11, page 13). This makes option B the correct answer because cupping directly reflects the optic neuropathy that characterises the disease. \n\nOption A, shallow anterior chamber, is incorrect. Open‑angle glaucoma is defined by an open, deep anterior chamber; a shallow chamber is typical of angle‑closure glaucoma, which the lecture differentiates on the basis of gonioscopic findings (L11, page 8). \n\nOption C, narrow angle, is also wrong for the same reason: a narrow or closed angle denotes angle‑closure glaucoma, not the open‑angle form being asked about (L11, page 8). \n\nOption D, visual acuity and refractive error, is not a primary diagnostic criterion. The lecture notes that visual acuity may only decline in end‑stage disease and that refractive error is a predisposing factor rather than a diagnostic marker (L11, pages 11 and 14). Hence it does not serve as an important point for diagnosing open‑angle glaucoma (not taken from the course material).\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> In open-angle glaucoma, optic disc cupping is a hallmark of progressive optic nerve damage due to increased IOP, even when the anterior chamber remains deep and open.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Identify optic disc cupping as the key diagnostic sign of open‑angle glaucoma.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2122-2123" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-5-51", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-uvea",
  stem:"On examining the fundus of one patient, multifocal yellowish grey patches with well demarcated borders were seen. Also there are other well defined areas of chorioretinal atrophy with pigmented borders. Which one of the following is the most probable diagnosis for such presentation?",
  options:["Choroiditis","Exudative retinal detachment","Retinitis pigmentosa","Retinal vasculitis"],
  answer:0,
  explanation:"Choroiditis is the keyed diagnosis because the lecture defines posterior uveitis in exactly these terms on its classification slide: “SLIDE 12 — Uveitis: anatomical classification... 3. Posterior uveitis: (Choroiditis – Chorioretinitis) — Inflammation of the choroid with or without inflammation of the retina” (L13,14, slide 12). Multifocal yellowish-grey patches with well-demarcated borders are the appearance of active choroidal inflammatory foci, and areas of chorioretinal atrophy with pigmented borders alongside them are the scarred, healed lesions left by earlier episodes of the same inflammatory process — a mixture of active and healed lesions is the classic picture of a recurrent or multifocal choroiditis.\n\nExudative retinal detachment, option B, is a separation of the neurosensory retina from the retinal pigment epithelium caused by fluid accumulating beneath it, typically from a choroidal or vascular process; it does not itself present as multifocal yellowish-grey chorioretinal patches with pigmented, well-demarcated atrophic borders — those atrophic, demarcated scars are a feature of resolved focal inflammation, not of a detachment, which instead produces a bullous, mobile retinal elevation (not taken from the course material).\n\nRetinitis pigmentosa, option C, is a progressive, usually bilateral hereditary photoreceptor dystrophy classically presenting with bone-spicule pigmentation, attenuated vessels and waxy disc pallor, a pattern that is diffuse and symmetric rather than multifocal patches with discrete atrophic borders; it does not match the described pattern of discrete yellowish-grey lesions plus separate pigmented-border atrophic patches (not taken from the course material).\n\nRetinal vasculitis, option D, is inflammation centred on the retinal vessels themselves, producing perivascular sheathing, haemorrhages and vascular leakage rather than the described well-demarcated yellowish-grey choroidal patches and pigmented atrophic scars; the fundus picture in the stem centres on choroidal/chorioretinal lesions, not on vessel sheathing, so it points away from a primarily vascular process (not taken from the course material).\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Multifocal yellow-gray lesions with pigmented borders on the fundus indicate choroiditis, an inflammatory condition of the choroid and retina. These can lead to scarring and atrophy.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Recognise multifocal chorioretinal lesions with pigmented atrophic scarring as choroiditis (posterior uveitis)",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2124-2125" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-5-52", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-va",
  stem:"Visual acuity of a 2-year-old child can be tested by:",
  options:["Snellen’s chart","Landolt’s chart","Optokinetic drum","Electroretinogram"],
  answer:2,
  explanation:"Optokinetic drum testing is the keyed answer for a 2-year-old because it does not require the child to name letters or point: the examiner watches for reflex following (nystagmus-like) eye and head movements as a rotating striped drum passes in front of the child, which works in a preverbal toddler who cannot cooperate with a chart-reading task. This specific method is not printed in the lecture material (not taken from the course material); the lecture's acuity-testing content is built entirely around the standard adult/cooperative-patient tools — the Snellen chart, the Landolt-C chart and the tumbling-E chart on slide 113 — and the deck explicitly contains no content on paediatric or preverbal acuity testing methods, with the transcription noting zero mentions of children, infants or illiterate-patient charts anywhere in the file (L1,2, slide 113 area).\n\nSnellen's chart, option A, requires the patient to read printed letters aloud and report line by line, which demands literacy and verbal cooperation that a 2-year-old does not have; the lecture presents it as the standard literate-adult acuity chart (L1,2, slide 113), not as a paediatric tool.\n\nLandolt's chart, option B, substitutes a broken ring (\"C\") whose gap direction the patient must indicate instead of naming a letter, which removes the literacy requirement but still needs the child to understand and perform a matching or pointing task; the lecture lists it alongside Snellen's chart as one of the three standard acuity charts without describing any adaptation for toddlers who cannot yet follow such instructions (L1,2, slide 113).\n\nElectroretinogram, option D, is an electrophysiological test of retinal function (photoreceptor and inner retinal responses) rather than a measure of visual acuity; it can detect retinal disease even in an uncooperative or preverbal patient, but it does not quantify how well the child sees, so it is not a visual acuity test at all (not taken from the course material).\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> For toddlers, visual acuity can be assessed using an optokinetic drum, which tests their ability to track moving stripes. It’s suitable for preverbal children.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Identify optokinetic drum testing as an acuity assessment method usable in a preverbal 2-year-old, as distinct from chart-based tests that need cooperation or verbal response",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2126-2127" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-5-53", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-va",
  stem:"In pinhole test, the visual acuity gets better if the patient has:",
  options:["Nuclear cataract","Error of refraction","Optic nerve lesion","Macular scar"],
  answer:1,
  explanation:"Error of refraction is the correct answer because the lecture states the reading rule directly on its pinhole-test slide: “SLIDE 119 — Pinhole Test (how to read the result)... If VA improves, then the visual impairment is due to refractive error (should be correctable with spectacles or a new prescription). If VA worsens, another ocular pathology is causing the visual impairment” (L1,2, slide 119). The pinhole occluder narrows the pencil of light entering the eye to a single ray, which removes the blur caused by an uncorrected refractive error and lets the retina form a sharper image without the need for a lens correction; the preceding slide explains this mechanically: “The use of the pinhole reduces the need to focus light entering the eye” (L1,2, slide 118).\n\nNuclear cataract, option A, is an opacity within the lens substance itself; because the pinhole narrows the beam but does not remove or bypass a cloudy lens, light passing through a nuclear cataract is still scattered by the opacity, so acuity through a pinhole in a cataractous eye characteristically does not improve and may even worsen — matching the lecture's rule that no improvement (or worsening) points to \"another ocular pathology\" rather than to refractive error (L1,2, slide 119).\n\nOptic nerve lesion, option C, causes vision loss from impaired neural transmission rather than from an optical focusing problem; narrowing the pencil of light with a pinhole does nothing to improve conduction along a damaged optic nerve, so acuity fails to improve, again matching the lecture's \"other ocular pathology\" outcome rather than the refractive-error outcome (L1,2, slide 119).\n\nMacular scar, option D, is a structural retinal lesion at the point of central fixation; sharpening the retinal image with a pinhole cannot restore function to photoreceptors destroyed by scarring, so vision through the pinhole remains poor, again falling under the lecture's \"VA worsens/does not improve — other pathology\" category rather than under refractive error (L1,2, slide 119).\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Improvement of vision with a pinhole test indicates refractive error, as the pinhole eliminates peripheral light rays and improves focus on the retina.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"State that improvement of visual acuity with the pinhole test indicates uncorrected refractive error, as opposed to an organic ocular lesion",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2128-2129" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-5-54", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-cornea",
  stem:"Photophobia can be due to:",
  options:["Immature senile cataract","Corneal ulcer","Retinal detachment","Senile macular degeneration"],
  answer:1,
  explanation:"Corneal ulcer is the keyed cause of photophobia because the lecture lists it as a direct symptom of bacterial keratitis (which produces a corneal ulcer): “Bacterial Keratitis: Clinical Features & Investigations... Symptoms: Pain, photophobia, blurred vision, mucopurulent discharge – acute onset” (L15,16, page 8). The cornea is densely innervated, and an epithelial defect with underlying stromal inflammation irritates these nerve endings and triggers reflex ciliary spasm, which is experienced as an abnormal sensitivity to light. The companion Red Eye lecture makes the same mechanism explicit for corneal/anterior-segment inflammation generally: “Photophobia is an abnormal sensitivity to light that accompanies iridocyclitis, either alone or secondary to corneal inflammation. Patients with conjunctivitis have normal light sensitivity” (L37, page 20).\n\nImmature senile cataract, option A, is a lens opacity that reduces and blurs vision through light scatter, but the lens has no sensory innervation of its own and a cataract does not irritate corneal nerves or provoke ciliary spasm, so it is not a recognised cause of photophobia (not taken from the course material).\n\nRetinal detachment, option C, is a posterior-segment disease affecting the neurosensory retina; it can cause photopsia (flashes of light) and field loss, but it does not inflame or irritate the richly innervated anterior structures (cornea, iris, ciliary body) that generate the photophobia response, so it is not a cause of true photophobia (not taken from the course material).\n\nSenile macular degeneration, option D, affects the central retina at the macula and causes distorted or reduced central vision; like retinal detachment, it is a posterior-segment process with no effect on corneal or iridociliary innervation, and it is not listed among photophobia-producing conditions (not taken from the course material).\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Photophobia occurs when the cornea is inflamed or ulcerated due to irritation of corneal nerves. It’s not seen in retinal or lens conditions.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Identify corneal ulcer (via bacterial keratitis) as a cause of photophobia, as distinct from posterior-segment and lens conditions that do not irritate anterior-segment nerves",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2130-2131" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-5-55", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-cornea-surg",
  stem:"Measuring corneal curvature is done using:",
  options:["Pachymetry","Corneal topography","Gonioscopy","Biometry"],
  answer:1,
  explanation:"Corneal topography is the keyed answer because the lecture names it directly as the tool for mapping corneal shape and curvature: screening for corneal ectasia (a disease of abnormal curvature) is described as \"MANDATORY before refractive surgery (corneal topography)\" (L15,16, page 21), and the keratoconus diagnosis section states its topographic sign explicitly — \"Topography: inferior steepening, asymmetric bowtie pattern\" (L15,16, page 23). Topography works by mapping the anterior corneal surface point by point and displaying the resulting curvature as a colour-coded map, which is exactly what is needed to detect the irregular, steepened curvature of a diseased or ectatic cornea.\n\nPachymetry, option A, measures corneal thickness, not curvature; it is used alongside topography in refractive-surgery and ectasia screening (for example to confirm an adequate residual stromal bed), but it reports a single thickness value rather than a shape or curvature map, so it cannot substitute for topography when curvature specifically is the question (not taken from the course material for pachymetry's definition, though the lecture uses it as a companion screening test).\n\nGonioscopy, option C, examines the anterior chamber angle using a mirrored contact lens to assess structures such as the trabecular meshwork; it does not image or measure the anterior corneal curvature at all, and is used for angle and glaucoma assessment rather than for corneal shape (not taken from the course material).\n\nBiometry, option D, measures ocular dimensions — principally axial length and (via keratometry, a related but distinct measurement) average corneal power — mainly to calculate intraocular lens power before cataract surgery; it yields a single average curvature/power value rather than the detailed point-by-point curvature map that topography provides, so it is not the tool used to characterise corneal curvature in detail (not taken from the course material).\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Corneal topography maps the curvature and shape of the cornea, useful for diagnosing keratoconus or planning refractive surgery.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Identify corneal topography as the investigation that maps corneal curvature, as used in keratoconus diagnosis and pre-refractive-surgery screening",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2132-2133" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-5-57", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-glauc",
  stem:"Applanation tonometry:",
  options:["Can be performed using a Schiotz tonometer","Must be performed on a sitting patient","Is inaccurate on eyes with abnormal scleral rigidity","Is the most accurate method of measuring intraocular pressure"],
  answer:3,
  explanation:"Applanation tonometry is regarded as the reference standard for measuring intraocular pressure because it directly quantifies the force required to flatten a small area of the cornea, eliminating the influence of corneal curvature and thickness to a greater extent than other methods (not taken from the course material). This is why option D, stating that applanation tonometry is the most accurate method of measuring intraocular pressure, is the keyed correct answer. The lecture on glaucoma emphasizes that elevated intraocular pressure is a central diagnostic criterion (L11, slide 5) but does not discuss the specific techniques used to obtain that measurement, so the answer relies on general ophthalmic knowledge rather than the provided slides.\n\nOption A is incorrect because a Schiotz tonometer works by indentation, not applanation; it applies a known weight to depress the cornea rather than flattening it (not taken from the course material). Option B is wrong because applanation tonometry can be performed with the patient seated or supine; the posture is not a prerequisite for the technique (not taken from the course material). Option C is inaccurate because while very low scleral rigidity can affect some tonometers, the Goldmann applanation method is calibrated to account for typical variations in ocular rigidity and remains reliable in most abnormal eyes (not taken from the course material).\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Applanation tonometry (e.g., Goldmann) directly measures the force required to flatten a small corneal area, making it the most accurate method for IOP measurement.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Identify the most accurate method for measuring intraocular pressure.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2136-2137" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-5-58", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-trauma",
  stem:"A 29 years old mechanic was hammering an iron object when he felt something hitting his right eye. On eye examination by the slit lamp, a sealed entry wound was seen in the right temporal cornea otherwise the rest of the anterior segment was free. What investigation is not advised in this condition?",
  options:["CT eye and orbit","Plain X ray to the orbit","MRI","B scan ultrasound"],
  answer:2,
  explanation:"MRI is the correct \"not advised\" investigation because the lecture states this as a core rule when a metallic intraocular foreign body is suspected: “SLIDE 52 — If you suspect IOFB ★ CORE... DO NOT DO MRI SCAN IF A METALLIC IOFB IS SUSPECTED” (L25, slide 52). A mechanic hammering iron who feels something strike the eye, with a sealed entry wound in the cornea, is the classic setup for a retained metallic intraocular foreign body from a high-velocity metal fragment; the strong magnetic field of an MRI scanner can move or heat a ferromagnetic fragment lodged inside the globe, causing further mechanical damage to intraocular structures.\n\nCT eye and orbit, option A, is explicitly recommended instead of MRI in this scenario: the same slide lists \"CT SCAN — Determine the location and presence of a metallic IOFB [and] show the shape and integrity of the traumatized globe\" (L25, slide 52), so it is an appropriate, advised investigation, not a contraindicated one.\n\nPlain X-ray to the orbit, option B, is likewise listed as an appropriate investigation on the same slide — \"Investigation may include: Plain film x-rays of the head\" (L25, slide 52) — and plain films have long been used to screen for radio-opaque metallic foreign bodies, so it is advised rather than avoided.\n\nB-scan ultrasound, option D, is also listed among the useful investigations on that slide (\"Ultrasound may be useful\", L25, slide 52), and the accompanying figures show a B-scan ultrasound image of an IOFB (L25, slide 53/54); it is generally used cautiously (avoiding pressure on an open globe) but is not the investigation the lecture singles out as prohibited — that designation is reserved for MRI alone.\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> MRI is contraindicated if a metallic intraocular foreign body is suspected, as the magnetic field can move or heat the metal, causing serious damage.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Recall that MRI is contraindicated when a metallic intraocular foreign body is suspected, unlike CT, plain X-ray or B-scan ultrasound",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2138-2139" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-5-59", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-ret-dr",
  stem:"In the management of diabetic retinopathy, optimal glycemic control is targeted to achieve which of the following HbA 1 c levels?",
  options:["5%","6%","7%","8%"],
  answer:2,
  explanation:"7% is the keyed HbA1c target because tight glycemic control is the lecture's stated strategy for limiting diabetic retinopathy: \"intensive glycemic control is associated with a reduced risk of newly diagnosed retinopathy and reduced progression of existing retinopathy in people with IDDM\" and, for type 2 disease, \"intensive glycemic control compared to conventional treatment was associated with reduction in progression to severe nonproliferative and proliferative retinopathy, a decreased incidence of macular edema, and reduction in need for panretinal and focal macular laser photocoagulation\" (L23). The lecture establishes that keeping blood glucose control tight reduces the onset and progression of retinopathy, but the specific numeric target of an HbA1c around 7% is not itself printed in this deck (not taken from the course material); it is the widely used general diabetes-control target that corresponds to \"intensive\" or \"tight\" glycemic control as the lecture describes it.\n\n5%, option A, is below the normal non-diabetic reference range for HbA1c and is not an achievable or recommended treatment target for a person with diabetes; setting a target this low would risk dangerous hypoglycaemia rather than meaningfully reducing retinopathy risk beyond what tight control at a realistic target already achieves (not taken from the course material).\n\n6%, option B, is close to the non-diabetic/near-normal range and is tighter than the standard target used in most diabetic patients; the lecture's own framing is about \"intensive\" versus \"conventional\" control achieving benefit, and the conventionally accepted intensive target sits at approximately 7%, not 6%, in routine practice (not taken from the course material).\n\n8%, option D, corresponds to looser, \"conventional\" rather than \"intensive\" glycemic control; the lecture states that conventional control (as opposed to intensive control) was associated with more progression to severe retinopathy and more need for laser treatment, so a target this high would not represent the level of control the lecture credits with reducing retinopathy risk (not taken from the course material for the specific percentage, though the concept of conventional versus intensive control being less protective is stated in the lecture).\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Optimal glycemic control in diabetic retinopathy aims for HbA1c around 7%, reducing the risk of progression and other microvascular complications.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"State the target HbA1c level for optimal glycemic control in diabetic retinopathy management",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2140-2141" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-5-66", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-squint",
  stem:"Corneal light reflex testing was performed on a patient. The light reflection was seen in the center of the right eye and in the temporal part of the pupil of the left eye. Which of the following is the significance of these findings?",
  options:["15 degree left esotropia","15 degree left exotropia","45 degree left esotropia","45 degree left exotropia"],
  answer:0,
  explanation:"15 degree left esotropia is the bank's printed key. The lecture's own worked photograph of the corneal light reflex test supports a temporally-displaced reflex meaning the eye is turned inward, not outward: on the cover-test slide, the fixing eye has its reflex centred, while the deviating eye is described as \"TURNED IN toward the nose: its iris is displaced nasally, a wide white crescent of sclera is exposed on its TEMPORAL side, and its corneal light reflex is displaced OUTWARD, sitting toward the temporal edge of the pupil rather than centrally... a manifest convergent deviation — ESOTROPIA\" (L29, slide 11). The stem describes exactly this pattern for the left eye — reflex in the temporal part of the pupil, with the right eye's reflex central — so by the lecture's own example this is a left esotropia, matching the printed key of A. The lecture also states plainly that the millimetre-to-degree conversion used to size a deviation from reflex displacement is never printed anywhere in this deck (L29, note on slides 5/11/18), so the specific figure of 15 degrees for this amount of displacement is not taken from the course material.\n\n15 degree left exotropia, option B, describes the correct magnitude class of deviation but the wrong direction: exotropia requires the deviating eye's reflex to be displaced nasally (toward the inner canthus), which is the mirror image of what the lecture's slide 6 diagram shows for exotropia — \"the eye ... is turned OUT ... its iris sits against the outer (temporal) corner and the wide white crescent is on the NASAL side\" (L29, slide 6) — not the temporal-reflex pattern given in the stem.\n\n45 degree left esotropia, option C, has the correct direction but an unsupported magnitude; the lecture never gives a millimetre-to-degree conversion scale, so no specific angular value, including 45 degrees, can be derived from the stem's description of reflex position alone (not taken from the course material).\n\n45 degree left exotropia, option D, is wrong on both counts: it inverts the direction (exotropia would need a nasally displaced reflex, not the temporal one described) and assigns an unsupported magnitude for the same reason as option C.\n\nDiscrepancy note: the page marks A (15 degree left esotropia) as the key, but its own printed explanation box reasons toward exotropia, stating \"A temporal light reflex means the eye is deviated outward (exotropia)\". This directly contradicts the lecture's own worked example, in which a temporally displaced corneal light reflex is used to demonstrate esotropia, not exotropia. Per standing rule, the printed key (A) is not disputed or moved; the box's stated reasoning is recorded here as a discrepancy only, without a ruling on which physiological direction convention the box's authors intended.\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> A temporal light reflex means the eye is deviated outward (exotropia). Each millimeter of displacement roughly equals 15 degrees of deviation.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Interpret a temporally displaced corneal light reflex on Hirschberg-style testing as esotropia, matching the lecture's own worked example",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2153-2154" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-5-67", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-refract",
  stem:"Which of the following is the most common cause of vision impairment?",
  options:["Cataract","Gluacoma","Uncorrected refractive errors","Trachoma"],
  answer:2,
  explanation:"Uncorrected refractive errors are the correct answer to \"most common cause of vision impairment\": myopia, hyperopia and astigmatism are extremely common in the general population, and when they are left uncorrected they blur vision without destroying the eye's structure, which is why they outnumber all disease-based causes of visual impairment worldwide combined. This global epidemiological ranking is not stated anywhere in the ophthalmology lecture material searched for this course (not taken from the course material); the lectures on refractive errors define emmetropia and ametropia and describe their optics, but they do not rank refractive error against other causes of vision impairment by prevalence.\n\nCataract, option A, is a major cause of vision impairment and, in many epidemiological rankings, the leading cause of blindness specifically, which the cataract lecture does print (\"Leading cause of reversible blindness worldwide\", L18, page 4) (as opposed to vision impairment in general, a broader category that also includes milder degrees of visual loss); it is not the single most common cause of vision impairment overall once uncorrected refractive error, which is far more prevalent and generally milder, is counted (not taken from the course material).\n\nGlaucoma, option B, is a major cause of irreversible blindness worldwide because of progressive optic nerve damage, which the chronic-visual-loss lecture prints as \"Leading cause of irreversible blindness\" (L35,36, page 5), but it is far less prevalent in the general population than uncorrected refractive error and is not ranked as the most common cause of vision impairment overall (not taken from the course material).\n\nTrachoma, option D, is an infectious cause of corneal scarring and blindness that remains endemic in some regions, but it affects a much smaller proportion of the global population than uncorrected refractive error and is not the leading cause of vision impairment worldwide (not taken from the course material).\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Globally, the most common cause of visual impairment is uncorrected refractive error (myopia, hyperopia, astigmatism).\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"State that uncorrected refractive error is the most common cause of vision impairment worldwide",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2155-2156" },

{ id:"ophep2-model-exam-5-76", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-orbit",
  stem:"Following ocular trauma, a patient presented with enophthalmos and anesthesia of lower eyelid, cheek, side of nose, upper lip, upper teeth and gums. Which of the following is the nerve that is most probably involved in the trauma and caused these manifestations?",
  options:["Supraorbital nerve","Infraorbital nerve","Facial nerve","Glossopharyngeal nerve"],
  answer:1,
  explanation:"The patient’s enophthalmos and loss of sensation over the lower eyelid, cheek, lateral nose, upper lip, and upper teeth is classic for an orbital‑floor (blow‑out) fracture that compresses the infra‑orbital canal. The deck states that \"INFRAORBITAL NERVE ANAESTHESIA: Involving the lower lid, cheek, side of nose, upper lip, upper teeth and gums if the fracture involves the infraorbital canal\" (L25, slide 22). Enophthalmos is listed as a possible sign of an orbital‑floor fracture (L25, slide 22). Therefore the nerve most likely injured is the infra‑orbital nerve, option B.\n\nThe supra‑orbital nerve supplies the forehead, scalp and upper eyelid; it does not innervate the lower lid, cheek, or upper lip, so it cannot explain the described anesthesia pattern (not taken from the course material).\n\nThe facial nerve is a motor nerve that controls the muscles of facial expression and does not provide cutaneous sensation to the mid‑facial region; thus it would not cause the sensory deficits noted (not taken from the course material).\n\nThe glossopharyngeal nerve carries taste and general sensation from the posterior third of the tongue, pharynx, and tonsils, and has no branches to the orbit or mid‑face, making it an implausible cause of the presented signs (not taken from the course material).\n\n**The endpoint file prints this explanation:**\n\n> Orbital floor fractures commonly injure the infraorbital nerve, causing numbness of the lower eyelid, cheek, and upper lip.\n\n*The expansion above this line is written for this bank; the box itself is the endpoint file’s own.*",
  objective:"Identify the nerve damaged in an orbital floor fracture that produces infra‑orbital sensory loss and enophthalmos",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2173-2174" },

{ id:"ophep2-summer-exam-1", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-neuro-optic",
  stem:"A 45-year-old healthy male undergoes a routine comprehensive eye examination. Ophthalmoscopy reveals healthy, pink optic discs with distinct margins. What is the universally accepted physiologically normal cup-to-disc (C/D) ratio in a healthy adult?",
  options:["0.2 to 0.4","0.5 to 0.7","0.8 to 0.9","1.0"],
  answer:0,
  explanation:"Option A is correct. A C/D ratio of 0.2 to 0.4 is the expected physiologically normal range in a healthy adult (not taken from the course material). This fits the normal fundus described in the lecture: the physiological cup is “small-to-moderate, central, occupying well under half the disc,” with a healthy neuroretinal rim and sharp disc margins (L21, slide 2). The healthy pink discs and distinct margins in the stem further support a normal optic-disc appearance.\n\nOption B is wrong because a C/D ratio of 0.5 to 0.7 is larger than the keyed normal range and should prompt assessment of the neuroretinal rim, inter-eye asymmetry, intraocular pressure, and visual field rather than being called universally normal (not taken from the course material). The lecture’s normal-disc image instead shows a cup occupying well under half the disc (L21, slide 2).\n\nOption C is wrong because a C/D ratio of 0.8 to 0.9 represents marked cupping, not the expected physiological cup in this healthy examination (not taken from the course material). The lecture contrasts the normal small central cup with glaucomatous discs in which the cup fills nearly the whole disc and the rim becomes a thin crescent (L21, slide 11).\n\nOption D is wrong because a C/D ratio of 1.0 means the cup occupies the entire disc, with no visible neuroretinal rim; this is not a physiologically normal finding (not taken from the course material). It is incompatible with the lecture’s normal disc, which has a healthy pink-orange rim all the way round (L21, slide 2).\n\nWritten for this bank — Opthalmology endpoint.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:"Identify the physiologically normal cup-to-disc ratio expected in a healthy adult.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2176-2177" },

{ id:"ophep2-summer-exam-2", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-squint",
  stem:"A 6-year-old child is diagnosed with severe anisometropic amblyopia. The ophthalmologist aggressively prescribes occlusion therapy (patching) for the dominant eye. Up to what approximate chronological age does the visual cortex retain sufficient plasticity to benefit significantly from this therapy?",
  options:["8 to 10 years","2 to 4 years","12 to 14 years","18 years"],
  answer:0,
  explanation:"A. 8 to 10 years is the keyed answer. In anisometropic amblyopia, unequal refractive input during early visual development causes the brain to favour the clearer dominant-eye image; patching the dominant eye compels use of the amblyopic eye while cortical visual development remains responsive. The clinically important sensitive period extends approximately to 8 to 10 years, so a 6-year-old can still benefit substantially from aggressive occlusion treatment (not taken from the course material). The lecture identifies occlusion as “Treatment of amblyopia” and shows opaque and translucent spectacle occluders (L29, slide 32), but it does not give the age range. Indeed, it explicitly states: “NO REGIMEN IS GIVEN” and “no age limit” (L29, slide 32).\n\nB. 2 to 4 years is wrong because this is within the highly responsive early period, not its approximate upper limit. Treatment should be instituted promptly at this age if amblyopia is detected, but benefit is not restricted to this narrow age range (not taken from the course material).\n\nC. 12 to 14 years is wrong as the usual approximate age at which visual-cortical plasticity provides significant benefit from conventional amblyopia treatment is earlier, around 8 to 10 years. Some older children may still show improvement, but this does not make 12 to 14 years the expected cut-off for substantial benefit (not taken from the course material).\n\nD. 18 years is wrong because routine occlusion therapy is much less predictably effective once the childhood sensitive period has passed; adult cortical plasticity does not support 18 years as the approximate age limit tested here (not taken from the course material).\n\nThe associated refractive lecture calls anisometropia “the most common cause of amblyopia in children” (L7,8, slide 83), but likewise supplies no occlusion regimen, critical period, or age limit.\n\nWritten for this bank — Opthalmology endpoint.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:"Identify that amblyopia patching has significant benefit while visual cortical plasticity persists, approximately until 8 to 10 years of age.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2178-2179" },

{ id:"ophep2-summer-exam-3", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-squint",
  stem:"A pediatric patient presents with an inward turning of the eyes (esotropia) that becomes more pronounced when focusing on near objects. Cycloplegic refraction reveals +4.50 D of bilateral hyperopia. What is the classic peak age of onset for this condition?",
  options:["2 to 4 years","Birth to 6 months","8 to 10 years","12 to 14 years"],
  answer:0,
  explanation:"Option A is keyed correctly. The presentation is accommodative esotropia: esotropia associated with hypermetropia and a high AC/A ratio, becoming greater at near fixation. The lecture gives its age as “2.5 - 5 years” (L29, slide 29). Thus 2 to 4 years best captures the classic preschool onset among the options. The lecture’s stated range extends to 5 years, a minor discrepancy from the keyed option’s upper limit; the printed key remains A.\n\nOption B is wrong because birth to 6 months is the lecture’s age for congenital esotropia, which is separately described as “< 6 months,” large-angle and constant (L29, slide 28), rather than accommodative esotropia.\n\nOption C is wrong because 8 to 10 years lies beyond the lecture’s stated “2.5 - 5 years” range for accommodative esotropia (L29, slide 29).\n\nOption D is wrong for the same reason: 12 to 14 years is well outside the preschool age range taught for accommodative esotropia (L29, slide 29). The +4.50 D cycloplegic finding is compatible with the lecture’s association with “Hypermetropia,” although the lecture gives no dioptric threshold or cycloplegic-refraction detail (L29, slide 29).\n\nWritten for this bank — Opthalmology endpoint.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:"Accommodative esotropia classically presents in preschool children, with the lecture giving an age range of 2.5 - 5 years.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2180-2181" },

{ id:"ophep2-summer-exam-4", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-orbit",
  stem:"An 8-year-old child presents with a swollen, erythematous right eyelid and a low-grade fever. Visual acuity, extraocular movements, and proptosis evaluation are completely normal. Which clinical finding most strongly differentiates this diagnosis from life-threatening orbital cellulitis?",
  options:["Normal Pupil Response","Elevated intraocular pressure","Presence of a relative afferent pupillary defect (RAPD)","Severe conjunctival chemosis"],
  answer:0,
  explanation:"This is preseptal cellulitis. The keyed answer, normal pupil response, is correct because the lecture states that in preseptal cellulitis “VA, pupillary reactions and ocular motility are UNIMPAIRED” (L10, slide 24). This child’s swollen red lid with normal visual acuity, eye movements, proptosis assessment, and pupil response therefore supports infection anterior to the orbital septum rather than orbital cellulitis.\n\nElevated intraocular pressure is wrong because raised IOP is listed among the ocular complications of bacterial orbital cellulitis, not as a normal finding in preseptal cellulitis (L10, slide 27). It would raise concern for the dangerous orbital process.\n\nPresence of a relative afferent pupillary defect (RAPD) is wrong because the lecture specifically lists “Relative afferent pupillary defect” among the clinical features of bacterial orbital cellulitis (L10, slide 26). A normal pupillary response, not an RAPD, fits the diagnosis in this vignette.\n\nSevere conjunctival chemosis is wrong because the lecture says that, in contrast to orbital cellulitis, “proptosis and chemosis are ABSENT” in preseptal cellulitis (L10, slide 24). Chemosis would therefore favour orbital involvement rather than the superficial preseptal infection.\n\nWritten for this bank — Opthalmology endpoint.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:"Preseptal cellulitis has preserved pupillary reactions, whereas orbital cellulitis may produce an RAPD and other signs of orbital involvement.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2182-2183" },

{ id:"ophep2-summer-exam-5", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-intro-exam",
  stem:"An 18-year-old male fails the Ishihara pseudoisochromatic plate test during a pre-employment physical examination, confirming red-green color blindness. He reports that his maternal grandfather had the same condition. What is the mode of inheritance of this disorder?",
  options:["X-linked recessive","Autosomal dominant","Autosomal recessive","Mitochondrial inheritance"],
  answer:0,
  explanation:"A. X-linked recessive is correct. Red-green colour blindness is usually caused by pathogenic variants affecting red- or green-sensitive cone photopigments on the X chromosome (not taken from the course material). An affected male has only one X chromosome, so one altered allele produces the condition; the history through his maternal grandfather is compatible with transmission through the maternal line (not taken from the course material). The lecture identifies “Colour blindness” as a visual complaint and lists “Colour vision testing” among complementary tests, but it gives no inheritance pattern and does not name Ishihara plates (L1,2, slide 103).\n\nB. Autosomal dominant inheritance would usually show vertical transmission in both sexes, with an affected parent commonly transmitting the disorder to about half of offspring (not taken from the course material). That pattern is not the typical inheritance of red-green colour blindness.\n\nC. Autosomal recessive inheritance requires biallelic pathogenic variants and affects males and females similarly (not taken from the course material). It does not account for the characteristic male predominance of red-green colour blindness.\n\nD. Mitochondrial inheritance is transmitted only through an affected mother: all her children may inherit the mitochondrial variant, while affected fathers do not transmit it (not taken from the course material). This is not the usual inheritance pattern of red-green colour blindness.\n\nWritten for this bank — Opthalmology endpoint.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:"Recognize that congenital red-green colour blindness is typically inherited as an X-linked recessive disorder.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2184-2185" },

{ id:"ophep2-summer-exam-6", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-cat",
  stem:"A 75-year-old male complains of gradually worsening distance vision. Paradoxically, he is delighted that he can suddenly read the newspaper without his reading glasses (“second sight”). Which type of cataract classically causes this refractive shift?",
  options:["Nuclear sclerotic cataract","Cortical cataract","Posterior subcapsular cataract","Rosette cataract"],
  answer:2,
  explanation:"The keyed answer is C, posterior subcapsular cataract. PSC is a posterior capsular opacity near the nodal point and is described as the most visually disabling per unit size; its worst symptoms are near vision, bright light, and miosis (L18, page 6). Thus it can cause major visual difficulty, particularly while reading in bright light. However, the lecture directly contradicts the printed key for the specific “second sight” refractive shift: it states, “Nuclear: myopic shift allows reading without glasses (temporary)” (L18, page 5), and under nuclear cataract, “‘Second sight’: elderly regain near vision temporarily” (L18, page 6). The printed key remains C as required, but this discrepancy should be noted.\n\nA. Nuclear sclerotic cataract is wrong according to the printed key. The lecture nevertheless identifies increased nuclear refractive index causing acquired myopia and predominantly impaired distant vision, which produces the temporary ability to read without glasses (L18, page 6). It also explicitly summarizes that a patient with nuclear cataract may read better because of “second sight” (L18, page 21).\n\nB. Cortical cataract is wrong because it consists of wedge-shaped or spoke-like peripheral opacities, with visual-acuity impact depending on their proximity to the visual axis (L18, page 6). The lecture associates cortical opacity with monocular diplopia and glare, rather than the acquired myopic shift described in the stem (L18, page 5).\n\nD. Rosette cataract is wrong because it is a traumatic cataract pattern, rather than the typical age-related refractive explanation for this vignette (not taken from the course material).\n\nWritten for this bank — Opthalmology endpoint.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:"Recognize the cataract type associated with the temporary myopic “second sight” phenomenon.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2186-2187" },

{ id:"ophep2-summer-exam-7", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-trauma",
  stem:"A 30-year-old male is struck in the face during a bar fight. He presents with blurred vision and severe aching pain in the affected eye. Slit-lamp examination reveals a distinct horizontal fluid level of bright red blood layering within the anterior chamber. What is the most likely diagnosis?",
  options:["Hyphema","Hypopyon","Subconjunctival hemorrhage","Vitreous hemorrhage"],
  answer:0,
  explanation:"A. Hyphema is correct. Following blunt facial trauma, the distinct horizontal level of bright red blood in the anterior chamber is a hyphema. The examination lecture explicitly labels anterior-chamber “Blood (Hyphema)” and distinguishes it from “Pus (Hypopyon)” (L3,4, slide 27). The trauma lecture also addresses hyphema after blunt trauma, including its grading and complications, such as re-bleeding in the “FIRST 5 DAYS” and uncontrolled high IOP (L25, slide 25; L25, slide 27).\n\nB. Hypopyon is wrong because it is pus, not blood, in the anterior chamber; the course labels it “Pus (Hypopyon)” (L3,4, slide 27). The described bright red fluid level is therefore inconsistent with hypopyon.\n\nC. Subconjunctival hemorrhage is wrong because the blood is beneath the conjunctiva on the external surface of the eye, rather than layering inside the anterior chamber (not taken from the course material). Although subconjunctival hemorrhage can occur after trauma, the trauma lecture lists it with orbital-floor-fracture presentation, not as anterior-chamber blood (L25, slide 20).\n\nD. Vitreous hemorrhage is wrong because it is bleeding into the vitreous cavity behind the lens, not a visible anterior-chamber fluid level (not taken from the course material). The course instead associates dense vitreous hemorrhage with a “Black reflex” (L3,4, slide 51), whereas this vignette gives the anterior-chamber blood sign of hyphema.\n\nWritten for this bank — Opthalmology endpoint.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:"Identify hyphema as blood layering in the anterior chamber after blunt ocular trauma.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2188-2189" },

{ id:"ophep2-summer-exam-8", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-onc",
  stem:"A 60-year-old female with a known history of advanced breast carcinoma presents with gradually decreasing vision in her left eye. Dilated fundus examination reveals a solitary, creamy-yellow, multilobulated subretinal mass. Anatomically, what is the most common intraocular site for metastatic tumors?",
  options:["Choroid","Retina","Optic nerve","Iris"],
  answer:0,
  explanation:"A. Choroid is correct. The lesion described is a choroidal metastasis: the lecture classifies “Choroidal metastasis (Breast-Bronchi)” as a secondary choroidal malignancy and illustrates “creamy multifocal subretinal lesions” as choroidal metastases (L24, slide 12). Thus, despite appearing subretinal on fundoscopy, the metastatic deposit is anatomically in the choroid. The lecture does not explicitly state that the choroid is the most common intraocular metastatic site; that prevalence statement is not taken from the course material.\n\nB. Retina is wrong because a subretinal lesion is located beneath the retina and may arise from the choroid; it is not therefore a retinal metastasis. The lecture’s metastatic examples are specifically labelled choroidal metastases (L24, slide 12).\n\nC. Optic nerve is wrong because optic-nerve metastasis is not the usual intraocular site for metastatic tumour (not taken from the course material).\n\nD. Iris is wrong because iris metastases can occur but are less common than choroidal metastases (not taken from the course material).\n\nWritten for this bank — Opthalmology endpoint.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:"Identify the choroid as the most common intraocular site of metastatic tumours.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2190-2191" },

{ id:"ophep2-summer-exam-9", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-ret-vasc",
  stem:"During a routine dilated fundus examination, an ophthalmologist evaluates the retinal vasculature for hypertensive changes by comparing the caliber of retinal veins to retinal arteries. What is the normal physiological vein-to-artery (V:A) diameter ratio in the retina?",
  options:["3:2","1:1","1:2","4:1"],
  answer:0,
  explanation:"Option A is correct: the normal retinal vein-to-artery diameter ratio is 3:2, meaning that retinal veins are normally wider than retinal arteries (not taken from the course material). This provides the baseline for recognising relative arteriolar narrowing during fundus examination. The examination lecture labels both “Retinal artery” and “Retinal vein” in its fundus picture, but explicitly lists “The normal retinal VEIN-TO-ARTERY RATIO” among points not covered in the deck (L3,4, slide 58).\n\nOption B, 1:1, is wrong because it would mean equal venous and arterial diameters; normally the veins are wider (not taken from the course material). Option C, 1:2, is wrong because it reverses the expected relationship and makes the vein narrower than the artery (not taken from the course material). Option D, 4:1, is wrong because it represents an extreme venous predominance, not the normal physiological calibre relationship (not taken from the course material).\n\nWritten for this bank — Opthalmology endpoint.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:"State the normal retinal vein-to-artery diameter ratio.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2192-2193" },

{ id:"ophep2-summer-exam-10", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-lac",
  stem:"A 7-month-old infant presents with chronic unilateral epiphora and recurrent mild mucopurulent discharge since birth. A fluorescein dye disappearance test is prolonged, confirming congenital nasolacrimal duct obstruction. What is the best initial management?",
  options:["Crigler massage","Immediate dacryocystorhinostomy (DCR)","Topical corticosteroids","Probing and irrigation"],
  answer:0,
  explanation:"A. Crigler massage is right. The lecture calls this “Massage of the lacrimal sac” and explains that it may rupture a membranous obstruction by hydrostatic pressure (L9, slide 22). This fits congenital nasolacrimal duct obstruction: the lower end of the duct near the valve of Hasner is the last part to canalize, and complete patency commonly occurs soon after birth (L9, slide 21). The infant’s epiphora and recurrent mucopurulent discharge are also consistent with the lecture’s description of epiphora, eyelash matting, and mucopurulent reflux on gentle lacrimal-sac pressure (L9, slide 21). Initial conservative management is appropriate because spontaneous resolution occurs in approximately 85% within the first year (L9, slide 21).\n\nB. Immediate dacryocystorhinostomy is wrong because it is not the initial treatment described for congenital obstruction. The congenital-treatment slide begins with massage, then lists probing, and describes further procedures only after probing failure (L9, slide 22). DCR is listed separately as treatment for acquired nasolacrimal duct obstruction (L9, slide 29).\n\nC. Topical corticosteroids are wrong because they do not relieve the membranous distal drainage obstruction. The lecture lists topical steroids or other anti-inflammatories for conjunctivochalasis, an acquired cause of epiphora, rather than congenital nasolacrimal duct obstruction (L9, slide 24).\n\nD. Probing and irrigation are wrong as the best initial choice in this 7-month-old because the lecture presents them after massage in its congenital-NLDO treatment sequence. Probing passes a fine wire through the canalicular system and nasolacrimal duct to disrupt the membrane at the valve of Hasner, followed by irrigation to confirm patency (L9, slide 22). It is therefore an escalation if conservative massage does not succeed, not the first step here.\n\nWritten for this bank — Opthalmology endpoint.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:"Congenital nasolacrimal duct obstruction in an infant is initially managed with lacrimal-sac massage because most cases resolve within the first year.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2194-2195" },

{ id:"ophep2-summer-exam-11", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-cat",
  stem:"A 35-year-old male with a known history of retinitis pigmentosa complains of progressively worsening glare from oncoming headlights while driving at night. Slit-lamp examination reveals a distinct granular opacity located on the posterior surface of the crystalline lens. What is the most likely diagnosis?",
  options:["Posterior subcapsular cataract","Cortical cataract","Nuclear sclerotic cataract","Vossius ring"],
  answer:0,
  explanation:"A. Posterior subcapsular cataract is the keyed diagnosis. The course lists “Retinal dystrophy, retinitis pigmentosa” under complicated cataract (L35,36, page 21), and states that posterior subcapsular cataracts cause “glare as well as image blur and distortion” (L35,36, page 27). Thus, a posterior lens opacity with disabling glare in a patient with retinitis pigmentosa fits PSC. The specific word “granular” is not used by the course to describe PSC (not taken from the course material).\n\nB. Cortical cataract is wrong because it is an opacity of lens fibres surrounding the nucleus and typically begins as peripheral “wedge-shaped / spoke-like” opacities, rather than a posterior capsular opacity (L18, page 6).\n\nC. Nuclear sclerotic cataract is wrong because it is an exaggeration of nuclear sclerosis, with yellowing progressing to brunescence. It predominantly affects distant vision and can produce acquired myopia or temporary “second sight,” unlike this posterior opacity with glare (L18, page 6).\n\nD. Vossius ring is wrong because it is a pigment imprint on the anterior lens capsule after blunt ocular trauma, not a posterior lens opacity associated with retinitis pigmentosa (not taken from the course material). The course likewise places Vossius ring with traumatic cataract following blunt injury (L18, page 24).\n\nWritten for this bank — Opthalmology endpoint.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:"Recognize posterior subcapsular cataract from posterior lens opacity and glare, including its association with retinitis pigmentosa.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2196-2197" },

{ id:"ophep2-summer-exam-12", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-lac",
  stem:"A 60-year-old female presents with a severely tender, erythematous, fluctuant swelling localized over the medial canthus. Purulent discharge refluxes from the punctum upon palpation. She also complains of tearing and pain around the affected eye.\n\nWhat is the most appropriate initial treatment for this condition?",
  options:["Topical and systemic antibiotics","Immediate dacryocystorhinostomy (DCR) surgery","Probing and irrigation","Topical corticosteroids"],
  answer:0,
  explanation:"This is acute dacryocystitis: the painful medial-canthal swelling, epiphora and abscess-like fluctuation match the lecture’s description of “pain in the medial canthal area,” “epiphora,” and “a very tender, tense red swelling” that commonly progresses to abscess formation (L9, slide 32). A is therefore the keyed initial treatment because this is an infection of the lacrimal sac, usually secondary to nasolacrimal-duct obstruction and commonly staphylococcal or streptococcal (L9, slide 32). Systemic antibiotic treatment addresses the acute infection. The lecture specifically states: “Initial treatment involves the application of warm compresses and oral antibiotics” (L9, slide 32). Thus, the topical-antibiotic component of option A is not stated in the course material; the printed key nevertheless remains A.\n\nB is wrong as the immediate step because the lecture says dacryocystorhinostomy is commonly required only “after the acute infection has been controlled” (L9, slide 32).\n\nC is wrong because “Irrigation and probing should not be performed” in acute dacryocystitis (L9, slide 32). The lecture also states that palpation is painful and should be avoided in acute dacryocystitis (L9, slide 11).\n\nD is wrong because corticosteroids do not treat the underlying bacterial lacrimal-sac infection (not taken from the course material). Initial management instead uses warm compresses and oral antibiotics; if pus points and an abscess is about to drain spontaneously, incision and drainage may be considered (L9, slide 32).\n\nWritten for this bank — Opthalmology endpoint.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:"Recognize acute dacryocystitis and select antibiotic treatment as the initial management, with DCR deferred until the acute infection is controlled.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2198-2199" },

{ id:"ophep2-summer-exam-13", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-insid",
  stem:"An 82-year-old female with a long-standing history of untreated wet age-related macular degeneration presents with profound irreversible central vision loss. Dilated fundus examination reveals a dense white fibrovascular disciform scar replacing the fovea. What is the most appropriate management for this lesion?",
  options:["Observation and low-vision aids","Intravitreal anti-VEGF injections","Pan-retinal photocoagulation","Scleral buckling"],
  answer:0,
  explanation:"A. Observation and low-vision aids is correct because this is an established disciform scar replacing the fovea with profound irreversible central visual loss. A fibrotic end-stage macular lesion cannot be restored by treating prior neovascular activity; management is supportive, including low-vision rehabilitation and aids to maximize remaining peripheral vision (not taken from the course material). The lecture supports that AMD affects central vision and can impair reading and other fine-detail activities (L35,36, page 39).\n\nB. Intravitreal anti-VEGF injections are used for active wet AMD with choroidal neovascularization, but they do not reverse an inactive dense fibrovascular scar or recover destroyed foveal function (not taken from the course material). There is an important course discrepancy: the lecture states broadly, “For wet AMD injections of Anti VEGF” (L35,36, page 45), whereas this vignette specifically describes irreversible scar-stage disease; the printed key remains A.\n\nC. Pan-retinal photocoagulation is not treatment for a macular disciform scar. PRP treats proliferative diabetic retinopathy; the lecture lists “PDR     PRP” under diabetic-retinopathy management (L35,36, page 51). It would not restore central vision from this scar (not taken from the course material).\n\nD. Scleral buckling is a surgical treatment for selected rhegmatogenous retinal detachments, not macular neovascularization or a disciform scar (not taken from the course material).\n\nWritten for this bank — Opthalmology endpoint.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:"Recognize that end-stage disciform scarring from wet AMD is managed with observation and low-vision support rather than lesion-directed treatment.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2200-2201" },

{ id:"ophep2-summer-exam-14", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-trauma",
  stem:"A 25-year-old male is struck directly in the eye by a fast-moving baseball. He presents with enophthalmos, restricted upward gaze, infraorbital numbness, and severe vertical diplopia that worsens on attempted upward gaze.\n\nWhich orbital wall is most commonly involved in this type of blowout fracture?",
  options:["Orbital floor","Orbital roof","Medial orbital wall","Lateral orbital wall"],
  answer:0,
  explanation:"A. Orbital floor is correct. This is the classic presentation of an orbital-floor blow-out fracture after blunt orbital trauma. The lecture specifically labels this injury “Blunt trauma: Blow-out fracture of the orbital floor” and lists trauma from a “FIST, TENNIS BALL” as the history (L25, slide 19; L25, slide 20). It explains that diplopia occurs in “BOTH UPGAZE AND DOWNGAZE” because of restrictive ocular motility from mechanical entrapment of the inferior oblique or inferior rectus, adjacent connective tissue, or fat (L25, slide 21). The patient’s restricted elevation and worse diplopia on attempted upgaze therefore fit this mechanism. Infraorbital numbness also fits the stated “INFRAORBITAL NERVE ANAESTHESIA” when the fracture involves the infraorbital canal, while enophthalmos is listed in severe cases (L25, slide 22). Thus the combination strongly identifies an orbital-floor fracture.\n\nB. Orbital roof is wrong because it is not the characteristic wall implicated by this blunt-trauma pattern; the course instead teaches the floor injury and its associated restrictive diplopia, infraorbital nerve anaesthesia, and enophthalmos. The lecture does not provide separate clinical features of orbital-roof fractures (not taken from the course material).\n\nC. Medial orbital wall is wrong because, although another wall may accompany a floor fracture, the CT teaching point is “Fracture of the orbital floor ± other orbital wall,” and the vignette’s taught signs identify the floor as the principal involved wall (L25, slide 23). The lecture does not separately describe medial-wall fractures (not taken from the course material).\n\nD. Lateral orbital wall is wrong because the described blow-out syndrome is taught as an orbital-floor fracture, not as a lateral-wall injury (L25, slide 19–22). The lecture does not separately describe the clinical presentation of lateral-wall fractures (not taken from the course material).\n\nWritten for this bank — Opthalmology endpoint.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:"Recognize that blunt orbital trauma with restrictive vertical diplopia, infraorbital numbness, and enophthalmos indicates an orbital-floor blow-out fracture.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2202-2203" },

{ id:"ophep2-summer-exam-15", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-vitreous",
  stem:"A 65-year-old male presents in distress after suddenly noticing a shower of new floaters and flashes of light in his right eye. Dilated fundus examination reveals a complete posterior vitreous separation with a prominent Weiss ring. Careful retinal examination shows no retinal tears, holes, or vitreoretinal traction. What is the most appropriate management?",
  options:["Observation and reassurance","Prophylactic barrier laser photocoagulation","Pars plana vitrectomy","Scleral buckling"],
  answer:0,
  explanation:"A. Observation and reassurance is correct. This is an uncomplicated complete posterior vitreous detachment: the Weiss ring supports separation of the posterior vitreous, and the careful examination has found no retinal tear, hole, or vitreoretinal traction. In this setting, reassurance with retinal-detachment warning advice and follow-up is appropriate (not taken from the course material). The lecture does not teach posterior vitreous detachment or Weiss ring — both are listed in its not-covered register (L20, slide 33); it states only that aging may cause “vitreous shrinkage” (L20, slide 6).\n\nB. Prophylactic barrier laser photocoagulation is wrong because there is no retinal break to surround. Laser retinopexy is used to secure selected retinal tears or breaks, not an uncomplicated posterior vitreous detachment with an intact retina (not taken from the course material). The lecture does not cover prophylactic laser for a break without detachment. Its laser reference is in pneumatic retinopexy, where a gas bubble seals a tear and is “often followed by LASER OR CRYOTHERAPY (FREEZING)” (L20, slide 14).\n\nC. Pars plana vitrectomy is wrong because there is neither a retinal detachment nor traction requiring operative relief. The lecture describes vitrectomy for rhegmatogenous retinal detachment as removal of vitreous with a gas or oil bubble to push the retina back (L20, slide 14), and for tractional detachment with membrane peeling to remove pulling fibrovascular tissue (L20, slide 15). Neither indication is present here.\n\nD. Scleral buckling is wrong because it is a repair for rhegmatogenous retinal detachment associated with a retinal break, not for isolated posterior vitreous detachment. The lecture says that surgery is almost always required to seal the retinal break in rhegmatogenous detachment and describes scleral buckling as a silicone band that helps the retina reattach (L20, slide 14). This patient has no break and no detachment.\n\nWritten for this bank — Opthalmology endpoint.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:"Recognize that an uncomplicated posterior vitreous detachment with no retinal break or traction is managed conservatively with observation and reassurance.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2204-2205" },

{ id:"ophep2-summer-exam-16", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-ret-dr",
  stem:"A 70-year-old patient presents with sudden onset of profound central visual distortion (metamorphopsia) and reduced central vision. Fluorescein angiography demonstrates active choroidal neovascularization with leakage beneath the macula.\n\nWhat is the current gold-standard treatment for this condition?",
  options:["Intravitreal anti-VEGF injections","Systemic corticosteroids","Laser photocoagulation","Observation alone"],
  answer:0,
  explanation:"The keyed option, A, intravitreal anti-VEGF injections, is right. This presentation is wet age-related macular degeneration: the lecture identifies the wet type as “RPE detachment and choroidal neovascularization” (L35,36, page 40) and states, “For wet AMD injections of Anti VEGF” (L35,36, page 45). Thus, in a patient with active submacular choroidal neovascularization, anti-VEGF treatment is the course-supported management.\n\nB, systemic corticosteroids, is wrong because the lecture’s treatment for wet AMD is anti-VEGF injections, not systemic corticosteroids (L35,36, page 45). Corticosteroids are not the current standard treatment for wet AMD-associated choroidal neovascularization (not taken from the course material).\n\nC, laser photocoagulation, is wrong as the current gold-standard choice. The lecture does mention that some macular degeneration types may be treated effectively with laser (L35,36, page 34), but it specifically gives anti-VEGF injections for wet AMD (L35,36, page 45). Therefore, the specific treatment statement for the wet, neovascular form supports A rather than laser.\n\nD, observation alone, is wrong because active wet AMD is treated with anti-VEGF injections in the lecture (L35,36, page 45), rather than simply observed. Observation would leave active neovascular leakage untreated (not taken from the course material).\n\nWritten for this bank — Opthalmology endpoint.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:"Wet age-related macular degeneration with choroidal neovascularization is treated with intravitreal anti-VEGF injections.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2206-2207" },

{ id:"ophep2-summer-exam-17", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-trauma",
  stem:"A 30-year-old metal worker is rushed to the emergency department after a high-velocity metallic fragment strikes his eye while hammering steel. CT scan of the orbit confirms a full-thickness globe penetration with a retained metallic intraocular foreign body.\n\nWhat is the most critical initial medical intervention prior to surgery?",
  options:["Intravenous broad-spectrum antibiotics","Topical corticosteroid drops","Topical pilocarpine","Intravenous mannitol"],
  answer:0,
  explanation:"A is correct. A penetrating open-globe injury with a retained metallic intraocular foreign body has a high risk of intraocular infection; prompt intravenous broad-spectrum antibiotics before definitive repair to reduce the risk of post-traumatic endophthalmitis (not taken from the course material). The scenario is clearly within the lecture’s open-globe/IOFB pathway: hammering is a suspicious history, and an IOFB “suggests globe penetration” (L25, slide 52). CT is also the appropriate imaging modality for locating a metallic IOFB (L25, slide 52).\n\nThere is a course-material gap relevant to the keyed intervention: L25 does not state that intravenous broad-spectrum antibiotics should be given for an open globe with an IOFB. Its immediate measures are instead to “Place EYE SHIELD & CALL for OPHTHALMOLOGIST immediately,” keep the patient NPO for general anaesthesia, and avoid pressure or further examination (L25, slide 51). These urgent protective measures should accompany systemic antimicrobial treatment; the lecture specifically says that no eye drops or ointment should be used on an open globe (L25, slide 51).\n\nB is wrong because topical corticosteroid drops are not the initial treatment for an open globe. They are topical eye drops, whereas the lecture directs “No further eye exam or eye drops or ointment on an open globe” (L25, slide 51). They do not provide the required systemic antimicrobial prophylaxis (not taken from the course material).\n\nC is wrong because topical pilocarpine is also an eye drop and must not be instilled into an open globe (L25, slide 51). It has no role in preventing infection after a penetrating injury with an IOFB (not taken from the course material).\n\nD is wrong because intravenous mannitol is used to lower raised intraocular pressure, not as routine initial treatment for a penetrated globe with an IOFB (not taken from the course material). The lecture explicitly says not to check eye pressure in suspected open globe injury (L25, slide 51), so there is no stated indication here for pressure-lowering treatment.\n\nWritten for this bank — Opthalmology endpoint.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:"A confirmed penetrating open-globe injury with a retained metallic IOFB requires prompt intravenous broad-spectrum antibiotic prophylaxis before surgery.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2208-2209" },

{ id:"ophep2-summer-exam-18", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-orbit",
  stem:"A 25-year-old female presents with acute painful swelling of the lateral one-third of the upper eyelid associated with erythema, tenderness, fever, and an “S-shaped” ptosis.\n\nIf this severe bacterial infection is left untreated, what is the most important local complication?",
  options:["Abscess formation","Retinal detachment","Glaucoma","Cataract"],
  answer:0,
  explanation:"The keyed answer is A. Abscess formation is the important local complication of an untreated severe bacterial infection in this region. The vignette localizes to the lacrimal gland: the course states that swelling of the lateral eyelid over the palpebral lobe produces the characteristic “S-SHAPED PTOSIS” (L10, slide 31). In its bacterial orbital-cellulitis teaching, the lecture lists “Subperiosteal abscess” among complications and notes that drainage of an orbital abscess should be considered early (L10, slide 27 and 29). Thus, suppuration with abscess formation is the relevant local progression.\n\nThe lecture’s acute dacryoadenitis slide does not itself describe a bacterial cause or abscess complication; it instead says “Idiopathic or due to viral (e.g. mumps, Epstein–Barr, cytomegalovirus” (L10, slide 31). This differs from the stem’s severe bacterial infection, but the printed key remains A.\n\nB. Retinal detachment is wrong because it is a separation of the neurosensory retina from the retinal pigment epithelium, not the expected local consequence of an acute bacterial lacrimal-gland/orbital infection (not taken from the course material).\n\nC. Glaucoma is wrong because glaucoma is an optic neuropathy commonly associated with raised intraocular pressure, rather than the principal local suppurative complication described here (not taken from the course material). The lecture does mention raised IOP among ocular complications of bacterial orbital cellulitis, but does not identify glaucoma as the key complication (L10, slide 27).\n\nD. Cataract is wrong because cataract is lens opacity and does not arise as the important immediate local complication of this acute infection (not taken from the course material).\n\nWritten for this bank — Opthalmology endpoint.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:"Recognize that an untreated severe bacterial infection with lateral upper-lid swelling and S-shaped ptosis may progress to local abscess formation.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2210-2211" },

{ id:"ophep2-summer-exam-19", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-lid",
  stem:"A 40-year-old patient presents with a severely tender, pointing red nodule on the inner surface of the eyelid that has persisted despite 5 days of warm compresses and topical antibiotics. Examination shows a large, tense, fluctuant lesion involving the tarsal plate.\n\nWhat is the most appropriate next step in management?",
  options:["Incision and drainage","Systemic corticosteroids","Intralesional triamcinolone injection","Cryotherapy"],
  answer:0,
  explanation:"A is correct. The large, tense, tender, pointing lesion deep in the lid/tarsal plate is an internal hordeolum (infected chalazion) with an abscess that has not settled with initial conservative treatment. The lecture’s internal-hordeolum photographs show a “large, tense, red-and-yellow swelling” behind the lash line with a “pale pointing centre,” and a “yellow pointing abscess” deep to the lid margin (L5, slide 20). Incision and drainage evacuates this fluctuant collection; in this severe persistent presentation, it is the appropriate next step (not taken from the course material). The lecture also depicts incision and curettage from the tarsal conjunctival side, with contents “expressed/curetted from within the tarsus” (L5, slide 19).\n\nThere is a timing discrepancy in the course material: L37 states, “Refer the patient for incision and curettage of the lesion if there is no resolution in 3–4 weeks,” (L37, page 31) whereas this vignette specifies only 5 days. The printed key remains A; the vignette’s severe tenderness, pointing, fluctuation, and tarsal involvement support drainage rather than continued observation.\n\nB is wrong. Systemic corticosteroids do not drain an eyelid abscess and could suppress the inflammatory response during infection (not taken from the course material).\n\nC is wrong. Intralesional triamcinolone may be used in selected chronic, noninfected chalazia, but this is a tender, pointing, fluctuant infected lesion; steroid injection would not provide source control (not taken from the course material).\n\nD is wrong. Cryotherapy is not treatment for an internal hordeolum or infected chalazion and would not evacuate pus (not taken from the course material).\n\nWritten for this bank — Opthalmology endpoint.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:"Recognize that a persistent, fluctuant, pointing internal hordeolum requires incision and drainage.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2212-2213" },

{ id:"ophep2-summer-exam-20", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-acute",
  stem:"A 75-year-old male with severe systemic atherosclerosis presents with sudden, profound, painless vision loss in his right eye. Fundus examination reveals a pale ischemic retina with a characteristic cherry-red spot at the fovea. Which finding would most likely be demonstrated during the swinging flashlight test?",
  options:["Ipsilateral relative afferent pupillary defect (RAPD)","Contralateral relative afferent pupillary defect","Fixed dilated pupil","Argyll Robertson pupil"],
  answer:0,
  explanation:"A is correct. The presentation is central retinal artery occlusion: the course case pairs a “white, opacified retina with a cherry-red macular spot” with the diagnosis “Central Retinal Artery Occlusion” (L31,32, slide 7; L31,32, slide 9). In that same right-eye case, “The swinging –flashlight test confirms a right relative afferent pupillary defect (Marcus Gunn pupil)” (L31,32, slide 6). Therefore, with the lesion in the right eye, the expected finding is an ipsilateral—right—RAPD. The examination lecture illustrates that, in RAPD, the pupil “Dilates” as the light is swung to the affected eye, unlike normal constriction (L3,4, slide 34).\n\nB is wrong because the defect is in the eye with the severe retinal ischaemia, not the unaffected fellow eye. The course case explicitly identifies the RAPD as right-sided while describing the left retina as normal (L31,32, slide 6–7).\n\nC is wrong because a fixed dilated pupil is an efferent pupillary abnormality, rather than the relative afferent abnormality demonstrated by this retinal lesion (not taken from the course material). The relevant course case instead records a right RAPD and preserved consensual reaction of the right pupil (L31,32, slide 5–6).\n\nD is wrong because Argyll Robertson pupil is a light-near dissociation syndrome associated in the lecture with “Affection of pretectal area,” “Small pupils,” and “Neurosyphilis,” rather than unilateral retinal artery occlusion (L22, slide 8).\n\nWritten for this bank — Opthalmology endpoint.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:"Recognize that central retinal artery occlusion produces an ipsilateral relative afferent pupillary defect on the swinging flashlight test.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2214-2215" },

{ id:"ophep2-summer-exam-21", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-acute",
  stem:"A 68-year-old male with poorly controlled hypertension and diabetes mellitus presents with sudden, painless, profound vision loss in his left eye that began one hour ago. Fundus examination reveals diffuse retinal whitening with a cherry-red spot at the macula. During the swinging flashlight test, illumination of the affected eye produces paradoxical pupillary dilation instead of constriction. Which pupillary abnormality is most likely present in this patient?",
  options:["Ipsilateral relative afferent pupillary defect (RAPD)","Contralateral relative afferent pupillary defect","Argyll Robertson pupil","Marcus Gunn jaw-winking phenomenon"],
  answer:0,
  explanation:"A is correct. This presentation is central retinal artery occlusion: sudden profound painless monocular visual loss with retinal whitening and a cherry-red macular spot. The acute-visual-loss lecture presents the same combination and identifies it as “Central Retinal Artery Occlusion” (L31,32, slide 9). In its case, the swinging-flashlight test “confirms a right relative afferent pupillary defect (Marcus Gunn pupil)” (L31,32, slide 6). Here, the affected eye is the left eye, so the defect is ipsilateral: a left RAPD. The pupil dilates paradoxically as the light is swung to that eye because the afferent light signal is relatively weaker; the pupil-and-visual-pathway lecture illustrates that, with light on the defective eye, “the pupils actually dilate as the torch swings across to it” (L22, slide 6). The retinal vascular lecture also lists CRAO with “APD - marked,” “Retinal whitening,” and “‘Cherry-red spot’ at macula” (L19.2, page 26).\n\nB is wrong because an RAPD is assigned to the eye with the impaired afferent retinal/optic-nerve input, not to the fellow eye. The vignette specifically says that illumination of the left eye causes dilation, localising the relative afferent defect to the left eye.\n\nC is wrong because Argyll Robertson pupil is a different pupil abnormality. The lecture describes “Small pupils” and “Light Near dissociation,” associated with affection of the pretectal area, neurosyphilis, DM, alcoholism, and encephalitis (L22, slide 8). It does not describe the unilateral swinging-flashlight finding in this case.\n\nD is wrong because Marcus Gunn jaw-winking phenomenon is an eyelid synkinesis, not an afferent pupillary defect. The course names “Marcus Gunn jaw-winking syndrome” as an indication for frontalis brow suspension (L5, slide 9), whereas it separately calls RAPD the “Marcus Gunn Pupil” (L31,32, slide 6).\n\nWritten for this bank — Opthalmology endpoint.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:"Identify paradoxical dilation on swinging-flashlight testing as an ipsilateral relative afferent pupillary defect.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2216-2217" },

{ id:"ophep2-summer-exam-22", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-glauc",
  stem:"A 65-year-old patient with elevated intraocular pressure is unable to tolerate topical beta-blockers because of severe bronchial asthma. The ophthalmologist prescribes a topical medication that lowers intraocular pressure by decreasing aqueous humor production from the ciliary body.\n\nWhich class of medication acts by this mechanism?",
  options:["Carbonic anhydrase inhibitors","Prostaglandin analogues","Cholinergic agonists","Topical corticosteroids"],
  answer:0,
  explanation:"Carbonic anhydrase inhibitors are correct. The treatment protocol explicitly lists “carbonic anhydrase inhibitors” among drugs that decrease aqueous humour production (L11, page 19). Therefore, a topical carbonic anhydrase inhibitor fits the stated pressure-lowering mechanism when topical beta-blockers are unsuitable because of severe bronchial asthma (not taken from the course material).\n\nProstaglandin analogues are wrong because the lecture places them under “Increasing aqueous humour drainage,” not under decreasing production (L11, page 19). They lower intraocular pressure chiefly by increasing uveoscleral aqueous outflow (not taken from the course material).\n\nCholinergic agonists are wrong because their glaucoma effect corresponds to the lecture’s “miotics,” which are likewise listed with drugs that increase aqueous humour drainage (L11, page 19). They contract the ciliary muscle and facilitate trabecular outflow rather than primarily suppressing aqueous production (not taken from the course material).\n\nTopical corticosteroids are wrong because they are not aqueous-suppressant glaucoma medication. They are anti-inflammatory drugs, and local corticosteroid use may instead elevate intraocular pressure (not taken from the course material).\n\nWritten for this bank — Opthalmology endpoint.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:"Carbonic anhydrase inhibitors lower intraocular pressure by decreasing aqueous humour production.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2218-2219" },

{ id:"ophep2-summer-exam-23", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-glauc",
  stem:"A 55-year-old hyperopic female with anatomically narrow anterior chamber angles is prescribed a systemic anticholinergic medication for gastrointestinal spasms. Within hours, she develops severe ocular pain, headache, nausea, blurred vision, and colored halos around lights. Examination reveals a mid-dilated fixed pupil and markedly elevated intraocular pressure.\n\nWhat ocular emergency was most likely precipitated by this medication?",
  options:["Acute angle-closure glaucoma","Optic neuritis","Rhegmatogenous retinal detachment","Central retinal artery occlusion"],
  answer:0,
  explanation:"A is correct: this is acute angle-closure glaucoma precipitated by systemic anticholinergic-induced mid-dilatation in a susceptible patient. The lecture identifies females and hyperopes as at risk, and lists “Use of systemic medications (anticholinergics)” among factors increasing risk through a mid-dilated pupil (L12, page 14). It describes the sequence from relative pupil block to iris bombe, narrowing of the anterior-chamber angle, impaired aqueous outflow, and a rapid severe rise in IOP—an “Ocular emergency” (L12, page 18). Her pain, coloured haloes, rapidly reduced vision, nausea and headache fit the listed symptoms, including “Nausea / Vomiting” (L12, page 19). The markedly raised IOP and mid-dilated fixed pupil likewise match the listed signs: “IOP → ++++ (stony hard)” and “Mid-dilated vertically oval non-reactive pupil” (L12, page 20).\n\nB is wrong because optic neuritis is an optic-nerve disorder, not medication-triggered closure of the anterior-chamber angle with an abrupt IOP rise and a mid-dilated non-reactive pupil. The course associates acute optic neuritis with a central scotoma (L21, slide 4); the features in this vignette instead directly reproduce the acute-angle-closure clinical picture.\n\nC is wrong because rhegmatogenous retinal detachment is caused by a retinal tear/break, whereas the course describes its typical symptoms as floaters, flashes, and a peripheral “curtain” or shadow (L20, slide 11). Those are not the acute painful, high-IOP, pharmacologically precipitated presentation here.\n\nD is wrong because central retinal artery occlusion has retinal vascular signs—retinal whitening, a cherry-red macular spot, and marked APD—with VA < 6/60 (L19.2, page 26). It does not explain the narrow angles, anticholinergic trigger, mid-dilated fixed pupil, or markedly elevated IOP.\n\nWritten for this bank — Opthalmology endpoint.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:"Recognize that systemic anticholinergics can precipitate acute angle-closure glaucoma in a hyperopic patient with narrow angles.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2220-2221" },

{ id:"ophep2-summer-exam-24", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-uvea",
  stem:"A 35-year-old male presents with a painful red eye, marked photophobia, and blurred vision. Slit-lamp examination demonstrates circumcorneal injection with inflammatory cells and flare in the anterior chamber.\n\nIn addition to topical corticosteroids, which medication is essential to relieve ciliary spasm and prevent posterior synechiae formation?",
  options:["Topical cycloplegic agent","Topical pilocarpine","Topical beta-blocker","Oral NSAIDs"],
  answer:0,
  explanation:"This presentation is anterior uveitis: the lecture lists ocular pain, red eye, photophobia, and blurred vision, with ciliary injection and anterior-chamber “Cells & flare” as clinical features (L13,14, slide 13, 14, 16). A topical cycloplegic-mydriatic agent is therefore the essential addition to topical corticosteroids. The lecture states that these medications decrease “photophobia and pain from iritis and ciliary spasm” and “prevent and break recently formed posterior synechiae” (L13,14, slide 20). Posterior synechiae are also specifically listed as a sequela of anterior uveitis (L13,14, slide 18).\n\nTopical pilocarpine is wrong because it is a miotic, so it constricts rather than cyclopleges and dilates the pupil; it would not achieve the stated pain relief or prevention of posterior synechiae (not taken from the course material). The lecture lists pilocarpine among topical miotics for glaucoma, not anterior uveitis (not taken from the course material).\n\nA topical beta-blocker is wrong because it lowers intraocular pressure rather than relieving ciliary spasm or maintaining pupillary dilation (not taken from the course material). The uveitis lecture reserves antiglaucoma medication for “case of increased IOP” (L13,14, slide 20), whereas this question asks for the routine adjunct that addresses pain and synechiae.\n\nOral NSAIDs are wrong because they do not provide cycloplegia or mydriasis and therefore cannot prevent posterior synechiae (not taken from the course material). In this lecture, oral NSAIDs such as ibuprofen are identified as the main treatment for episcleritis (L13,14, slide 31) and are also listed for scleritis (L13,14, slide 37), not as the required adjunct in anterior uveitis.\n\nWritten for this bank — Opthalmology endpoint.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:"In anterior uveitis, topical cycloplegic-mydriatic medication relieves ciliary-spasm pain and prevents or breaks recently formed posterior synechiae.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2222-2223" },

{ id:"ophep2-summer-exam-25", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-uvea",
  stem:"A 33-year-old male presents with acute onset of unilateral eye redness, deep aching ocular pain, and marked photophobia. Vision is mildly reduced in the affected eye. Slit-lamp examination using a narrow oblique beam reveals inflammatory cells suspended in the anterior chamber along with a protein-rich “flare,” producing a characteristic “headlight-in-fog” appearance due to breakdown of the blood–aqueous barrier.\n\nWhich condition is most consistent with these findings?",
  options:["Acute iridocyclitis","Viral conjunctivitis","Dry eye disease","Episcleritis"],
  answer:0,
  explanation:"A. Acute iridocyclitis is correct. The lecture classifies anterior uveitis as “iritis / iridocyclitis” (L13,14, slide 12). Its listed symptoms are “Usually, unilateral,” “Ocular pain,” “Red eye,” “Photophobia,” and “Blurred vision” (L13,14, slide 13), matching this acute unilateral painful red eye with photophobia and mildly reduced vision. The decisive slit-lamp finding is anterior-chamber inflammation: the lecture lists “Anterior chamber: Cells & flare ± hypopyon” as a sign of anterior uveitis (L13,14, slide 16). Thus, the suspended cells and protein-rich flare seen in the narrow oblique beam identify anterior uveitis/acute iridocyclitis. Flare results from breakdown of the blood–aqueous barrier, allowing protein into the aqueous and producing the “headlight-in-fog” appearance (not taken from the course material).\n\nB. Viral conjunctivitis is an inflammation of the conjunctival surface and usually causes conjunctival injection, watery discharge, and irritation rather than deep aching pain, marked photophobia, or anterior-chamber cells and flare (not taken from the course material). It therefore does not explain the intraocular inflammatory signs.\n\nC. Dry eye disease typically produces ocular-surface dryness, burning, grittiness, and fluctuating vision, often bilaterally; it does not cause acute deep ocular pain, marked photophobia, or inflammatory cells and flare in the anterior chamber (not taken from the course material).\n\nD. Episcleritis is a superficial inflammation that usually causes sectoral redness and mild discomfort, with vision preserved. It does not produce anterior uveitis findings such as cells and flare (not taken from the course material).\n\nWritten for this bank — Opthalmology endpoint.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:"Recognize anterior-chamber cells and flare with a unilateral painful photophobic red eye as acute iridocyclitis (anterior uveitis).",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2224-2225" },

{ id:"ophep2-summer-exam-26", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-uvea",
  stem:"A 32-year-old male with a history of ankylosing spondylitis presents with a severe unilateral red eye, deep boring ocular pain, and marked photophobia. Slit-lamp examination reveals circumcorneal congestion and inflammatory cells circulating within the anterior chamber.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?",
  options:["Acute iritis","Bacterial conjunctivitis","Viral keratitis","Episcleritis"],
  answer:0,
  explanation:"A. Acute iritis is correct. The lecture classifies anterior uveitis as “iritis / iridocyclitis” and describes it as usually unilateral, with ocular pain, red eye and photophobia (L13,14, slide 12; L13,14, slide 13). It also lists ankylosing spondylitis among immune-mediated causes of uveitis (L13,14, slide 11). The circumcorneal congestion is the ciliary injection described for anterior uveitis, and anterior-chamber inflammatory cells are directly consistent with the listed sign, “Anterior chamber: Cells & flare ± hypopyon” (L13,14, slide 16; L13,14, slide 16). Together, these findings identify acute anterior uveitis/iritis.\n\nB. Bacterial conjunctivitis is wrong because the course image shows superficial conjunctival injection with mucopurulent material on the lashes and medial canthus, while the cornea is clear (L6, slide 17). This vignette instead has ciliary injection and anterior-chamber cells, both supporting intraocular inflammation rather than isolated conjunctival disease.\n\nC. Viral keratitis is wrong because herpes simplex epithelial keratitis is taught as a corneal epithelial process with a classic dendritic ulcer and terminal buds (L15,16, page 13). No corneal epithelial lesion or dendritic ulcer is described; the decisive stated slit-lamp abnormality is inflammatory cells circulating in the anterior chamber.\n\nD. Episcleritis is wrong because it is described as a benign, self-limited red eye causing only mild pain or discomfort and mild localised tenderness (L13,14, slide 30). It does not account for this severe painful photophobic presentation with anterior-chamber inflammation.\n\nWritten for this bank — Opthalmology endpoint.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:"Recognize acute anterior uveitis (iritis) from unilateral painful photophobic red eye, ciliary injection, anterior-chamber cells, and associated ankylosing spondylitis.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2226-2227" },

{ id:"ophep2-summer-exam-27", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-vissym",
  stem:"A 28-year-old female reports recurrent episodes of expanding scintillating zig-zag lines that gradually spread across her visual field and last approximately 20–30 minutes. The visual symptoms completely resolve and are immediately followed by a severe unilateral throbbing headache associated with nausea and photophobia.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?",
  options:["Migraine with aura","Rhegmatogenous retinal detachment","Vitreous floaters","Posterior vitreous detachment"],
  answer:0,
  explanation:"A. Migraine with aura is correct. Recurrent, gradually expanding scintillating zig-zag visual phenomena lasting 20–30 minutes, with complete resolution followed immediately by unilateral throbbing headache, nausea, and photophobia, is the typical pattern of migraine visual aura followed by migraine headache (not taken from the course material). The course defines photopsia only as “Seeing flashes of light in the absence of a light source” and floaters as “Seeing floating black shadows in bright illumination” (L1,2, slide 82); it does not teach migraine aura or its duration.\n\nB. Rhegmatogenous retinal detachment is wrong. Its course pattern is floaters, flashes of light, and a “curtain” or peripheral shadow, often with sudden, rapid progression (L20, slide 11). This patient instead has a fully reversible, spreading zig-zag phenomenon and then a characteristic headache; no persistent curtain or shadow is described.\n\nC. Vitreous floaters are wrong. Floaters are black shadows seen floating in bright illumination (L1,2, slide 82), rather than recurrent expanding scintillating zig-zag lines followed by headache. Floaters do not account for the associated nausea and photophobia (not taken from the course material).\n\nD. Posterior vitreous detachment is wrong. The course does not cover posterior vitreous detachment as a named entity (L20, slide 33). It may cause floaters and flashes, but it does not characteristically cause a transient spreading visual aura followed by unilateral throbbing headache with nausea and photophobia (not taken from the course material).\n\nWritten for this bank — Opthalmology endpoint.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:"The fact tested is that a reversible, gradually spreading scintillating visual aura lasting 20–30 minutes followed by migrainous headache indicates migraine with aura.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2228-2229" },

{ id:"ophep2-summer-exam-28", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-systemic",
  stem:"A 55-year-old female with a history of Sjögren syndrome complains of chronic bilateral eye irritation, burning, and a persistent gritty “foreign body” sensation. Schirmer testing demonstrates less than 5 mm of wetting after 5 minutes in both eyes. Slit-lamp examination reveals punctate epithelial erosions of the cornea.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?",
  options:["Keratoconjunctivitis sicca","Vernal keratoconjunctivitis","Phlyctenular keratoconjunctivitis","Epidemic keratoconjunctivitis"],
  answer:0,
  explanation:"A. Keratoconjunctivitis sicca is correct. The lecture states that dry eye in rheumatologic conditions is “referred to as keratoconjunctivitis sicca” and that the most common causes include “Sjögren syndrome, SLE, and rheumatoid arthritis” (L23, page 33). Thus, Sjögren syndrome with chronic bilateral irritation and burning is the course-supported diagnostic association. The gritty foreign-body sensation, Schirmer wetting of less than 5 mm after 5 minutes, and punctate epithelial erosions support aqueous-deficient dry eye with ocular-surface epithelial damage (not taken from the course material).\n\nB. Vernal keratoconjunctivitis is an allergic ocular-surface disorder, classically dominated by marked itching and often associated with giant tarsal papillae and/or limbal disease, rather than Sjögren-associated tear deficiency (not taken from the course material). The course labels vernal keratoconjunctivitis and shows its characteristic clinical images, but does not provide this clinical distinction in text (L6, slide 38).\n\nC. Phlyctenular keratoconjunctivitis produces focal inflammatory phlyctenules at the conjunctiva, limbus, or cornea, rather than bilateral aqueous-deficient dry eye caused by Sjögren syndrome (not taken from the course material). The course names phlyctenular keratoconjunctivitis but does not state its cause, symptoms, or diagnostic differentiation in text (L6, slide 46).\n\nD. Epidemic keratoconjunctivitis is an acute, highly contagious adenoviral conjunctivitis, typically with watery discharge, follicular conjunctivitis, and possible preauricular lymphadenopathy, not a chronic Sjögren-related dry-eye syndrome (not taken from the course material). The course covers viral conjunctivitis but does not name epidemic keratoconjunctivitis or adenovirus (L6, slide 34–36).\n\nWritten for this bank — Opthalmology endpoint.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:"Recognize Sjögren syndrome as a common cause of keratoconjunctivitis sicca.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2230-2231" },

{ id:"ophep2-summer-exam-29", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-systemic",
  stem:"A 48-year-old male with previously normal vision presents with sudden blurring of distance vision over the past week, while near vision remains relatively preserved. He also reports excessive thirst, frequent urination, and recent weight loss. Ophthalmic examination reveals a newly developed myopic refractive error with otherwise normal ocular structures.\n\nWhat is the most likely underlying cause of this refractive change?",
  options:["Undiagnosed diabetes mellitus","Nuclear sclerotic cataract","Retinal detachment","Presbyopia"],
  answer:0,
  explanation:"A. Undiagnosed diabetes mellitus is correct. The thirst, polyuria and weight loss are classic symptoms of diabetes mellitus (not taken from the course material), and the sudden refractive change with a normal ocular examination supports a glucose-related lens refractive change rather than a structural eye lesion. The lecture explicitly states that diabetes mellitus has ocular complications “including refractive changes and cataract formation” (L23, page 4). Acute hyperglycaemia can alter lens hydration and refractive index, producing a transient myopic shift (not taken from the course material). This explains blurred distance vision while near vision remains relatively preserved: the refractive-errors lecture lists “Blurred distance vision” and says near vision is usually not affected in myopia (L7,8, slide 43).\n\nB. Nuclear sclerotic cataract can cause acquired myopia: increased nuclear refractive index causes “acquired myopia,” and a nuclear cataract may give temporary “second sight” with better near reading (L18, page 6). However, this patient’s ocular structures are described as normal, and the abrupt onset with systemic diabetic symptoms makes diabetes the more likely cause.\n\nC. Retinal detachment is not a refractive shift. Rhegmatogenous detachment more characteristically causes floaters, flashes and a peripheral “curtain” or shadow (L20, slide 11), rather than isolated new myopia with preserved near vision.\n\nD. Presbyopia is age-related recession of the near point, so near images can no longer be focused using accommodation (L7,8, slide 73). It causes difficulty with near vision, the opposite of this presentation, and does not explain acute distance blur or diabetic symptoms.\n\nWritten for this bank — Opthalmology endpoint.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:"Diabetes mellitus can produce transient refractive changes, including an acute myopic shift.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2232-2233" },

{ id:"ophep2-summer-exam-30", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-refract",
  stem:"A 48-year-old diabetic patient is reviewed one month after presenting with severe hyperglycemia that had caused sudden blurred distance vision due to a transient myopic shift. His blood glucose is now well controlled with insulin therapy.\n\nWhat refractive change is most likely to occur after normalization of blood glucose levels?",
  options:["Resolution of the myopic shift toward baseline refraction","Permanent worsening of the myopic shift","Development of marked irregular astigmatism","Irreversible cataract formation"],
  answer:0,
  explanation:"A is correct. The hyperglycaemia-associated myopic shift is transient; once blood glucose normalizes, lens hydration and refractive power return toward their previous state, so the refraction moves back toward baseline. This is consistent with the course definition of myopia as parallel rays focusing “IN FRONT OF” the retina (L7,8, slide 39), although the glucose-related reversible shift itself is not taught in the lectures (not taken from the course material).\n\nB is wrong because persistent or permanently worsening myopia would not describe a transient metabolic refractive change after glycaemic control. Resolution toward the pre-hyperglycaemic refraction is expected (not taken from the course material).\n\nC is wrong because irregular astigmatism is a meridional refractive abnormality, not the expected consequence of correcting hyperglycaemia. The course describes astigmatism as failure of rays to focus to a single retinal point, producing two focal lines (L7,8, slide 61); it does not link it to glucose normalization.\n\nD is wrong because the vignette describes a reversible refractive shift, not cataract formation. Diabetes is listed among systemic associations of complicated cataract (L17, page 3), but this does not make cataract an inevitable or irreversible result of a short-lived hyperglycaemic refractive change (not taken from the course material).\n\nWritten for this bank — Opthalmology endpoint.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:"Recognize that a transient myopic refractive shift during severe hyperglycaemia resolves toward baseline after blood glucose normalization.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2234-2235" },

{ id:"ophep2-summer-exam-31", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-refract",
  stem:"A 40-year-old male with diabetes mellitus abruptly stops taking his insulin therapy. Three days later, he presents complaining of blurred distance vision and difficulty recognizing distant objects. Laboratory testing reveals a blood glucose level of 450 mg/dL.\n\nWhich refractive error is most likely induced by this acute hyperglycemic state?",
  options:["Myopia","Hyperopia","Presbyopia","Astigmatism"],
  answer:0,
  explanation:"A. Myopia is the keyed answer. Acute severe hyperglycaemia can produce a transient myopic refractive shift through altered lens hydration and refractive index (not taken from the course material). This makes distant objects blur, matching the vignette. The lecture defines myopia as parallel rays being focused “IN FRONT OF” the retina with accommodation at rest and lists “Index myopia” with increased refractive index of the lens nucleus as a refractive cause (L7,8, slide 39; L7,8, slide 42). The cataract lecture mentions “Snowflake cataract (acute hyperglycaemia)” (L18, page 21), but neither lecture states the associated acute refractive shift; L7,8, slide 42 gives only the refractive mechanism, and acute hyperglycaemia itself is not covered in L7,8.\n\nB. Hyperopia is incorrect because it is the opposite optical state: parallel rays are focused “BEHIND” the retina with accommodation at rest (L7,8, slide 49). It would not explain the printed-keyed acute myopic shift in this setting (not taken from the course material).\n\nC. Presbyopia is incorrect because it is age-related recession of the near point, usually in persons 40 years of age or older, producing impaired near accommodation rather than an abrupt distance-vision change after hyperglycaemia (L7,8, slide 73). The acute metabolic association is not taken from the course material.\n\nD. Astigmatism is incorrect because it results from unequal refractive power in different meridians, most commonly corneal in origin, rather than a transient uniform lens-related refractive shift from acute hyperglycaemia (L7,8, slide 64; not taken from the course material).\n\nWritten for this bank — Opthalmology endpoint.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:"Acute hyperglycaemia may induce a transient myopic refractive shift with blurred distance vision.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2236-2237" },

{ id:"ophep2-summer-exam-32", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-refract",
  stem:"The human eye functions as a powerful optical system with a total refractive power of approximately 60 diopters. Which anatomical structure contributes the greatest proportion of the eye’s refractive power?",
  options:["Cornea","Crystalline lens","Aqueous humor","Vitreous humor"],
  answer:0,
  explanation:"A. Cornea is correct. The lecture’s optical-elements diagram assigns the cornea 42-44 Dioptres of the eye’s approximately 60 Dioptres total optical power, making it the largest contributor. It therefore supplies substantially more refractive power than the crystalline lens (L7,8, slide 23).\n\nB. Crystalline lens is incorrect because the same diagram assigns the lens 18-20 Dioptres, less than the cornea’s 42-44 Dioptres (L7,8, slide 23). The lens remains an important optical element and can alter its power during accommodation, but it does not provide the greatest proportion of the resting eye’s refractive power (not taken from the course material).\n\nC. Aqueous humor is incorrect. The lecture’s diagram identifies the cornea and lens as the eye’s optical elements, not the aqueous humor (L7,8, slide 23). Aqueous humor contributes little refractive effect because its refractive index is close to that of adjacent ocular media (not taken from the course material).\n\nD. Vitreous humor is incorrect for the same reason: it is not listed as one of the optical elements supplying the stated major powers (L7,8, slide 23). Its refractive contribution is small because its refractive index is similar to that of the surrounding ocular media (not taken from the course material).\n\nWritten for this bank — Opthalmology endpoint.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:"The cornea provides the greatest proportion of the eye’s approximately 60 Dioptres of refractive power.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2238-2239" },

{ id:"ophep2-summer-exam-33", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-orbit",
  stem:"A 45-year-old female with autoimmune thyroid disease presents with progressive bilateral proptosis, eyelid retraction, and diplopia on upward gaze. The ophthalmologist suspects extraocular muscle enlargement related to orbital inflammatory disease.\n\nWhich imaging modality is most appropriate for evaluating the orbital soft tissues and extraocular muscles?",
  options:["MRI of the orbit","Fluorescein angiography","B-scan ultrasonography","Corneal topography"],
  answer:0,
  explanation:"A. MRI of the orbit is correct. This presentation is characteristic of thyroid eye disease: the lecture describes bilateral proptosis with lid retraction (L10, slide 18) and restrictive myopathy causing double vision in positions of gaze (L10, slide 19). MRI is the appropriate modality because it can image orbital inflammatory disease (L10, slide 14), and the TED MRI figure specifically shows “enlarged extraocular muscle bellies” (L10, slide 21). It therefore directly evaluates the suspected orbital soft-tissue and extraocular-muscle enlargement.\n\nB. Fluorescein angiography is wrong because it is not an orbital soft-tissue or extraocular-muscle imaging study (not taken from the course material). In the course, fluorescein angiography is listed among investigations for malignant melanoma (L24, slide 15), not for thyroid eye disease or orbital inflammatory disease.\n\nC. B-scan ultrasonography is wrong because it is not the best study for defining the extraocular muscles and orbital inflammatory soft tissues in this vignette (not taken from the course material). The course illustrates B-scan ultrasonography for posterior scleritis, where it shows thickened sclera and a T-sign (L13,14, slide 37), a different anatomical target.\n\nD. Corneal topography is wrong because it maps corneal curvature rather than orbital tissues or extraocular muscles (not taken from the course material). The lecture identifies corneal topography as mandatory screening for ectasia before refractive surgery (L15,16, page 22), so it addresses corneal rather than orbital pathology.\n\nWritten for this bank — Opthalmology endpoint.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:"MRI evaluates orbital inflammatory disease and can demonstrate enlarged extraocular muscle bellies in thyroid eye disease.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2240-2241" },

{ id:"ophep2-summer-exam-34", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-cat-surg",
  stem:"A 75-year-old patient who underwent uncomplicated cataract extraction with intraocular lens implantation two years ago presents with gradually progressive blurred vision and increasing glare sensitivity. Slit-lamp examination reveals a dense fibrotic opacity located behind the intraocular lens implant.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?",
  options:["Posterior capsular opacification","Endophthalmitis","Retinal detachment","Cystoid macular edema"],
  answer:0,
  explanation:"Posterior capsular opacification is correct. This is the typical late complication after cataract extraction with IOL implantation: the lecture states that it occurs \"Months–years\" after surgery and is the \"Most common late complication\" (L18, page 18). Lens epithelial cells \"migrate on the posterior capsule → 'after-cataract'\", producing an opacity behind the implanted IOL and a gradual return of blurred vision (L18, page 18). The two-year gradual course and fibrotic posterior capsular opacity therefore fit PCO. The lecture gives Nd:YAG laser capsulotomy as definitive outpatient treatment (L18, page 18).\n\nEndophthalmitis is wrong because it is an infective, vision-threatening postoperative complication with a much earlier presentation: \"24–72 hrs (acute) or weeks (delayed)\" (L18, page 18). Its listed signs are pain, redness, reduced VA, hypopyon, and yellowish vitreous haze, rather than an isolated dense opacity behind the IOL (L18, page 18).\n\nRetinal detachment is wrong because the lecture identifies it as a higher-risk complication when there has been posterior capsule rupture with vitreous loss (L18, page 18); this operation was uncomplicated. Retinal detachment involves separation of the neurosensory retina and typically causes flashes, floaters, or a curtain-like visual-field defect rather than a posterior capsular opacity behind an IOL (not taken from the course material).\n\nCystoid macular edema is wrong because it occurs \"Weeks–months\" after surgery and represents fluid accumulating in the macular Henle's layer (L18, page 18). It is diagnosed by OCT showing a \"petaloid fluid pattern,\" not by a fibrotic opacity behind the IOL (L18, page 18).\n\nWritten for this bank — Opthalmology endpoint.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:"Recognize posterior capsular opacification as the common late cause of recurrent gradual visual blurring after cataract surgery with IOL implantation.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2242-2243" },

{ id:"ophep2-summer-exam-35", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-pupil",
  stem:"A 28-year-old male is evaluated in the trauma bay after sustaining a severe head injury in a motor vehicle accident. On examination, the right pupil measures 2 mm, while the left pupil is fixed and dilated at 8 mm.\n\nWhat is the clinical term used to describe unequal pupil sizes?",
  options:["Anisocoria","Strabismus","Leukocoria","Amblyopia"],
  answer:0,
  explanation:"A. Anisocoria is correct because it means a difference in size between the two pupils. The examination shows marked inequality: the right pupil is 2 mm and the left is fixed and dilated at 8 mm. The lecture states that normally pupils are “round and equal in diameter (Isocoria)” and that a “Difference in size between both pupils termed anisocoria” (L3,4, slide 29). Thus, regardless of the trauma context or the possible cause of the fixed dilated pupil, the clinical term describing the unequal sizes is anisocoria. The 6 mm difference is also greater than the lecture’s 1 mm threshold for anisocoria requiring assessment in both very bright and very dim lighting (L3,4, slide 30).\n\nB. Strabismus is incorrect. Strabismus denotes ocular misalignment, in which the visual axes are not directed at the same target, rather than unequal pupil diameters (not taken from the course material). This stem gives pupil measurements, not an eye-position abnormality.\n\nC. Leukocoria is incorrect. Leukocoria refers to a white pupillary reflex or “white pupil,” not a difference in pupil diameter. The lecture labels leukocoria as a pupil colour finding in mature cataract (L3,4, slide 20). Here, the abnormality described is a fixed dilated pupil, not a white reflex.\n\nD. Amblyopia is incorrect. Amblyopia is reduced visual acuity and is not a visible pupil-size finding. The course specifically notes that “amblyopia is a visual acuity, not an appearance” (L29, slide 23). No visual-acuity deficit is described; the finding is unequal pupil size, so anisocoria remains the keyed answer.\n\nWritten for this bank — Opthalmology endpoint.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:"Identify anisocoria as the term for unequal pupil sizes.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2244-2245" },

{ id:"ophep2-summer-exam-36", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-lac",
  stem:"A 55-year-old female with chronic cicatrizing conjunctival disease presents with severe dry eye symptoms, foreign body sensation, and ocular surface irritation. Examination reveals extensive conjunctival scarring with marked reduction of the mucin layer of the tear film.\n\nWhich cells are primarily responsible for producing the mucous layer of the tear film?",
  options:["Goblet cells","Meibomian glands","Lacrimal gland acini","Corneal endothelial cells"],
  answer:0,
  explanation:"A. Goblet cells is correct. The lecture’s “The Secretory Part / Mucin and Lipid part” slide includes a diagram explicitly captioned “Distribution of goblet cells on the conjunctiva with eyelids everted” (L9, slide 5). Goblet cells are specialised conjunctival epithelial cells that secrete mucins, forming the mucous component of the tear film and helping the aqueous tears spread over the ocular surface (not taken from the course material). Thus, loss of these cells in cicatrizing conjunctival disease explains the reduced mucin layer and severe ocular-surface symptoms. The lecture also labels a distinct “MUCIN LAYER   0.2 µm” within the tear film (L9, slide 3).\n\nB. Meibomian glands are incorrect because they produce the lipid component of the tear film, not its mucous component (not taken from the course material). The lecture places meibomian glands on the “Mucin and Lipid part” slide, but the adjacent figure separately identifies conjunctival goblet cells (L9, slide 5).\n\nC. Lacrimal gland acini are incorrect because they principally secrete the aqueous portion of tears (not taken from the course material). This matches the lecture’s designation of the lacrimal gland within “The Secretory Part / Aqueous part” (L9, slide 4).\n\nD. Corneal endothelial cells are incorrect because they maintain corneal deturgescence through fluid transport across the posterior cornea; they do not secrete tear-film mucin (not taken from the course material).\n\nWritten for this bank — Opthalmology endpoint.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:"Identify conjunctival goblet cells as the cells that produce the mucous layer of the tear film.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2246-2247" },

{ id:"ophep2-summer-exam-37", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-onc",
  stem:"A pediatrician examines a 6-month-old infant and notices a dense white pupillary reflex in the right eye during red reflex testing. Which serious intraocular condition must be urgently excluded in this patient?",
  options:["Retinoblastoma","Congenital glaucoma","Optic neuritis","Choroidal melanoma"],
  answer:0,
  explanation:"A. Retinoblastoma is correct. A dense white pupillary reflex is leukocoria. The lecture describes retinoblastoma as the most common primary intraocular malignancy of childhood and states that it arises from primitive retinal cells (L24, slide 19). Its clinical features include a yellow/white pupillary reflex, and leukocoria occurs in 60% of cases (L24, slide 20). The vignette is especially concerning because the child is 6 months old: the lecture gives a mean age of 4 months with a known history, and says that 90% of cases occur before 3 years of age (L24, slide 19). Retinoblastoma must therefore be urgently excluded. The lecture includes red reflex testing and ocular U/S (B-Scan) in its assessment, with B-Scan detecting calcification (L24, slide 22).\n\nB. Congenital glaucoma is wrong because it is not the serious intraocular malignancy classically signalled by leukocoria. It can present in infancy with enlarged eyes, corneal oedema, and blue sclera, but these findings are also listed as possible secondary manifestations of retinoblastoma (L24, slide 20). A dense white pupil should direct urgent exclusion toward retinoblastoma.\n\nC. Optic neuritis is wrong because it is an optic-nerve inflammatory disorder and does not characteristically produce a dense white pupillary reflex (not taken from the course material).\n\nD. Choroidal melanoma is wrong because it is a different intraocular tumour and is not the childhood tumour identified by this leukocoria vignette. The lecture instead specifically links retinoblastoma with a child presenting with a yellow/white pupillary reflex (L24, slide 20).\n\nWritten for this bank — Opthalmology endpoint.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:"Recognize leukocoria in an infant as a presentation requiring urgent exclusion of retinoblastoma.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2248-2249" },

{ id:"ophep2-summer-exam-38", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-ret-dr",
  stem:"During fundus examination, an ophthalmologist estimates the size of a retinal lesion by comparing it with the diameter of the optic disc.\nWhat is the average normal diameter of the human optic disc?",
  options:["1.5 mm","3.0 mm","5.0 mm","0.5 mm"],
  answer:0,
  explanation:"A. 1.5 mm is correct. The lecture defines one disc diameter as “(1500 μm)” when describing clinically significant macular oedema: “Retinal oedema one disc area or larger any part of which is within one disc diameter (1500 μm) of centre of fovea” (L19.1, page 11). Since 1500 μm equals 1.5 mm, the normal optic-disc diameter used as a fundus reference is 1.5 mm.\n\nB. 3.0 mm is wrong because it is twice the lecture’s stated one-disc-diameter reference of 1500 μm (L19.1, page 11).\n\nC. 5.0 mm is wrong because it is much larger than the stated 1500 μm, or 1.5 mm, disc-diameter reference (L19.1, page 11).\n\nD. 0.5 mm is wrong because it is only one third of the stated 1500 μm disc diameter (L19.1, page 11).\n\nWritten for this bank — Opthalmology endpoint.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:"Recognize that one optic disc diameter is approximately 1.5 mm (1500 μm).",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2250-2251" },

{ id:"ophep2-summer-exam-39", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-intro-exam",
  stem:"A 40-year-old healthy female undergoes routine ophthalmologic examination. Goldmann applanation tonometry is performed to assess intraocular pressure.\n\nWhat is the average normal intraocular pressure (IOP) in the healthy adult population?",
  options:["15 mmHg","21 mmHg","8 mmHg","25 mmHg"],
  answer:0,
  explanation:"Option A is the printed key. The lecture, however, states: “Normal range 10-21 mmHg (mean = 16 mmHg)” (L3,4, slide 36). Thus, the keyed value 15 mmHg is close to the stated normal population mean, but it does not exactly match the lecture’s quoted mean of 16 mmHg. Goldmann applanation tonometry is an appropriate context because the lecture lists “Applanation Tonometry — e.g., Goldman applanation tonometer” (L3,4, slide 39).\n\nOption B, 21 mmHg, is wrong as the average because the lecture gives it as the upper end of the normal range, not the mean: “Normal range 10-21 mmHg (mean = 16 mmHg)” (L3,4, slide 36).\n\nOption C, 8 mmHg, is wrong because it is below the lecture’s stated normal range of 10-21 mmHg (L3,4, slide 36).\n\nOption D, 25 mmHg, is wrong because it is above the stated normal range of 10-21 mmHg (L3,4, slide 36).\n\nWritten for this bank — Opthalmology endpoint.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:"The question tests the average normal intraocular pressure in healthy adults.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2252-2253" },

{ id:"ophep2-summer-exam-40", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-neuro",
  stem:"A 60-year-old male with a 40-pack-year smoking history presents with right shoulder pain radiating to the arm. Examination reveals ipsilateral ptosis, miosis, and facial anhidrosis.\n\nWhich underlying pathology must be urgently excluded?",
  options:["Pancoast tumor","Pituitary adenoma","Optic nerve glioma","Multiple sclerosis"],
  answer:0,
  explanation:"A. Pancoast tumor is the keyed answer. The ipsilateral ptosis and miosis indicate Horner syndrome, which the lecture labels “Sympathetic paralysis (Horner Syndrome)” (L5, slide 11); the slide’s photograph also shows mild ptosis and miosis. In this older heavy smoker, shoulder pain radiating to the arm together with Horner syndrome requires urgent exclusion of an apical lung (Pancoast) tumour, which can disrupt the cervical sympathetic pathway (not taken from the course material). The lecture does not state the association between Horner syndrome and Pancoast tumour, nor facial anhidrosis.\n\nB. Pituitary adenoma may produce visual-field defects through optic-chiasm compression, but it does not classically produce the ipsilateral sympathetic pattern of ptosis, miosis, and facial anhidrosis (not taken from the course material).\n\nC. Optic nerve glioma primarily causes optic-nerve dysfunction, such as reduced visual acuity, colour-vision impairment, visual-field loss, or optic-disc changes; it does not explain this Horner syndrome with shoulder and arm pain (not taken from the course material).\n\nD. Multiple sclerosis can cause optic neuritis or internuclear ophthalmoplegia, but it is not the urgent characteristic cause of this smoking-associated Horner syndrome and shoulder pain (not taken from the course material).\n\nWritten for this bank — Opthalmology endpoint.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:"Recognize Horner syndrome in a smoker with shoulder pain and urgently exclude a Pancoast tumour.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2254-2255" },

{ id:"ophep2-summer-exam-41", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-neuro",
  stem:"A 25-year-old patient presents with marked limitation of upward gaze. Examination reveals that the pupils constrict normally during accommodation to a near target but fail to constrict in response to light. Convergence-retraction nystagmus is also observed.\n\nWhich neurological region is most likely affected?",
  options:["Dorsal midbrain","Occipital lobe","Optic chiasm","Cerebellum"],
  answer:0,
  explanation:"A. Dorsal midbrain is correct. This presentation is dorsal midbrain syndrome: the lecture lists “Light Near dissociation,” “Upward gaze paralysis,” and “Convergence-retraction nystagmus” together under this syndrome (L22, slide 9). The preserved pupillary constriction with accommodation but absent response to light is light-near dissociation. Marked limitation of upward gaze and convergence-retraction nystagmus make the dorsal-midbrain localisation especially characteristic. The same slide also lists lid retraction and possible causes: “Pineal body tumors, Stroke, MS” (L22, slide 9).\n\nB. The occipital lobe is wrong because an occipital lesion affects the visual cortex and classically causes a contralateral homonymous visual-field defect, rather than vertical-gaze paralysis, convergence-retraction nystagmus, or light-near dissociation (not taken from the course material). The lecture’s visual-pathway diagram places the primary visual cortex after the optic radiations, not in the dorsal midbrain (L22, slide 13).\n\nC. The optic chiasm is wrong because chiasmal disease affects crossing optic nerve fibres and typically produces a bitemporal visual-field defect, not this combination of pupillary and vertical eye-movement signs (not taken from the course material). The lecture separately identifies the optic chiasm as part of the visual pathway (L22, slide 13).\n\nD. The cerebellum is wrong because cerebellar lesions more often cause impaired coordination, gait ataxia, or gaze-evoked nystagmus; they do not produce the dorsal-midbrain syndrome triad described here (not taken from the course material).\n\nWritten for this bank — Opthalmology endpoint.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:"Recognize dorsal midbrain syndrome from light-near dissociation, upward-gaze paralysis, and convergence-retraction nystagmus.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2256-2257" },

{ id:"ophep2-summer-exam-42", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-neuro",
  stem:"A 50-year-old patient with untreated late-stage syphilis undergoes neurologic examination. Both pupils are small and irregular. They fail to constrict in response to light but constrict normally during accommodation.\n\nWhat is the name of this pupillary abnormality?",
  options:["Argyll Robertson pupil","Adie tonic pupil","Marcus Gunn pupil","Horner pupil"],
  answer:0,
  explanation:"The keyed answer is Argyll Robertson pupil. The combination of small, irregular pupils with failure of the light response but preservation of constriction for accommodation is light-near dissociation. The lecture identifies Argyll Robertson pupil with “Small pupils,” “Light Near dissociation,” and “Neurosyphilis” (L22, slide 8). Its clinical photograph also shows pupils that are “miotic and irregular in outline” (L22, slide 5). Thus the late-stage syphilis context and the pupillary findings match option A.\n\nAdie tonic pupil is wrong. It is typically a unilateral dilated pupil with a poor light response and slow tonic near response, rather than bilaterally small irregular pupils with preserved normal accommodation (not taken from the course material). Adie’s pupil is not mentioned in the lecture.\n\nMarcus Gunn pupil is wrong. The lecture labels it “Relative Afferent Pupillary Defect” and shows that illuminating the affected eye produces less constriction, whereas illuminating the fellow eye constricts both pupils (L22, slide 6). This is a swinging-flashlight finding, not light-near dissociation in both pupils.\n\nHorner pupil is wrong. Horner syndrome can cause miosis, but the lecture lists “Dilation Lag,” “Ptosis,” “Apparent enophthalmos,” “Anhydrosis,” and congenital heterochromia with it (L22, slide 11). It does not present as the syphilis-associated light-near dissociation described here.\n\nWritten for this bank — Opthalmology endpoint.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:"Recognize Argyll Robertson pupil as small irregular pupils with light-near dissociation associated with neurosyphilis.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2258-2259" },

{ id:"ophep2-summer-exam-43", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-systemic",
  stem:"A 45-year-old male presents to the emergency department with severe headache and a blood pressure of 240/130 mmHg. Fundus examination reveals flame-shaped retinal hemorrhages, cotton-wool spots, and bilateral optic disc swelling.\n\nAccording to the Keith-Wagener-Barker classification, what grade of hypertensive retinopathy is present?",
  options:["Grade 4 hypertensive retinopathy","Grade 1 hypertensive retinopathy","Grade 2 hypertensive retinopathy","Grade 3 hypertensive retinopathy"],
  answer:0,
  explanation:"A is correct: this is Grade 4 hypertensive retinopathy. The lecture states that Grade 4 is “grade 3 plus disc swelling” (L23, page 23). This patient has the Grade 3 retinal findings—flame-shaped haemorrhages and cotton-wool spots/exudates—plus bilateral optic disc swelling. The stated blood pressure also fits the lecture’s description of a severe acute rise: diastolic pressure greater than 120 mm Hg and systolic pressure greater than 200 mm Hg, which produces “cotton-wool spots, flame-shaped hemorrhages” (L23, page 20). The lecture adds that in malignant hypertension optic disc swelling can resemble papilloedema (L23, page 18). The lecture labels this sequence “Modified Scheie Classification,” rather than Keith-Wagener-Barker, but its Grade 4 criterion supports the printed key (L23, page 23).\n\nB is wrong because Grade 1 is only “barely detectable arterial narrowing” (L23, page 23), without retinal haemorrhages, exudates, or disc swelling.\n\nC is wrong because Grade 2 is “obvious arterial narrowing with focal irregularities” (L23, page 23). It does not include the retinal lesions or swollen discs described here.\n\nD is wrong because Grade 3 is Grade 2 plus “retinal hemorrhages and/or exudates” (L23, page 23). Although this patient has those Grade 3 features, the additional bilateral optic disc swelling advances the grade to Grade 4.\n\nWritten for this bank — Opthalmology endpoint.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:"Identify Grade 4 hypertensive retinopathy as Grade 3 retinal haemorrhages/exudates plus optic disc swelling.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2260-2261" },

{ id:"ophep2-summer-exam-44", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-lid",
  stem:"A 65-year-old female complains of chronic foreign body sensation, tearing, and ocular irritation in her right eye. Examination reveals that the eyelid margin is normally positioned against the globe; however, several eyelashes are misdirected inward and rub directly against the corneal surface during blinking. What is the term for this condition?",
  options:["Trichiasis","Entropion","Ectropion","Distichiasis"],
  answer:0,
  explanation:"A. Trichiasis is correct: it means misdirected eyelashes rubbing the ocular surface while the eyelid margin remains in its normal position (not taken from the course material). This exactly matches the vignette’s inward-pointing lashes with a normally positioned lid margin. The course explicitly includes a slide headed “Trichiasis,” although it states that it has “no body text, no definition” (L5, slide 27).\n\nB. Entropion is wrong because it is inward turning of the eyelid margin itself, which carries the lash line toward the globe (not taken from the course material). The stem specifically says that the margin is normally positioned. The course separately labels slides as “Entropion / Cicatricial” and “Entropion / Senile (Involutional)” (L5, slide 28–29), while also noting that slide 27 gives no printed distinction from entropion (L5, slide 27).\n\nC. Ectropion is wrong because it is outward eversion of the eyelid margin, rather than isolated inward misdirection of lashes (not taken from the course material). The course separately presents “Ectropion / Cicatricial,” “Ectropion / Paralytic,” and “Ectropion / Senile (Involutional)” (L5, slide 30–32).\n\nD. Distichiasis is wrong because it is an accessory row of eyelashes, usually arising from the meibomian-gland orifices; the stem describes several lashes from the usual margin that are misdirected, not a second lash row (not taken from the course material). Distichiasis is listed as absent from this deck (L5, slide 27).\n\nWritten for this bank — Opthalmology endpoint.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:"Identify trichiasis as isolated inward misdirection of eyelashes despite a normally positioned eyelid margin.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2262-2263" },

{ id:"ophep2-summer-exam-45", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-cornea-surg",
  stem:"A 15-year-old boy with severe atopic disease, including eczema and allergic asthma, has a long history of vigorous eye rubbing due to chronic ocular itching. Over time, he develops progressive visual distortion and irregular astigmatism caused by progressive corneal thinning and protrusion.\n\nWhat is the most likely corneal complication?",
  options:["Keratoconus","Corneal arcus","Band keratopathy","Fuchs endothelial dystrophy"],
  answer:0,
  explanation:"A. Keratoconus is correct. This vignette describes corneal ectasia: progressive thinning and protrusion producing irregular astigmatism and visual distortion. The lecture defines ectasia as conditions with “progressive corneal thinning & protrusion” that “cause irregular astigmatism,” and identifies keratoconus as the most common type (L15,16, page 22). It specifically describes keratoconus as progressive, often asymmetric corneal thinning and protrusion, with onset at puberty; “eye rubbing” and “atopy” are listed risk factors (L15,16, page 23). Thus, the boy’s age, severe atopy, habitual rubbing, progressive distortion, and irregular astigmatism point directly to keratoconus.\n\nB. Corneal arcus is wrong. The lecture describes arcus senilis as an age-related “lipid arc at limbus,” physiological in elderly people, rather than a progressive ectatic disorder causing corneal thinning, protrusion, and irregular astigmatism (L15,16, page 29).\n\nC. Band keratopathy is wrong. It is calcium deposition in Bowman’s layer in the interpalpebral zone and is associated with chronic ocular disease, hypercalcaemia, and chronic uveitis (L15,16, page 30). This is a corneal deposition disorder, not the thinning-and-protrusion process described in the stem.\n\nD. Fuchs endothelial dystrophy is wrong. The lecture attributes it to abnormal endothelial cell function with loss of the dehydrating pump, guttata, and corneal oedema progressing to bullous keratopathy; characteristic vision is worse in the morning, and risk factors include older age in the 5th–6th decade (L15,16, page 28). That mechanism and demographic do not fit this adolescent with atopy and eye rubbing.\n\nWritten for this bank — Opthalmology endpoint.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:"Recognize keratoconus as progressive corneal ectasia causing thinning, protrusion, and irregular astigmatism, with eye rubbing and atopy as risk factors.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2264-2265" },

{ id:"ophep2-summer-exam-46", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-cornea",
  stem:"A patient presents with a painful red eye and photophobia. Fluorescein dye is instilled into the eye, and examination under cobalt blue light demonstrates numerous tiny punctate areas of bright green staining scattered across the corneal epithelium.\n\nWhat is the clinical term for this corneal finding?",
  options:["Punctate epithelial keratitis","Geographic ulcer","Dendritic ulcer","Corneal abrasion"],
  answer:0,
  explanation:"A is correct. Numerous tiny, scattered punctate epithelial staining spots are termed punctate epithelial keratitis (not taken from the course material). The finding represents diseased or absent corneal epithelium: the lecture states that fluorescein becomes green under cobalt-blue illumination and that areas of bright-green staining denote “absent or diseased epithelium” (L3,4, slide 16). It also lists punctate epithelial keratitis among the corneal manifestations of herpes zoster ophthalmicus (L15,16, page 17).\n\nB is wrong because a geographic ulcer is a large irregular epithelial lesion, rather than numerous tiny punctate spots. The lecture explicitly says, “Geographic = large irregular lesion” (L15,16, page 14).\n\nC is wrong because a dendritic ulcer has a linear branching configuration with terminal buds, not scattered punctate staining. The lecture describes the “Classic dendritic ulcer with terminal buds” and instructs the examiner to look for a “linear branching pattern with terminal buds on fluorescein staining” (L15,16, page 14 and 16).\n\nD is wrong because a corneal abrasion is a discrete traumatic epithelial defect rather than the named pattern of multiple punctate epithelial lesions (not taken from the course material). Although it can stain with fluorescein, the lecture describes abrasion as a defect whose extent is delineated by fluorescein staining (L15,16, page 47).\n\nWritten for this bank — Opthalmology endpoint.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:"Recognize numerous tiny scattered fluorescein-staining corneal epithelial lesions as punctate epithelial keratitis.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2266-2267" },

{ id:"ophep2-summer-exam-47", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-ret-dr",
  stem:"A 65-year-old diabetic patient with reduced central vision undergoes optical coherence tomography (OCT), which reveals center-involving cystoid macular edema.\n\nWhat is the current gold-standard pharmacologic treatment for this condition?",
  options:["Intravitreal anti-VEGF injections","Topical beta-blockers","Systemic corticosteroids","Pilocarpine"],
  answer:0,
  explanation:"Option A is correct. This presentation represents diabetic macular oedema with cystoid macular oedema affecting the centre of the macula. The lecture describes diffuse diabetic maculopathy as having “Frequent cystoid macular oedema” and lists “intra vitreal injection” (L19.1, page 13). Its treatment page explicitly includes “Intra vitreal Injection of anti VEGEF” (L19.1, page 15). Thus intravitreal anti-VEGF injections are the keyed pharmacologic treatment; they reduce VEGF-driven retinal vascular leakage and macular oedema (not taken from the course material). There is a course discrepancy: the cited treatment bullet is printed under grid treatment for diffuse retinal thickening more than 500 µm from the centre of the fovea, whereas this vignette specifies centre involvement; the printed key remains A.\n\nOption B is wrong. Topical beta-blockers lower intraocular pressure by reducing aqueous-humour production and are used in glaucoma; they do not treat retinal vascular leakage or diabetic macular oedema (not taken from the course material).\n\nOption C is wrong. Systemic corticosteroids are not the current standard first-line pharmacologic treatment for diabetic centre-involving macular oedema. Although steroids can be used in selected retinal inflammatory conditions or as local ocular therapy in some macular-oedema settings, they carry important systemic adverse effects in a diabetic patient and do not replace intravitreal anti-VEGF treatment here (not taken from the course material).\n\nOption D is wrong. Pilocarpine is a cholinergic miotic that can increase trabecular aqueous outflow in selected glaucoma situations; it has no role in treating diabetic cystoid macular oedema (not taken from the course material).\n\nWritten for this bank — Opthalmology endpoint.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:"Identify intravitreal anti-VEGF injection as the pharmacologic treatment for diabetic macular oedema with cystoid centre involvement.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2268-2269" },

{ id:"ophep2-summer-exam-48", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-trauma",
  stem:"A surgeon is preparing to reconstruct an eye with severe chemical injury that has lost the ability to regenerate normal corneal epithelium. The procedure requires transplantation of corneal epithelial stem cells.\n\nFrom which anatomical region should these stem cells be harvested?",
  options:["Limbus","Central cornea","Tarsal conjunctiva","Conjunctival fornix"],
  answer:0,
  explanation:"A. Limbus is correct. The lecture identifies loss of “LIMBAL STEM CELLS” after chemical injury as a cause of persistent corneal epithelial defects, and lists limbal stem cell transplantation among surgical interventions for these injuries (L25, slide 5; L25, slide 13). Thus, when normal corneal epithelial regeneration has been lost, the stem cells for transplantation are harvested from the limbus.\n\nB. Central cornea is wrong because the course specifically locates the relevant stem-cell population at the limbus, not the central cornea (L25, slide 5). The central cornea is therefore not the appropriate source for limbal epithelial stem-cell transplantation.\n\nC. Tarsal conjunctiva is wrong. It lines the inner surface of the eyelid and is not the anatomical reservoir of corneal epithelial stem cells; harvesting it would not replace the lost limbal stem-cell population (not taken from the course material).\n\nD. Conjunctival fornix is wrong. The fornix is the conjunctival recess between the lid and globe, rather than the limbal region identified in the lecture. Although the lecture notes that retained particles may be trapped in the conjunctival fornices after chemical injury, it does not identify the fornix as a source of corneal epithelial stem cells (L25, slide 10).\n\nWritten for this bank — Opthalmology endpoint.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:"Corneal epithelial stem cells required for ocular-surface reconstruction are harvested from the limbus.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2270-2271" },

{ id:"ophep2-summer-exam-49", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-cornea",
  stem:"A 30-year-old male with a painful red eye was mistakenly treated with topical corticosteroid eye drops for presumed allergic conjunctivitis. Four days later, he returns with worsening pain, photophobia, and decreased vision. Fluorescein staining demonstrates a large amoeboid epithelial ulcer with scalloped borders.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?",
  options:["Geographic corneal ulcer","Acanthamoeba keratitis","Bacterial corneal ulcer","Fungal keratitis"],
  answer:0,
  explanation:"A. Geographic corneal ulcer is the keyed answer. The steroid exposure followed by worsening painful photophobic red eye and a large epithelial ulcer fits steroid-exacerbated epithelial herpes simplex keratitis presenting as a geographic ulcer. The lecture describes epithelial HSV as “Dendritic / Geographic,” states that “Geographic = large irregular lesion,” and warns: “NEVER use topical steroids in EPITHELIAL herpes keratitis” because they promote viral replication and can result in perforation (L15,16, page 14; L15,16, page 16). It further specifically states that topical steroids in epithelial HSV “Can cause geographic ulcer” (L15,16, page 48). The terms “amoeboid” and “scalloped borders” are not stated in the course material; the course instead describes the geographic lesion as “large irregular” (L15,16, page 14).\n\nB. Acanthamoeba keratitis is wrong because its taught pattern is contact lens wear with tap-water or swimming exposure, severe pain out of proportion, early perineural infiltrates, and a late ring infiltrate (L15,16, page 18). These discriminating features are not given, whereas the steroid-associated geographic epithelial ulcer is explicitly taught for HSV.\n\nC. Bacterial corneal ulcer is wrong because the lecture associates it with rapid onset, mucopurulent discharge, a large central/paracentral ulcer, hypopyon, and contact lens history (L15,16, page 20). A geographic ulcer after inappropriate topical steroid treatment instead points to epithelial HSV.\n\nD. Fungal keratitis is wrong because the course associates it with trauma from plant matter, feathery satellite lesions, slower onset, and Sabouraud culture; topical steroid history can occur but is not the defining morphology here (L15,16, page 20).\n\nWritten for this bank — Opthalmology endpoint.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:"Recognize that topical corticosteroids can exacerbate epithelial HSV keratitis and produce a geographic corneal ulcer.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2272-2273" },

{ id:"ophep2-summer-exam-50", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-ret-vasc",
  stem:"A 65-year-old diabetic patient with long-standing retinal vascular disease presents with severe ocular pain and markedly elevated intraocular pressure. Slit-lamp examination reveals fine abnormal new blood vessels growing over the anterior surface of the iris.\n\nWhat is the term for this ocular finding?",
  options:["Rubeosis iridis","Iridodialysis","Posterior synechiae","Iris coloboma"],
  answer:0,
  explanation:"A. Rubeosis iridis is correct. The fine abnormal new vessels on the anterior iris are rubeosis iridis (not taken from the course material). The retinal vascular lecture lists “Rubeosis irides in 50%” among signs of ischaemic central retinal vein occlusion and instructs clinicians to “Look for rubeosis and angle new vessels” during follow-up (L19.2, page 12; L19.2, page 14). The severe pain and markedly raised intraocular pressure fit associated neovascular glaucoma (not taken from the course material); the glaucoma lecture states that a neovascular membrane can grow over the trabecular meshwork and contract, pulling the peripheral iris over the trabeculum (L12, page 26).\n\nB. Iridodialysis is an iris-root separation, producing a peripheral crescentic gap and a displaced pupil, rather than new vessels spread over the iris surface. The course illustrates it as an iris defect (L13,14, slide 8).\n\nC. Posterior synechiae are adhesions involving the posterior iris and are associated in the course with a festooned, irregular pupil in uveitis, not iris-surface neovascularization (L3,4, slide 31).\n\nD. Iris coloboma is a structural iris defect, illustrated as an inferiorly extending keyhole-shaped pupil defect, not acquired fine iris vessels (L13,14, slide 8).\n\nWritten for this bank — Opthalmology endpoint.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:"Recognize rubeosis iridis as abnormal neovascularization on the anterior iris, often associated with retinal ischaemia and neovascular glaucoma.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2274-2275" },

{ id:"ophep2-summer-exam-51", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-vitreous",
  stem:"A 55-year-old male with long-standing poorly controlled diabetes mellitus presents with sudden, painless, profound vision loss in his right eye. Direct ophthalmoscopy reveals a markedly reduced red reflex, making fundus details difficult to visualize.\n\nWhat is the most likely cause of this acute visual loss?",
  options:["Vitreous hemorrhage","Diabetic macular edema","Cataract","Central retinal artery occlusion"],
  answer:0,
  explanation:"A. Vitreous hemorrhage is the keyed answer. The markedly reduced red reflex and inability to see fundus details localize the problem to an opacity in the ocular media. The lecture states that media opacities including “vitreous haemorrhage” appear as black shadows against the reflex (L3,4, slide 50), and specifically lists “Dense vitreous hemorrhage (Black reflex)” under an absent reflex (L3,4, slide 51). In long-standing poorly controlled diabetes, proliferative diabetic retinopathy can produce fragile new vessels that bleed into the vitreous, causing abrupt, painless, profound visual loss and obscuring the fundus (not taken from the course material).\n\nB. Diabetic macular edema causes visual loss from retinal thickening at the macula, not from a hemorrhage that blocks the visual axis. It therefore would not be expected to markedly reduce the red reflex or prevent visualization of fundus details in this manner (not taken from the course material).\n\nC. Cataract is also a media opacity, and the lecture does note that a mature cataract can cause an absent reflex (L3,4, slide 51). However, cataract-related visual loss is usually gradual rather than sudden; the acute painless onset in this diabetic patient favors vitreous hemorrhage (not taken from the course material).\n\nD. Central retinal artery occlusion causes severe acute visual loss, but the fundus is expected to show retinal whitening, a cherry-red macular spot, and narrowed retinal vessels rather than being obscured by a black or markedly reduced red reflex (L19.2, page 26).\n\nWritten for this bank — Opthalmology endpoint.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:"A dense vitreous hemorrhage causes sudden painless visual loss with a markedly reduced or absent black red reflex and obscured fundus view.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2276-2277" },

{ id:"ophep2-summer-exam-52", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-glauc",
  stem:"An 8-month-old infant presents with excessive tearing, marked photophobia, blepharospasm, and bilaterally enlarged cloudy corneas. Examination demonstrates markedly elevated intraocular pressure.\n\nWhat is the definitive first-line treatment for this condition?",
  options:["Goniotomy","Topical beta-blockers alone","Systemic carbonic anhydrase inhibitors","Observation only"],
  answer:0,
  explanation:"A. Goniotomy is the keyed definitive first-line treatment for congenital/developmental glaucoma. The infant’s watering, photophobia and blepharospasm match the lecture’s symptom triad, while enlarged corneas, corneal oedema and increased IOP are listed signs (L12, page 35; L12, page 39). Goniotomy is an angle operation that opens the abnormal aqueous outflow pathway (not taken from the course material). However, the lecture specifically states, “Clear cornea → goniotomy” (L12, page 48). Because this vignette describes cloudy corneas, this conflicts with the lecture: for an “Opaque cornea” it lists trabeculotomy, trabeculectomy with antimetabolites, or synthetic valve implantation (L12, page 49). The printed key remains A as required.\n\nB. Topical beta-blockers alone are wrong because medical treatment is described as “Temporary till surgery,” rather than definitive therapy (L12, page 47). They may lower IOP temporarily (not taken from the course material), but do not provide the required surgical correction.\n\nC. Systemic carbonic anhydrase inhibitors are also not definitive first-line treatment. They can reduce IOP as a short-term measure (not taken from the course material), but the lecture distinguishes temporary medical treatment from surgical management (L12, page 47).\n\nD. Observation only is wrong because the child has markedly raised IOP with structural corneal enlargement and oedema. Untreated congenital glaucoma can cause progressive optic nerve damage and visual loss (not taken from the course material); the lecture presents surgery as the treatment pathway (L12, page 47).\n\nWritten for this bank — Opthalmology endpoint.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:"Recognize congenital/developmental glaucoma and identify goniotomy as the printed-key definitive surgical treatment.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2278-2279" },

{ id:"ophep2-summer-exam-53", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-conj",
  stem:"A 12-year-old boy with severe asthma and atopic disease presents with intense bilateral ocular itching, photophobia, and thick ropy mucoid discharge. Slit-lamp examination demonstrates giant cobblestone papillae on the superior tarsal conjunctiva.\n\nWhich medication is most appropriate for long-term maintenance therapy?",
  options:["Mast cell stabilizers","Systemic corticosteroids","Topical antibiotics","Topical antivirals"],
  answer:0,
  explanation:"The keyed option, mast cell stabilizers, is appropriate for long-term maintenance of vernal keratoconjunctivitis because it reduces mast-cell degranulation and helps prevent recurrent allergic inflammation (not taken from the course material). The presentation is consistent with the lecture’s Vernal KeratoConjunctivitis image: the everted upper lid has “LARGE, FLAT-TOPPED, POLYGONAL GIANT PAPILLAE” in a “cobblestone mosaic” (L6, slide 38). The course material teaches this diagnostic sign but does not state maintenance treatment.\n\nSystemic corticosteroids are not appropriate for routine long-term maintenance because their substantial systemic adverse effects make them unsuitable for chronic control when safer topical preventive treatment is available (not taken from the course material). They may be reserved for selected severe disease under specialist supervision (not taken from the course material).\n\nTopical antibiotics are wrong because this is allergic rather than bacterial conjunctival disease; antibiotics treat susceptible bacterial infection and do not prevent the mast-cell-mediated inflammation of vernal keratoconjunctivitis (not taken from the course material).\n\nTopical antivirals are wrong because they target viral ocular infection, not allergic conjunctival inflammation or giant papillae (not taken from the course material).\n\nWritten for this bank — Opthalmology endpoint.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:"Vernal keratoconjunctivitis with giant cobblestone papillae is maintained with mast cell stabilizers.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2280-2281" },

{ id:"ophep2-summer-exam-54", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-va",
  stem:"A 65-year-old patient with end-stage optic nerve disease is evaluated in a low-vision clinic. Best-corrected visual acuity in the better-seeing eye is measured as 3/60.\n\nAccording to standard legal and WHO definitions, how is this patient classified?",
  options:["Legally blind","Mild visual impairment","Normal vision","Moderate visual impairment"],
  answer:0,
  explanation:"A is the keyed answer: a best-corrected visual acuity of 3/60 meets the usual legal-blindness threshold, so this patient is legally blind (not taken from the course material). The lecture explains that the upper Snellen fraction is the testing distance and the lower number is the distance at which a normal eye reads that line; therefore, 3/60 represents profoundly reduced acuity (L1,2, slide 116). The lecture does not teach legal-blindness or WHO visual-impairment classification thresholds.\n\nThere is a terminology discrepancy: under the WHO acuity categories, 3/60 is severe visual impairment, whereas blindness is acuity worse than 3/60 (not taken from the course material). Nevertheless, the printed key is retained because the question’s legal classification is legally blind.\n\nB is wrong because mild visual impairment is substantially better acuity than 3/60 (not taken from the course material). C is wrong because normal vision is represented by much better acuity, such as 6/6 in the lecture’s conversion table (L1,2, slide 114). D is wrong because moderate visual impairment is also a less severe category than an acuity of 3/60 (not taken from the course material).\n\nWritten for this bank — Opthalmology endpoint.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:"Recognize that best-corrected visual acuity of 3/60 meets the usual legal-blindness threshold.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2282-2283" },

{ id:"ophep2-summer-exam-55", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-sclera",
  stem:"A 30-year-old female presents with a mildly red eye without pain, photophobia, or visual impairment. To differentiate superficial episcleral vessel congestion from deeper scleral inflammation, the ophthalmologist instills topical drops that rapidly blanch the superficial vessels.\n\nWhich class of medication was most likely used?",
  options:["Topical vasoconstrictors","Topical anesthetics","Topical antibiotics","Topical cycloplegics"],
  answer:0,
  explanation:"A. Topical vasoconstrictors are correct. The lecture specifically describes the “Blanching effect of Phenylephrine eye drops to differentiate conjunctivitis, episcleritis and anterior scleritis” (L13,14, slide 40). Phenylephrine is a topical sympathomimetic vasoconstrictor (not taken from the course material), so it can blanch susceptible superficial ocular vessels. This fits the intended diagnostic use in a mildly red, non-painful eye. The lecture identifies episcleritis as a relatively common, benign, self-limited red eye and describes mild pain and/or discomfort, whereas anterior scleritis has moderate to severe pain with globe tenderness (L13,14, slide 30 and 34). There is a discrepancy between the stem’s wording and the lecture table: the table states that episcleritis does not blanch with Phenylephrine 2.5 % but does blanch with Phenylephrine 10%; anterior scleritis does not blanch with either concentration (L13,14, slide 40).\n\nB. Topical anesthetics numb the ocular surface for examination or procedures; they do not produce the vasoconstrictive blanching response used in this test (not taken from the course material).\n\nC. Topical antibiotics treat susceptible bacterial ocular infection; they are not used to acutely distinguish superficial from deep vascular inflammation by blanching vessels (not taken from the course material).\n\nD. Topical cycloplegics paralyse the ciliary muscle and dilate the pupil; they do not serve as the vessel-blanching diagnostic drop (not taken from the course material).\n\nWritten for this bank — Opthalmology endpoint.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:"Recognize phenylephrine, a topical vasoconstrictor, as the drop used for blanching-based differentiation of ocular redness.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2284-2285" },

{ id:"ophep2-summer-exam-56", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-neuro-optic",
  stem:"A 40-year-old obese female with a long history of chronically elevated intracranial pressure develops progressive visual decline. Fundus examination initially showed swollen optic discs, but over time the discs became pale, flat, and atrophic following prolonged papilledema.\n\nWhat is the term for this type of optic nerve atrophy?",
  options:["Secondary optic atrophy","Primary optic atrophy","Consecutive optic atrophy","Glaucomatous optic atrophy"],
  answer:0,
  explanation:"A. Secondary optic atrophy is correct. The key feature is optic atrophy following a period of disc swelling from papilledema: chronic raised intracranial pressure first produces papilledema, then the previously swollen disc becomes pale, flat and atrophic. This is secondary optic atrophy (not taken from the course material). The lecture identifies papilledema with “↑ ICP” and marked disc elevation, “+ + +” (L21, slide 8). Its optic-atrophy slide shows the Secondary disc as “DIRTY GREY-WHITE,” with “INDISTINCT AND BLURRED” margins and hazy peripapillary retina, described as “the residue of the swelling that preceded the atrophy” (L21, slide 14). This fits the sequence in the stem.\n\nB. Primary optic atrophy is wrong because it is not the atrophy that follows visible disc oedema. It classically follows damage to the optic nerve without prior intraocular disc swelling, leaving a pale disc with sharply defined margins (not taken from the course material). The lecture’s Primary photograph is a “CHALKY WHITE disc” with margins that are “CLEARLY DEFINED AND SHARP” and a normal surrounding retina (L21, slide 14), unlike the post-papilledema scenario.\n\nC. Consecutive optic atrophy is wrong because it follows retinal disease, rather than prolonged papilledema or raised intracranial pressure (not taken from the course material). The lecture depicts it as a “WAXY YELLOW disc” with “MARKEDLY ATTENUATED” vessels and an abnormal background retina in which “the retinal disease that caused the atrophy is visible” (L21, slide 14).\n\nD. Glaucomatous optic atrophy is wrong because it results from glaucomatous optic neuropathy, characteristically with pathological cupping, not from papilledema (not taken from the course material). The lecture shows a “deep and large” cup occupying most of the disc, with pallor “CONFINED TO THE CUP” (L21, slide 14).\n\nThe lecture names all four types but does not explicitly define their causes; the causal classification above is therefore not taken from the course material.\n\nWritten for this bank — Opthalmology endpoint.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:"Identify secondary optic atrophy as optic disc pallor and atrophy that follows prolonged papilledema.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2286-2287" },

{ id:"ophep2-summer-exam-57", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-cornea",
  stem:"A 22-year-old contact lens wearer presents with excruciating ocular pain, severe photophobia, and decreased vision. She admits to rinsing and storing her contact lenses in tap water. Slit-lamp examination reveals a ring-shaped corneal infiltrate.\n\nWhat is the most appropriate initial management?",
  options:["Corneal scraping for culture followed by targeted antimicrobial therapy","Empirical topical corticosteroid therapy","Immediate penetrating keratoplasty","Observation only"],
  answer:0,
  explanation:"A is right. This is Acanthamoeba keratitis: the lecture links it to “contact lens wear + tap water / swimming,” severe disproportionate pain, and a late “RING infiltrate” (L15,16, page 18). Initial investigation includes “corneal scraping & culture,” and the lecture specifies culture on non-nutrient agar with E. coli overlay (L15,16, page 19). The printed keyed sequence therefore appropriately obtains microbiological material before antimicrobial treatment. The lecture’s organism-specific treatment is PHMB (polyhexamethylene biguanide) plus propamidine isethionate hourly for weeks (L15,16, page 19).\n\nB is wrong because this presentation is an active infectious keratitis, not an indication for empirical steroid monotherapy. The lecture states, “AVOID topical steroids until infectious cause is excluded or controlled” (L15,16, page 11); steroids may potentiate infection and delay healing in bacterial keratitis (L15,16, page 48).\n\nC is wrong as immediate penetrating keratoplasty is not the initial step. The lecture reserves therapeutic keratoplasty for active keratitis failing medical therapy, while tectonic grafting restores integrity in descemetocele or perforation (L15,16, page 38). Neither failure of medical treatment nor perforation is given here.\n\nD is wrong because the severe pain, reduced vision, water exposure, and ring infiltrate identify a potentially sight-threatening corneal infection requiring diagnostic sampling and treatment, not observation. The lecture specifically says never to use tap water with contact lenses and to use lens solution (L15,16, page 19).\n\nWritten for this bank — Opthalmology endpoint.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:"Recognize Acanthamoeba keratitis from contact-lens water exposure, severe disproportionate pain, and a ring infiltrate, then obtain corneal scraping and culture before treatment.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2288-2289" },

{ id:"ophep2-summer-exam-58", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-peds",
  stem:"A premature infant born at 26 weeks’ gestation develops advanced fibrovascular proliferation within the retina, leading to tractional retinal detachment.\n\nWhat is the most appropriate surgical intervention at this advanced stage?",
  options:["Vitrectomy","Laser photocoagulation","Intravitreal anti-VEGF injection alone","Observation"],
  answer:0,
  explanation:"A. Vitrectomy is correct. This infant has tractional retinal detachment from advanced fibrovascular proliferation. The lecture explains that, in tractional retinal detachment, scar tissue or membranes physically pull the retina away, and treatment must remove that mechanical force. It specifically states: “Vitrectomy & Membrane Peeling” removes the vitreous and peels “FIBROVASCULAR MEMBRANES or scar tissue that is pulling on the retina” (L20, slide 15). Thus vitrectomy addresses the established traction causing the detachment.\n\nB. Laser photocoagulation is not the appropriate intervention once advanced tractional detachment is present. The ROP lecture lists laser photocoagulation as a modality for active ROP at “threshold disease,” rather than as surgery to remove pre-existing tractional membranes (L19.2, page 37). It cannot peel the fibrovascular tissue already pulling the retina off.\n\nC. Intravitreal anti-VEGF injection alone may suppress active neovascular drive, but it does not surgically release an established tractional retinal detachment (not taken from the course material). The indicated management here must remove the tractional membranes.\n\nD. Observation is inappropriate because the retina is already detached by traction. The lecture frames tractional-detachment treatment as relieving the pulling force, not watchful waiting (L20, slide 15).\n\nWritten for this bank — Opthalmology endpoint.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:"Established tractional retinal detachment from fibrovascular membranes requires vitrectomy with membrane peeling to relieve retinal traction.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2290-2291" },

{ id:"ophep2-summer-exam-59", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-cornea",
  stem:"A 25-year-old male presents to the emergency department with severe pain following a corneal abrasion. A single drop of topical tetracaine produces immediate and dramatic pain relief. The patient requests a prescription for continued home use.\n\nWhy is prolonged outpatient use of topical anesthetic eye drops contraindicated?",
  options:["They can cause corneal melting and perforation","They precipitate acute angle-closure glaucoma","They induce rapid cataract formation","They cause retinal detachment"],
  answer:0,
  explanation:"A is correct. Prolonged outpatient topical anesthetic use is unsafe because it impairs corneal surface repair: the lecture states that these drops “Inhibit epithelial healing,” “Cause stromal melting,” and can produce “Anaesthetic keratopathy → perforation”; therefore, they should “NEVER” be prescribed for prolonged outpatient use (L15,16, page 48). In a corneal abrasion, pain relief may encourage repeated use while the injured cornea continues to be damaged and loses protective sensation.\n\nB is wrong. Acute angle-closure glaucoma results from obstruction of aqueous outflow at the anterior chamber angle, not from topical corneal anesthetic use (not taken from the course material).\n\nC is wrong. Rapid cataract formation is not an adverse effect of topical tetracaine or other topical anesthetics (not taken from the course material).\n\nD is wrong. Retinal detachment is separation of the retina and is not caused by prolonged topical anesthetic use (not taken from the course material).\n\nWritten for this bank — Opthalmology endpoint.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:"Prolonged topical anesthetic use inhibits corneal epithelial healing and can cause stromal melting, anesthetic keratopathy, and perforation.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2292-2293" },

{ id:"ophep2-summer-exam-60", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-acute",
  stem:"A 75-year-old female presents with sudden painless vision loss in one eye. She also reports scalp tenderness, jaw claudication, proximal muscle stiffness, and recent fatigue.\n\nWhich investigation is considered the gold-standard diagnostic test for confirming the underlying condition?",
  options:["Temporal artery biopsy","Carotid endarterectomy","Optic nerve sheath fenestration","Cranial burr hole procedure"],
  answer:0,
  explanation:"A. Temporal artery biopsy is the keyed answer. This presentation is characteristic of giant cell arteritis causing arteritic anterior ischaemic optic neuropathy: acute painless monocular visual loss with scalp tenderness, jaw claudication, constitutional symptoms and proximal stiffness. Temporal artery biopsy demonstrates the vasculitic arterial inflammation and is the gold-standard confirmatory test (not taken from the course material). The acute-visual-loss lecture presents a similar elderly woman with visual loss, headache, fatigue and weight loss (L31,32, slide 20), and diagnoses “Anterior ischemic optic neuropathy” (L31,32, slide 23). However, it explicitly states that the management slide contains “Nothing ... about the temporal artery, about biopsy, about ESR/CRP” (L31,32, slide 24), so the biopsy fact is not taught in the lecture. The lecture also records that “jaw claudication, scalp tenderness, polymyalgia” are “not printed” (L31,32, slide 31), despite these being decisive clues in this question.\n\nB. Carotid endarterectomy is surgery to remove atherosclerotic plaque from a carotid artery, principally to reduce stroke risk in selected carotid stenosis; it neither confirms giant cell arteritis nor samples the affected temporal artery (not taken from the course material).\n\nC. Optic nerve sheath fenestration is a procedure used to protect vision in selected cases of raised intracranial pressure, especially idiopathic intracranial hypertension. It is not a diagnostic test for giant cell arteritis and does not establish the cause of this patient’s optic neuropathy (not taken from the course material).\n\nD. A cranial burr hole is a neurosurgical opening used, for example, to drain certain intracranial haemorrhages or obtain cranial access. It has no diagnostic role in confirming giant cell arteritis (not taken from the course material).\n\nWritten for this bank — Opthalmology endpoint.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:"Recognise temporal artery biopsy as the gold-standard confirmatory investigation for suspected giant cell arteritis.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2294-2295" },

{ id:"ophep2-summer-exam-61", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-neuro-motil",
  stem:"A 60-year-old female presents with sudden onset of severe unilateral headache associated with complete ptosis and diplopia. Examination shows the affected eye positioned “down and out,” with a fixed dilated pupil that does not react to light.\n\nWhat is the most urgent next diagnostic step?",
  options:["Cerebrovascular imaging with CTA","Fasting blood glucose measurement","Tensilon test","Orbital CT scan"],
  answer:0,
  explanation:"A is right. Complete ptosis, a “down and out” eye, diplopia, and a fixed dilated pupil indicate a complete third-nerve palsy with pupil involvement. The lecture identifies “ptosis + ‘down and out’ + a dilated pupil” as “a complete third nerve palsy with pupil involvement” (L30, slide 12). It further labels pupil involvement as “Compression” and illustrates a posterior communicating artery aneurysm compressing the superficial pupillary fibres, producing “pupillary dilation” (L30, slide 13). Sudden severe unilateral headache with this pupil-involving palsy therefore requires urgent cerebrovascular imaging with CTA to identify an aneurysm (not taken from the course material). The lecture does not specify an investigation for nerve palsy, stating that CT, MRI, and angiography are not mentioned (L30, slide 13).\n\nB is wrong because fasting blood glucose may help assess diabetes as a vascular risk factor, but it is not the urgent test for this presentation. The lecture contrasts vascular disease causing central nerve-fibre infarction, in which the “pupil normal,” with external aneurysmal compression causing pupillary dilation (L30, slide 13). This patient has a dilated, nonreactive pupil, fitting compression rather than the pupil-spared vascular pattern.\n\nC is wrong because a tension test does not investigate a third-nerve palsy or exclude a compressive intracranial aneurysm (not taken from the course material).\n\nD is wrong because orbital CT is aimed at orbital pathology and does not provide the required dedicated assessment of the cerebral circulation for a suspected posterior communicating artery aneurysm. CTA is the urgent vascular study in this clinical context (not taken from the course material).\n\nWritten for this bank — Opthalmology endpoint.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:"A painful pupil-involving complete third-nerve palsy requires urgent cerebrovascular imaging for a compressive aneurysm.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2296-2297" },

{ id:"ophep2-summer-exam-62", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-orbit",
  stem:"A 45-year-old female presents with progressive bilateral eye protrusion associated with gritty foreign body sensation, excessive tearing, and an abnormal “staring” appearance caused by upper eyelid retraction. Examination also reveals conjunctival congestion and limitation of upward gaze.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?",
  options:["Thyroid eye disease","Orbital cellulitis","Cavernous sinus thrombosis","Myasthenia gravis"],
  answer:0,
  explanation:"A. Thyroid eye disease is correct. This is the characteristic chronic bilateral orbitopathy: progressive bilateral proptosis, gritty foreign-body sensation and tearing/lacrimation, conjunctival congestion, upper-lid retraction producing a staring appearance, and restrictive extraocular-muscle disease. The lecture lists “Grittiness, red eyes, lacrimation” in thyroid eye disease (L10, slide 16), illustrates bilateral lid retraction as Dalrymple and Kocher signs (L10, slide 17), and shows “bilateral proptosis with lid retraction” (L10, slide 18). It also identifies restrictive myopathy with bilateral lid retraction and proptosis in thyroid eye disease (L10, slide 19), which accounts for limited upward gaze.\n\nB. Orbital cellulitis can cause proptosis, conjunctival injection and painful ophthalmoplegia, but it is an acute infection rather than a progressive bilateral presentation. The lecture describes marked pyrexia, tender warm erythematous lids, painful ophthalmoplegia, and infection usually arising from the ethmoid sinuses (L10, slide 26). These infectious features and the classic thyroid lid signs are not present here.\n\nC. Cavernous sinus thrombosis may produce bilateral proptosis, chemosis, venous congestion and ocular-motility signs, but its onset is rapid and it is associated with severe headache, malaise, nausea and vomiting after infection such as sinusitis, cellulitis or otitis (L10, slide 32). That acute, systemically unwell pattern does not fit this progressive thyroid eye disease vignette.\n\nD. Myasthenia gravis causes variable, fatigable ptosis and extraocular-muscle weakness, not lid retraction with proptosis. The course material demonstrates variability of the ptosis, which resolves after rest, and a positive Ice Pack Test (L5, slide 13), and shows the ptosis worsening on sustained upgaze (L30, slide 18) and states that myasthenia may imitate nerve or muscle palsy (L30, slide 20). It does not explain the bilateral protrusion, congestion, and thyroid-pattern lid retraction here.\n\nWritten for this bank — Opthalmology endpoint.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:"Recognize thyroid eye disease from bilateral progressive proptosis, lid retraction, ocular-surface symptoms, and restrictive ocular motility.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2298-2299" },

{ id:"ophep2-summer-exam-63", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-cat-surg",
  stem:"A 72-year-old patient with a completely dense mature cataract is evaluated before cataract surgery. Visual acuity is reduced to hand motion only. The ophthalmologist wants to assess whether the retina and optic nerve still retain gross visual function.\n\nWhich test is most important to perform?",
  options:["Light projection test","Snellen visual acuity test","Amsler grid testing","Confrontation visual field testing"],
  answer:0,
  explanation:"A. Light projection test is right. In a dense cataract, the pre-operative assessment specifically includes “Projection of light in all 4 directions (dense cataract)” (L18, page 14). A penlight is directed from the superior, inferior, temporal, and nasal quadrants while the other eye is occluded; the patient identifies the direction of illumination. The result is recorded as good or bad light projection (L1,2, slide 130). Thus, it provides a gross assessment of remaining visual pathway function when only hand-motion vision is present and the fundus cannot be assessed adequately through the opaque lens.\n\nB. Snellen visual acuity testing is wrong because it measures letter-chart acuity, which cannot be meaningfully obtained when vision is only hand motion. It quantifies acuity but does not test whether the patient can localise light from the four quadrants in this dense-cataract setting.\n\nC. Amsler grid testing is wrong because the course assigns it to the central 10° of the visual field (L1,2, slide 123). It requires sufficient central vision and fixation to detect distortion or central field defects, neither of which is practical through a completely dense mature cataract with hand-motion vision.\n\nD. Confrontation visual field testing is wrong because the course distinguishes it from light projection: confrontation is for VA = 1/60 or better, whereas light projection is for VA less than 1/60 (L1,2, slide 123). It is therefore not the most appropriate gross visual-function test here.\n\nWritten for this bank — Opthalmology endpoint.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:"In a dense cataract with profoundly reduced vision, assess gross visual function by testing projection of light in all four directions.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2300-2301" },

{ id:"ophep2-summer-exam-64", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-ret",
  stem:"A 55-year-old highly myopic male presents with a sudden “curtain-like” shadow descending over the superior visual field of his right eye. Fundus examination demonstrates an extensive rhegmatogenous retinal detachment involving the peripheral retina, while central visual acuity remains 6/6.\n\nWhich factor is most responsible for the favorable visual prognosis?",
  options:["The macula remains attached","Young patient age","Presence of a natural crystalline lens","Normal intraocular pressure"],
  answer:0,
  explanation:"A is correct: the macula remains attached. The presentation is consistent with rhegmatogenous retinal detachment (RRD): the lecture describes RRD as a detachment caused by a retinal tear or break, through which vitreous fluid enters the subretinal space, and lists high myopia as a common cause (L20, slide 6). It also identifies floaters, flashes, and a “curtain” or peripheral shadow as RRD symptoms (L20, slide 11). Preserved central acuity of 6/6 is explained by sparing of the macula; because the macula mediates central visual acuity, keeping it attached preserves central function and is the key favourable prognostic feature (not taken from the course material). The lecture itself does not teach macula-on versus macula-off detachment or prognostic timing, so this prognostic claim is not taken from the course material.\n\nB is wrong. Younger age is not the principal reason for the favourable visual prognosis in this vignette. The relevant prognostic distinction is whether the macula is attached, rather than the patient’s age (not taken from the course material). Indeed, the lecture lists aging as a common cause of RRD, not as the favourable feature determining visual outcome here (L20, slide 6).\n\nC is wrong. Presence of a natural crystalline lens is not the factor accounting for preserved central visual acuity in an established RRD. Lens status can affect retinal-detachment risk in some clinical settings, but it does not outweigh macular attachment as the determinant of central visual prognosis in this scenario (not taken from the course material).\n\nD is wrong. Normal intraocular pressure does not explain the good visual prognosis. The retinal-detachment lecture does not cover intraocular-pressure findings in retinal detachment; the decisive feature is macular sparing (not taken from the course material).\n\nWritten for this bank — Opthalmology endpoint.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:"The fact tested is that a macula-on retinal detachment has a more favourable visual prognosis because central retinal function is preserved.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2302-2303" },

{ id:"ophep2-summer-exam-65", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-peds",
  stem:"A full-term infant is delivered at 38 weeks’ gestation with a birth weight of 3.2 kg after an uncomplicated pregnancy and delivery. The parents ask whether retinal screening for retinopathy of prematurity is necessary. What is the most appropriate recommendation?",
  options:["No screening is required","Immediate screening within 24 hours","Screening at 4 weeks of age","Screening before hospital discharge"],
  answer:0,
  explanation:"Option A is correct. This infant was born full-term at 38 weeks and weighs 3.2 kg, so he does not have the preterm, low-birth-weight context associated with ROP. The lecture describes ROP as “Proliferative retinopathy in preterm and low birth weight infants,” which may lead to “epiretinal fibrosis and tractional retinal detachment” (L33,34, page 22). Therefore routine ROP screening is not indicated for the infant described (not taken from the course material).\n\nOption B is wrong because immediate examination within 24 hours is not the routine timing for ROP screening in an otherwise well full-term infant; moreover, this infant has no screening indication (not taken from the course material).\n\nOption C is wrong because screening at 4 weeks of age applies only when an infant meets an ROP screening indication; it should not be arranged simply because the patient is a newborn (not taken from the course material).\n\nOption D is wrong because discharge timing does not create an indication for ROP screening. A full-term infant of this birth weight does not require screening before discharge (not taken from the course material). The lecture names ROP and describes its complications and management, including observation, laser, intravitreal anti-VEGF, and PPV, but it does not provide screening eligibility criteria or screening timing (L33,34, page 19).\n\nWritten for this bank — Opthalmology endpoint.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:"ROP screening is not required for a full-term infant with normal birth weight; it is directed toward preterm, low-birth-weight infants.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2304-2305" },

{ id:"ophep2-summer-exam-66", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-orbit",
  stem:"A 32-year-old female presents with progressive horizontal diplopia and a sensation of tightness around the eyes. Examination reveals bilateral proptosis and limitation of elevation, particularly when attempting upward gaze. Forced duction testing is positive, demonstrating mechanical resistance to passive movement of the globe. Visual acuity is normal, and there is no pupillary abnormality.\n\nWhich type of strabismus is most consistent with this presentation?",
  options:["Restrictive strabismus","Paralytic strabismus","Sensory strabismus","Accommodative esotropia"],
  answer:0,
  explanation:"A. Restrictive strabismus is correct. The key finding is a positive forced duction test: resistance to passive globe movement indicates a mechanical restriction rather than simply inadequate muscle activation (not taken from the course material). This patient’s bilateral proptosis, tight orbital sensation, horizontal diplopia, and especially restricted elevation fit thyroid eye disease with restrictive myopathy. The lecture lists “Restrictive myopathy (e.g. thyroid eye disease)” among causes of defective ocular motility (L10, slide 10), and captions a figure “Restrictive myopathy and bilateral lid retraction and proptosis in thyroid eye disease” (L10, slide 11). It further states that restrictive myopathy causes “Double vision and often discomfort in some p[ositions of gaze” (L10, slide 19). Normal visual acuity and no pupillary abnormality also make optic neuropathy less likely; the TED optic-neuropathy slide lists central-vision impairment and relative afferent pupillary defect among assessment findings (L10, slide 20). The lecture announces tests to differentiate restrictive from neurological motility defects, but its list is cut off; forced duction is not printed by name (L10, slide 10).\n\nB. Paralytic strabismus results from impaired neural drive to an extraocular muscle, not mechanical tethering. Although it can cause diplopia and limited movement, passive movement is not mechanically resisted on forced duction testing (not taken from the course material). Thus the positive test and TED-pattern proptosis favour restriction. The lecture distinguishes restrictive from neurological motility defects as separate categories (L10, slide 10).\n\nC. Sensory strabismus is a deviation associated with impaired vision or disrupted binocular sensory fusion (not taken from the course material). Normal visual acuity, a mechanical limitation of elevation, proptosis, and positive forced duction do not support this mechanism. The course lists sensory esotropia but does not explain its cause or diagnostic features (L29, slide 27).\n\nD. Accommodative esotropia is an inward deviation associated in the lecture with “Hypermetropia,” “High AC/A,” and age “2.5 - 5 years” (L29, slide 29). It does not explain bilateral proptosis or resistance to passive elevation, so it cannot account for this presentation.\n\nWritten for this bank — Opthalmology endpoint.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:"Recognize mechanical limitation with a positive forced duction test and thyroid eye disease features as restrictive strabismus.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2306-2307" },

{ id:"ophep2-summer-exam-67", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-insid",
  stem:"A 75-year-old patient presents with gradually progressive central visual distortion. Fundus examination reveals multiple small yellow deposits beneath the retina, located between the retinal pigment epithelium and Bruch’s membrane in the macular region.\n\nWhat is the term for these deposits?",
  options:["Drusen","Cotton-wool spots","Hard exudates","Roth spots"],
  answer:0,
  explanation:"A. Drusen is correct. The deposits described are drusen: small yellow extracellular deposits associated with macular degeneration. The lecture states, “Drusen are hyaline nodules (or colloid bodies) deposited in Bruch's membrane,” and explains that Bruch’s membrane separates the inner choroidal vessels from the retinal pigment epithelium (L35,36, page 41). It also says that drusen may be “small and discrete” or larger with irregular shapes and indistinct edges (L35,36, page 41). The stem’s macular location and gradual central distortion are therefore consistent with drusen. The lecture notes that patients with drusen alone tend to have normal or near-normal visual acuity with minimal metamorphopsia (L35,36, page 41). The stem’s wording places the deposits between the retinal pigment epithelium and Bruch’s membrane, whereas the lecture describes them as deposited “in Bruch's membrane” (L35,36, page 41).\n\nB. Cotton-wool spots are superficial retinal nerve-fibre-layer lesions caused by focal retinal ischaemia, not deposits at the retinal pigment epithelium–Bruch’s membrane interface (not taken from the course material).\n\nC. Hard exudates are intraretinal lipid deposits from vascular leakage, commonly seen with diabetic retinal disease; they are not drusen beneath the retina in the macular Bruch’s membrane region (not taken from the course material).\n\nD. Roth spots are white-centred retinal haemorrhages, rather than yellow subretinal deposits. The course lists Roth spots among retinal findings in blood dyscrasias but does not define them (L19.2, page 43); the distinguishing description is not taken from the course material.\n\nWritten for this bank — Opthalmology endpoint.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:"Identify drusen as yellow deposits at the retinal pigment epithelium–Bruch’s membrane interface in macular degeneration.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2308-2309" },

{ id:"ophep2-summer-exam-68", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-glauc",
  stem:"A 60-year-old patient with chronic glaucoma undergoes automated visual field testing. The results demonstrate a dense curved scotoma extending from the blind spot and arching around fixation toward the nasal visual field.\n\nWhat is the name of this characteristic visual field defect?",
  options:["Arcuate scotoma","Central scotoma","Bitemporal hemianopia","Homonymous hemianopia"],
  answer:0,
  explanation:"A is correct. This is an arcuate scotoma: a dense curved field defect arising from the blind spot and arching around fixation toward the nasal field, following the arcuate retinal nerve-fibre bundles (not taken from the course material). In open-angle glaucoma, the lecture links neural rim thinning to visual-field changes and specifically lists “Arcuate scotomata (superior, inferior)” (L11, page 14). The described chronic glaucoma setting and arcuate configuration therefore fit this named defect.\n\nB is wrong because a central scotoma is centred on fixation rather than curving from the blind spot around it. The course illustrates central scotoma as a central oval defect in acute optic neuritis (L21, slide 4).\n\nC is wrong because bitemporal hemianopia is loss of the temporal outer half of each eye’s field, not a local curved scotoma. The lecture’s chiasmal field figure shows the outer temporal halves lost with nasal halves spared (L22, slide 15).\n\nD is wrong because homonymous hemianopia is loss of the same half of the visual field in both eyes, typically from a post-chiasmal lesion, rather than an arcuate defect associated with glaucoma. The lecture describes a left homonymous hemianopia as loss of the left half in both eyes (L22, slide 17).\n\nWritten for this bank — Opthalmology endpoint.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:"Recognize arcuate scotoma as a characteristic glaucomatous visual-field defect.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2310-2311" },

{ id:"ophep2-summer-exam-69", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-glauc",
  stem:"During ophthalmoscopic examination of a patient with suspected glaucoma, thinning of the neuroretinal rim is noted. According to the ISNT rule, glaucomatous damage most commonly begins in a characteristic region of the optic disc. Where does glaucomatous neuroretinal rim loss most commonly begin?",
  options:["Inferotemporal region","Nasal region","Superonasal region","Central region"],
  answer:0,
  explanation:"A. Inferotemporal region is correct. The ISNT rule describes the normal rim as thickest inferiorly, then superiorly, nasally, and thinnest temporally; glaucomatous loss characteristically affects the inferior and temporal rim early, producing inferotemporal thinning or notching. The lecture’s glaucoma fundus photograph shows that the surviving neuroretinal rim is “thinnest inferiorly and temporally” (L21, slide 10). Although the term “ISNT rule” is not printed in the lecture, this finding supports the keyed inferotemporal location.\n\nB. The nasal region is wrong because the lecture describes the retinal vessel trunk as emerging nasally in the glaucomatous disc; it does not identify the nasal rim as the characteristic site of initial loss (L21, slide 10). Nasal rim loss is not the typical early ISNT-rule pattern (not taken from the course material).\n\nC. The superonasal region is wrong. Superior rim damage can occur in glaucoma, but superonasal loss is not the characteristic initial site tested here; the course image instead identifies inferior and temporal rim thinning (L21, slide 10). The usual early pattern is inferotemporal or superotemporal, rather than superonasal (not taken from the course material).\n\nD. The central region is wrong because the centre of the disc is the cup, not the neuroretinal rim. In glaucoma, the lecture depicts a greatly enlarged cup with only a thin rim remaining at the edge of the disc (L21, slide 10). Rim loss therefore concerns the peripheral rim, not a central region.\n\nWritten for this bank — Opthalmology endpoint.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:"Identify the inferotemporal neuroretinal rim as the characteristic early site of glaucomatous rim loss under the ISNT rule.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2312-2313" },

{ id:"ophep2-summer-exam-70", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-cornea",
  stem:"A 40-year-old female with chronic juvenile idiopathic arthritis-associated ocular inflammation presents with a gray-white horizontal corneal opacity located within the interpalpebral zone. Examination reveals superficial calcium deposition within the cornea. What is the traditional definitive treatment for removing this corneal opacity?",
  options:["EDTA chelation therapy","Phototherapeutic keratectomy","Penetrating keratoplasty","Topical corticosteroids"],
  answer:0,
  explanation:"The finding is band keratopathy: superficial calcium deposition in Bowman's layer in the interpalpebral zone. The lecture states, “Band keratopathy: calcium deposits in Bowman's layer in interpalpebral zone,” and associates it with “chronic uveitis,” which fits this patient’s chronic juvenile idiopathic arthritis-associated ocular inflammation (L15,16, page 30). EDTA chelation therapy is the traditional definitive treatment because it chelates and removes the deposited calcium; the lecture explicitly lists “Treatment of band keratopathy: EDTA chelation (effective)” (L15,16, page 30).\n\nPhototherapeutic keratectomy is not the keyed traditional treatment. The lecture does list excimer laser PTK as an alternative treatment, but EDTA chelation is the treatment specified by the printed key (L15,16, page 30).\n\nPenetrating keratoplasty is not appropriate for this superficial calcific opacity. It replaces the full thickness of the cornea and is generally reserved for severe corneal opacity or structural disease not amenable to superficial treatment (not taken from the course material).\n\nTopical corticosteroids may be used to control the associated ocular inflammation, but they do not chelate or remove established corneal calcium deposits (not taken from the course material).\n\nWritten for this bank — Opthalmology endpoint.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:"Recognize band keratopathy and identify EDTA chelation as its traditional definitive treatment.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2314-2315" },

{ id:"ophep2-summer-exam-71", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-cornea",
  stem:"A 65-year-old patient presents with a painful vesicular rash distributed over the right forehead, upper eyelid, and the tip of the nose. He also complains of photophobia and decreased vision. Slit-lamp examination reveals corneal inflammation with multiple raised pseudodendritic lesions. What is the most likely diagnosis?",
  options:["Herpes zoster ophthalmicus","Herpes simplex keratitis","Bacterial keratitis","Acanthamoeba keratitis"],
  answer:0,
  explanation:"A is correct: this is herpes zoster ophthalmicus (HZO). The unilateral painful vesicular eruption over the forehead and upper lid fits varicella-zoster virus reactivation in the CN V1 (ophthalmic) dermatome. Crucially, vesicles on the tip of the nose are Hutchinson's sign, indicating nasociliary-branch involvement and a “HIGH risk of ocular complications” (L15,16, page 17). The photophobia and reduced vision are consistent with corneal involvement. The lecture specifically lists epithelial keratitis among HZO complications and gives “pseudodendritic ulcer” as a corneal manifestation (L15,16, page 17). It further distinguishes the HZO pseudodendrite as “smaller, stellate, lacks terminal buds” (L15,16, page 17), supporting the raised pseudodendritic lesions described here.\n\nB is wrong because herpes simplex keratitis classically produces a dendritic ulcer with a “linear branching pattern with terminal buds on fluorescein staining” (L15,16, page 16), rather than the pseudodendritic lesions of HZO. The characteristic V1 vesicular rash with nose-tip involvement also points to HZO rather than HSV.\n\nC is wrong because bacterial keratitis is described as acute pain, photophobia, blurred vision and mucopurulent discharge, with an epithelial defect, stromal infiltrate and circumcorneal injection (L15,16, page 9). Those features do not explain the dermatomal vesicular rash, Hutchinson's sign, or pseudodendrites.\n\nD is wrong because Acanthamoeba keratitis is strongly associated with contact lens wear plus tap-water or swimming exposure, severe pain out of proportion to the signs, early perineural infiltrate and a late ring infiltrate (L15,16, page 18). None is described, whereas the rash distribution and pseudodendrites directly support HZO.\n\nWritten for this bank — Opthalmology endpoint.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:"Recognize herpes zoster ophthalmicus from V1 vesicles with Hutchinson's sign and pseudodendritic keratitis.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2316-2317" },

{ id:"ophep2-summer-exam-72", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-sclera",
  stem:"A 35-year-old female presents with a mildly painful sectoral redness of one eye without visual loss or photophobia. To differentiate superficial episcleral inflammation from deeper scleral inflammation, the ophthalmologist instills a diagnostic eye drop that causes blanching of the superficial vessels within minutes. Which medication was most likely used?",
  options:["Phenylephrine 2.5%","Topical anesthetic drops","Topical antibiotic drops","Topical cycloplegic drops"],
  answer:0,
  explanation:"A. Phenylephrine 2.5% is the keyed answer because phenylephrine is a topical sympathomimetic vasoconstrictor; blanching after instillation indicates that the injected vessels are in a superficial, vasoconstrictable vascular bed rather than in the deeper scleral circulation (not taken from the course material). The clinical picture also resembles episcleritis: the lecture describes it as usually unilateral, with “Mild pain and/or discomfort,” and identifies simple episcleritis as “Sectoral or diffuse injection” (L13,14, slide 30; L13,14, slide 31). However, the lecture’s printed table conflicts with the premise and keyed concentration: it states that Phenylephrine 2.5 % blanches conjunctivitis but not episcleritis, whereas Phenylephrine 10% blanches episcleritis; anterior scleritis does not blanch with either concentration (L13,14, slide 40). The printed key remains A.\n\nB. Topical anesthetic drops reduce ocular surface sensation and may facilitate examination, but they are not used to produce diagnostic vascular blanching (not taken from the course material).\n\nC. Topical antibiotic drops treat susceptible superficial bacterial infection; they do not provide the immediate vasoconstrictive blanching test described in the stem (not taken from the course material).\n\nD. Topical cycloplegic drops paralyse accommodation and dilate the pupil. They are used in selected painful anterior-segment inflammatory conditions, but are not the diagnostic agent used to blanch superficial red-eye vessels (not taken from the course material).\n\nWritten for this bank — Opthalmology endpoint.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:"Identify phenylephrine as the diagnostic vasoconstrictor drop used in assessing superficial versus deeper ocular redness.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2318-2319" },

{ id:"ophep2-summer-exam-73", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-cornea",
  stem:"A 55-year-old male presents with progressive unilateral hearing loss, tinnitus, facial weakness, and decreased corneal sensation on the same side. Examination also reveals exposure keratopathy due to incomplete eyelid closure. MRI demonstrates a slowly enlarging tumor compressing cranial nerves V, VII, and VIII. Where is this lesion most likely located?",
  options:["Cerebellopontine angle","Cavernous sinus","Orbital apex","Optic chiasm"],
  answer:0,
  explanation:"A cerebellopontine angle lesion is the keyed answer. A slowly enlarging mass in this region can compress cranial nerves VIII, VII and V together, producing unilateral hearing loss and tinnitus, facial weakness with incomplete lid closure, and reduced corneal sensation. The course material links these ocular consequences to the relevant nerves: “Neurotrophic keratopathy: trigeminal anaesthesia” and lists “acoustic neuroma surgery” among its causes (L15,16, page 34). It also states that “Incomplete lid closure → inferior epithelial breakdown” and lists “VII nerve palsy” as a cause of exposure keratopathy (L15,16, page 36). The anatomical location of acoustic neuroma/vestibular schwannoma at the cerebellopontine angle is not taken from the course material.\n\nCavernous sinus disease can affect cranial nerves III, IV, V1, V2 and VI, typically producing ophthalmoplegia, sensory loss in the ophthalmic/maxillary distributions, and sometimes proptosis; it does not characteristically compress VIII to cause unilateral hearing loss and tinnitus (not taken from the course material).\n\nAn orbital apex lesion affects the optic nerve and ocular motor nerves, causing visual loss, ophthalmoplegia and V1 sensory disturbance. It would not account for auditory symptoms from VIII compression (not taken from the course material).\n\nAn optic chiasm lesion causes characteristic visual-field defects, especially bitemporal hemianopia when central chiasmal fibres are compressed. It does not explain ipsilateral VII, V and VIII findings or exposure keratopathy from facial weakness (not taken from the course material).\n\nWritten for this bank — Opthalmology endpoint.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:"Recognize that a cerebellopontine angle mass can compress cranial nerves V, VII and VIII, causing corneal sensory loss, facial palsy and auditory symptoms.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2320-2321" },

{ id:"ophep2-summer-exam-74", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-trauma",
  stem:"A 25-year-old male is struck in the eye by a baseball. CT scan demonstrates an orbital floor fracture with entrapment of the inferior rectus muscle within the fracture defect. What is the most likely visual complaint resulting from this injury?",
  options:["Binocular diplopia","Monocular diplopia","Flashes and floaters","Central scotoma"],
  answer:0,
  explanation:"A. Binocular diplopia is correct. The CT description is the mechanism taught for an orbital floor fracture: “RESTRICTIVE ocular motility due to MECHANICAL ENTRAPMENT of the INFERIOR OBLIQUE or INFERIOR RECTUS muscles within the fracture” (L25, slide 21). The lecture specifically states that this produces diplopia in “BOTH UPGAZE AND DOWNGAZE” (L25, slide 21). Entrapment prevents the two eyes from moving together, so each eye fixes the target differently and two images are perceived when both eyes are open (not taken from the course material). The baseball mechanism also matches the listed history of “FIST, TENNIS BALL,” and CT may show tissue entrapment, the “TEAR DROP SIGN” (L25, slide 20 and 23).\n\nB. Monocular diplopia is wrong because this is an ocular-motility restriction involving alignment of the two eyes, hence binocular diplopia. In the same trauma lecture, “UNIOCULAR DIPLOPIA” is instead attached to iridodialysis and lens subluxation (L25, slide 29 and 34), which are different blunt-trauma injuries.\n\nC. Flashes and floaters are not the expected complaint from inferior-rectus entrapment. They are included among associated symptoms to ask about in ocular trauma (L25, slide 56), but “Flashes of light” and “Dark floaters” are shown with rhegmatogenous retinal detachment (L20, slide 12), not orbital muscle entrapment.\n\nD. A central scotoma is a central visual-field defect, not diplopia from restrictive eye movement. The course associates “Acute optic neuritis” with “central scotoma” (L21, slide 4), rather than with orbital floor fracture and inferior-rectus entrapment.\n\nWritten for this bank — Opthalmology endpoint.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:"Inferior rectus entrapment in an orbital floor fracture causes restrictive ocular motility and binocular diplopia, particularly in upgaze and downgaze.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2322-2323" },

{ id:"ophep2-summer-exam-75", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-neuro-motil",
  stem:"A 32-year-old female presents with fluctuating ptosis and intermittent binocular diplopia that worsen toward the end of the day and improve with rest. An ice-pack test produces marked temporary improvement of her ptosis. What is the most likely diagnosis?",
  options:["Myasthenia gravis","Multiple sclerosis","Thyroid eye disease","Horner syndrome"],
  answer:0,
  explanation:"Myasthenia gravis is correct. The fluctuating ptosis that worsens late in the day and improves with rest is fatigable weakness: the lecture’s sustained-upgaze sequence shows that “the ptosis WORSENS as the position is held” (L30, slide 18). Its myasthenic neuromuscular-junction diagram labels “Receptors blocked by antibody” and “Reduced transmission” (L30, slide 17). The ice-pack finding is especially supportive: after the “Ice Pack Test,” the lecture shows that “BOTH LIDS ARE RAISED” (L30, slide 19). Intermittent binocular diplopia also fits ocular myasthenia, since the lecture shows variable EOM weakness and states that myasthenia can imitate “ANY nerve or muscle palsy” (L30, slide 20).\n\nMultiple sclerosis is wrong because it does not characteristically produce this combination of clearly fatigable ptosis and a marked reversible ice-pack response. It can cause diplopia through central ocular-motor pathway disease, but that is not the pattern demonstrated here (not taken from the course material).\n\nThyroid eye disease is wrong. The lecture does include double vision from restrictive myopathy, but associates it with lid retraction, proptosis, and discomfort in some positions of gaze (L10, slide 19); its lid signs are explicitly retraction and lid lag (L10, slide 17). These findings differ from fluctuating, fatigable ptosis that improves after an ice pack.\n\nHorner syndrome is wrong. Although it can cause ptosis, the taught associated signs are “Miosis,” “Dilation Lag,” “Apparent enophthalmos,” “Anhydrosis,” and congenital heterochromia (L22, slide 11). It does not account for fluctuating diplopia, fatigability, or the positive ice-pack response (not taken from the course material).\n\nWritten for this bank — Opthalmology endpoint.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:"Recognize fluctuating fatigable ptosis with diplopia and improvement after an ice-pack test as ocular myasthenia gravis.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2324-2325" },

{ id:"ophep2-summer-exam-76", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-lid",
  stem:"An oculoplastic surgeon is repairing a full-thickness eyelid laceration involving the lid margin. To achieve proper alignment and avoid postoperative lid notching, the surgeon carefully aligns an anatomical landmark that separates the anterior and posterior lamellae of the eyelid. What is the name of this landmark?",
  options:["Gray line","White line of Toldt","Schwalbe line","Arcus marginalis"],
  answer:0,
  explanation:"A. Gray line is correct. The lecture’s lid-margin diagram labels the “Grey line” between the “Front skin / muscle layer” and the “Tarsoconjunctival layer” (L1,2, slide 48). Thus, in a full-thickness lid-margin repair, it is the visible landmark used to restore the relationship of the anterior and posterior lamellae; careful re-apposition helps preserve a smooth margin and avoid notching (not taken from the course material).\n\nB. White line of Toldt is wrong. It is a peritoneal fusion plane along the lateral aspect of the colon, used in abdominal surgery, not an eyelid-margin landmark (not taken from the course material).\n\nC. Schwalbe line is wrong. The lecture identifies “Schwalbe’s Line” as part of the gonioscopic angle anatomy, listed after trabecular meshwork in the mnemonic “I CAN’T SEE THE STUPID LINE” (L1,2, slide 22). It is therefore an anterior-chamber angle structure, not the line separating eyelid lamellae.\n\nD. Arcus marginalis is wrong. This is the periosteal thickening at the orbital rim, rather than a landmark within the lid margin that guides alignment of its anterior and posterior lamellae (not taken from the course material).\n\nWritten for this bank — Opthalmology endpoint.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:"Identify the gray line as the lid-margin landmark between the anterior and posterior eyelid lamellae.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2326-2327" },

{ id:"ophep2-summer-exam-77", module:"ophtho", bank:"endpoint", chapter:"op-glauc",
  stem:"A 60-year-old male undergoes a routine ophthalmologic examination. He is asymptomatic and has normal central visual acuity. Intraocular pressure is measured at 30 mmHg in both eyes, and fundus examination demonstrates marked vertical cupping of the optic discs bilaterally. What is the most likely diagnosis?",
  options:["Primary open-angle glaucoma","Age-related macular degeneration","Cataract","Diabetic retinopathy"],
  answer:0,
  explanation:"A. Primary open-angle glaucoma is the keyed diagnosis. The combination of raised IOP and bilateral marked optic-disc cupping represents glaucoma: the lecture defines it as a triad of “Elevated intraocular pressure (IOP),” “Optic nerve affection (cupping),” and visual-field defects (L11, page 5). Normal central visual acuity does not exclude it, because central vision diminishes only in end-stage disease (L11, page 14), and patients are “Most commonly → asymptomatic” (L11, page 17). The age also fits, as older age (> 5th decade) is a predisposing factor for primary open-angle glaucoma (L11, page 11). The vignette does not provide gonioscopy or exclude secondary causes; the lecture states “Primary → no obvious cause” and defines an open angle by visible exposure of the trabecular meshwork to aqueous humour on gonioscopy (L11, page 7; L11, page 8). Nevertheless, the printed key is retained.\n\nB. Age-related macular degeneration primarily affects the macula and therefore central vision, rather than producing glaucomatous optic-disc cupping with raised IOP (not taken from the course material).\n\nC. Cataract is an opacity of the natural crystalline lens and typically causes progressive painless reduction in vision; it does not account for marked bilateral optic-disc cupping (not taken from the course material).\n\nD. Diabetic retinopathy is a retinal microvascular disease with retinal lesions such as microaneurysms, haemorrhages, exudates, or neovascularization, rather than the cupped optic discs characteristic of glaucoma (not taken from the course material).\n\nWritten for this bank — Opthalmology endpoint.pdf prints no explanation here.",
  objective:"Recognize primary open-angle glaucoma from asymptomatic raised IOP with glaucomatous optic-disc cupping despite preserved central visual acuity.",
  source:"Opthalmology endpoint.pdf p.2328-2329" }
];
